{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01M7PfbC23RJb62YJAAfZF8k","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# OutBloom 2006: Coast 2 Coast\n2\t\n3\t> *One day. One road. Every flower in bloom.*\n4\t\n5\tThis is the build plan for a feature-complete demo inspired by **OutRun 2006: Coast 2 Coast**. Read it together with\n6\t`TASK.md`; where the two seem to disagree, `TASK.md` wins and this plan bends.\n7\t\n8\t**Title.** It was picked the way `TASK.md` asks: fifty playful riffs on the source's name were brainstormed silently,\n9\tthen Python's `secrets.randbelow(50)` drew #49, **OutBloom 2006: Coast 2 Coast**. The title is final, so don't re-roll it.\n10\t\"Bloom\" carries three meanings and the whole design leans on them: flowers (the world), love in bloom (the passenger),\n11\tand the over-bright 2000s *bloom lighting* the source era is remembered for (the look).\n12\t\n13\t**The pitch.** An arcade road racer on the engine's pixel-art pseudo-3D road. You drive an open-top dream car, with a\n14\tdemanding passenger beside you, across the island of **Bellafiore** in a single day. You start on the Alba Coast at\n15\tsunrise and race to the Vespera Coast before the sun sets. A run is five one-minute stages. At every checkpoint the road\n16\tforks, so you choose your own route: 15 stages, 16 routes and 5 goals, each goal with its own ending. A clock counts down\n17\tthe whole way, and traffic, drifts, slipstreams and your passenger's whims fill the minutes in between.\n18\t\n19\t---\n20\t\n21\t## Contents\n22\t\n23\t1. Quick facts\n24\t2. What makes the source distinctive, and how OutBloom plays it\n25\t3. Originality rules\n26\t4. Deliverables, folders and setup\n27\t5. Architecture\n28\t6. Engine changes\n29\t7. Systems (the full spec of every mechanic)\n30\t8. Content (world, stages, goals, cars, cast, traffic, requests, missions, unlocks, music, writing)\n31\t9. Art direction and asset recipes\n32\t10. Build order and milestones\n33\t11. Risks and how to de-risk them\n34\t12. Testing\n35\t13. Definition of done: every TASK.md requirement and where it lives\n36\t- Appendices: A. tuning defaults (the dev sliders) · B. input map · C. glyphs · D. the debug handle and events · E. research notes\n37\t\n38\t---\n39\t\n40\t## 1. Quick facts\n41\t\n42\t| | |\n43\t|---|---|\n44\t| Genre | Arcade road racing: an OutRun-style checkpoint racer. A passenger-request \"date\" layer (*Love in Bloom*) and a mission career (*Coast 2 Coast Tour*) sit on top. |\n45\t| Perspective | Third-person chase camera behind the car, on a sprite-scaled pseudo-3D road (the engine's `road` module). Also a high chase and a bumper camera. Side-view vignettes for the start line, endings and the garage, and a stylised overhead map for the career. |\n46\t| Players | 1 in every mode. 2 on one machine, split screen (*Versus*). 1–4 online in AI Hub *ghost* sessions. |\n47\t| Engine | my-3D2dge **v0.85.2** handoff package (`dist/handoff`, commit `4390a98`), the full engine with every module, changed as listed in §6. |\n48\t| Look | Engine-native pixel art at 480×270 (`expand: true`), with a pixel-safe bloom pass, lens flare and dithered fog. |\n49\t| Audio | Everything is synthesised in code with the engine's chip synth, extended with instruments and effects (§6, §7.14–7.16). Voices are syllable-synth gibberish. |\n50\t| Deliverable | One self-contained HTML file in this folder's root, plus `exit-interview.json` beside it. |\n51\t\n52\t**Names.** Take the model/effort/timestamp part from *your* `TASK.md`. With this `TASK.md` the names are:\n53\t\n54\t- GameTitle slug: `OutBloom2006Coast2Coast`. SOURCE slug: `OutRun2006Coast2Coast` (spaces and punctuation removed, words joined).\n55\t- Dev folder: `OutBloom2006Coast2Coast-OutRun2006Coast2Coast-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174755/`\n56\t- Bundle: `OutBloom2006Coast2Coast-OutRun2006Coast2Coast-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174755.html`, in the root, beside `TASK.md`.\n57\t- Exit interview: `exit-interview.json`, in the root.\n58\t- The title on screen and in `aiHub.name` reads exactly **OutBloom 2006: Coast 2 Coast**.\n59\t\n60\t---\n61\t\n62\t## 2. What makes the source distinctive, and how OutBloom plays it\n63\t\n64\t| Source DNA | OutBloom mechanic | Where |\n65\t|---|---|---|\n66\t| Sun-drenched scenery that changes every minute (beaches, cities, mountains, canyons) | 15 hand-designed stages from 13 biome kits. Time of day runs from dawn to night as the trip goes west. The bloom lighting makes the sun and the sea glare. | §7.2, §8.2 |\n67\t| A branching route: choose a road at each checkpoint, with several goals | A five-row pyramid of stages. The road physically forks into two (a new renderer feature). Each goal has its own ending in three moods. | §7.1, §8.2–8.3 |\n68\t| Racing the clock, with checkpoints that extend the time | The **daylight clock**: a countdown, extended at each checkpoint gate. When it runs out, \"the sun sets\" and the run ends. | §7.6 |\n69\t| Weaving through civilian traffic | Lane traffic of 11 vehicle types with lane changes. Near misses and overtakes score. | §7.4 |\n70\t| The power slide: tap the brake while turning to drift at full speed | The drift model: a brake tap (classic), a quick down-up shift (manual), or the Drift button. Its angle, counter-steer and score are all modelled. | §7.3 |\n71\t| Slipstreaming the car ahead | Slipstream builds behind any vehicle and gives a slingshot when you pull out. | §7.3 |\n72\t| The passenger and her on-the-fly requests (Heart Attack) | **Love in Bloom**: three original passengers with 14 request types. You earn **petals**, which give a grade (E to AAA \"Full Bloom\"), and the grade sets the ending's mood. | §7.7, §8.7 |\n73\t| The Coast 2 Coast career: missions from characters, miles, unlocks | The **Coast 2 Coast Tour**: 14 postcard missions from four characters, with drift duels, draft dashes, a knockout and a rival-couple duel. You earn **seeds** and plant them in the **Garage Garden** to unlock cars, songs, colours and outfits. | §7.8–7.9, §8.8–8.9 |\n74\t| Choosing the song on the car radio before the race | A radio dial with 5 stations of original songs (plus 2 to unlock), switchable during the race. | §7.14, §8.10 |\n75\t| Dream cars from one famous marque | Five original open-top cars from the fictional marque **Fioretti**, from a 1962 roadster to a 2026 hypercar. | §8.4 |\n76\t| The flag-waving starter at the start line | **Bruno Brezza**, a retired rally champion who waves his sunflower flag at every start and gives the racing missions. | §8.5 |\n77\t| Time attack, online racing | Time Trial with ghosts, local split-screen Versus, and AI Hub ghost-mode online races. | §7.10–7.12 |\n78\t| Tone: carefree, romantic, cheesy, sunny | Short, peppy, cheeky lines, big friendly banners, and endings that are comic and romantic. | §8.11 |\n79\t\n80\tThe core loop, which goes at the top of `src/00-core.js`: **drive fast through a minute of beautiful road, read the bend\n81\tand drift it, thread the traffic, please your passenger, pick a fork, beat the clock to the next checkpoint, and do it\n82\tfive times to reach the coast.**\n83\t\n84\t---\n85\t\n86\t## 3. Originality rules\n87\t\n88\t`TASK.md` forbids recreating the source. Hold to these:\n89\t\n90\t- **No real brands.** No Ferrari or any real maker, model, badge or prancing horse. Every car is a Fioretti, designed\n91\t  here (§8.4).\n92\t- **No source characters.** No characters or names from the source. The cast is original (§8.5), and there is no\n93\t  \"Flagman\": Bruno is our own character.\n94\t- **No source places or layouts.** No source stage names or layouts, and no copied course order. Bellafiore and its 15\n95\t  stages are ours (§8.2).\n96\t- **No source music.** No melodies, quotes or remixes of the series' songs. Every song is composed fresh in the styles\n97\t  the series is known for (Latin fusion, Balearic pop-funk, shuffle, Eurobeat, city pop) (§8.10).\n98\t- **No copied UI.** No source logo, HUD layout or typography. Our HUD and menus are designed here (§7.17–7.18).\n99\t- **Our own mode names.** *Love in Bloom* (not \"Heart Attack\"), *Coast 2 Coast Tour*, *Seeds* and the *Garage Garden*\n100\t  (not \"miles\"). The checkpoint banner reads **TIME EXTENDED!**, not the series' phrase.\n101\t- **Reinterpreted mission types.** Mission types are new versions with their own names and rules: Drift Duel, Draft\n102\t  Dash, Last One Wilts, Heartbreakers (§8.8).\n103\t- **About screen disclaimer.** The About screen says: \"An original homage. Not affiliated with or endorsed by SEGA or\n104\t  Sumo Digital.\"\n105\t\n106\t---\n107\t\n108\t## 4. Deliverables, folders and setup\n109\t\n110\t### 4.1 Working rules (from TASK.md, made concrete)\n111\t\n112\t- **Stay in your folders.** Work only inside this folder and the dev folder. Other agents share this machine and the\n113\t  parent directories, so don't read other runs' folders.\n114\t- **Mind the disk.** `/tmp` on this machine is a small, nearly full tmpfs. Keep clones, caches and screenshots inside\n115\t  the dev folder (for example `dev/.cache/`), never in `/tmp`.\n116\t- **Get only what you need.** Fetch the engine with a shallow clone into the dev folder, then copy `dist/handoff/*`:\n117\t  `git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git .cache/my-3d2dge`. Pin to v0.85.2 (`4390a98`) and\n118\t  note any newer version you take.\n119\t- **Don't clone the AI Hub repo** (it is over 5 GB). Download only these four files with `curl -fsSL` from\n120\t  `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/`: `session.js` (the client, pasted verbatim),\n121\t  `harness.html`, `bridge.js` and `round.js` (the last three are for the conformance test only).\n122\t- **Servers.** Use a server only for the hub harness. Bind 127.0.0.1 on an OS-picked port\n123\t  (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`), read the port it prints, and confirm the page title is ours before\n124\t  trusting a result. Stop it by the PID you started. Never use `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`.\n125\t- **Prefer offline tools.** Use `file://` and the engine's headless tools for everything else.\n126\t\n127\t### 4.2 Dev folder layout\n128\t\n129\t```\n130\tOutBloom2006Coast2Coast-OutRun2006Coast2Coast-…/      (the dev folder)\n131\t  engine/                  copy of dist/handoff (engine, docs, tools, kits); my-3d2dge.js is CHANGED (see CHANGES.md)\n132\t  engine/CHANGES.md        every engine change: what and why (feeds the exit interview)\n133\t  vendor/aihub-session.js  AI Hub client 1.4.0, byte-for-byte as downloaded\n134\t  vendor/hub/              harness.html, bridge.js, round.js (tests only, never bundled)\n135\t  src/                     the game: plain scripts, one IIFE each, loaded in manifest order\n136\t  src/manifest.json        ordered list of src files (single source of truth for page, bundle and tests)\n137\t  index.html               dev page: loads the engine, then src files one by one (file:// works)\n138\t  build.mjs                writes the single-file bundle into the root\n139\t  tests/run.mjs            THE test command (§12)\n140\t  tests/suites/*.mjs       headless suites;  tests/browser/*.mjs  Playwright suites\n141\t  package.json             playwright 1.63.0 (as the handoff pins)\n142\t  .cache/                  clones, screenshots, filmstrips (git-ignored, never bundled)\n143\t```\n144\t\n145\t### 4.3 The bundle (`node build.mjs`)\n146\t\n147\t1. Start from the template in `index.html`. Put `<meta charset=\"utf-8\">` first, then **the session client inline as the\n148\t   first script in `<head>`**, unmodified, as the hub requires.\n149\t2. Add the hub tags: `hub:session` = `1`, `hub:session-players` = `1-4`, `hub:session-mode` = `ghost`,\n150\t   `hub:session-goal` = \"Fastest to the Flower Sprint goal wins\", `hub:session-howto` = \"Steer stick/←→ · gas\n151\t   R2/✕/W · brake L2/□/S · drift ○/Space\", and `hub:session-minutes` = `4`. Add the catalogue tags too: `hub:type` =\n152\t   `Game` and `hub:players` = `1-4`.\n153\t3. Add `<title>OutBloom 2006: Coast 2 Coast</title>`, the viewport tag and the page CSS from the handoff template.\n154\t4. Put `E.bootSplash`-style markup at the start of `<body>` (our title, the sunset colours).\n155\t5. Inline `engine/my-3d2dge.js`, then every `src/` file in manifest order, each as its own `<script>`.\n156\t6. Write the file into the root, then lint it: no `TODO`/`TBD`/`FIXME`/`placeholder`/`lorem` (case-insensitive), no\n157\t   `fetch(`/`WebSocket`/`RTCPeerConnection`/`XMLHttpRequest`/`alert(`/`confirm(`/`prompt(`/`window.open` outside the\n158\t   verbatim session client, and no external URLs loaded. The bundle needs no network at all.\n159\t\n160\t---\n161\t\n162\t## 5. Architecture\n163\t\n164\t### 5.1 Runtime shape\n165\t\n166\t- **One game.** `new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: [480, 270], expand: true, input: OB.INPUT, menu: {...}, id: 'outbloom2006' })`\n167\t  is created in `src/00-core.js` and exposed as `OB.game`.\n168\t- **One namespace.** Every file is `(() => { 'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge, OB = window.OB || (window.OB = {}); … })();`.\n169\t  Headless tests give the vm context a `window` (the context itself) and an `addEventListener` stand-in, as\n170\t  `kits/test.mjs` does.\n171\t- **Data is data.** Stages, cars, requests, missions, songs, lines and unlocks are plain objects in `src/01-data-*.js`,\n172\t  validated by the content-lint suite. Designers extend the game by editing these files; the dev tools (§7.23) are the\n173\t  in-game half of the \"normal designer tools\".\n174\t- **The sim is pure and deterministic.** `OB.Race` (with `CarSim`, `Traffic`, `Rivals`, `Requests` and `Rules`) runs on\n175\t  fixed `dt` steps from seeded streams. It knows nothing about drawing, and drawing only reads its state. Headless tests\n176\t  drive the sim through `game.advance` and `input.tap/hold`, exactly as a player would.\n177\t- **An event bus decouples everything.** `OB.bus.emit(name, data)` is the only channel from the sim to scoring, the\n178\t  passenger, audio, particles, the HUD and mission rules. Tests subscribe to it too, and the event list is Appendix D.\n179\t- **Randomness.** Gameplay never uses `Math.random`. Use `OB.rand.stream(label, seed)`, which is `E.rng`. In an AI Hub\n180\t  session the traffic stream is `AIHub.rng('traffic')`. Cosmetic effects use their own stream, so that toggling effects\n181\t  never changes a run.\n182\t\n183\t### 5.2 Source files (`src/manifest.json` order)\n184\t\n185\t| File | Owns | Key API |\n186\t|---|---|---|\n187\t| `00-core.js` | game creation, constants, units, `OB.bus`, `OB.rand`, `OB.tuning` (live) + `OB.TUNING_DEFAULTS`, env flags (`OB.env.hub`, `OB.env.touchOnly`, `OB.env.headless`) | `OB.tune(key)`, `OB.bus.on/off/emit`, `OB.rand.stream()` |\n188\t| `01-data-world.js` | biome kits, 15 stages, route graph, 5 goals, hub/versus routes, sky presets | `OB.STAGES`, `OB.GOALS`, `OB.KITS`, `OB.ROUTES` |\n189\t| `01-data-cars.js` | Fioretti cars, colours, traffic types | `OB.CARS`, `OB.PAINTS`, `OB.TRAFFIC` |\n190\t| `01-data-play.js` | request types, mission list, unlock beds, economy constants | `OB.REQUESTS`, `OB.MISSIONS`, `OB.BEDS` |\n191\t| `01-data-cast.js` | characters (rig specs, voice params, colours) and every line of text: barks, requests, reactions, endings, briefs, help, credits | `OB.CAST`, `OB.LINES`, `OB.line(key, vars)` |\n192\t| `01-data-music.js` | songs (radio, themes, jingles) as engine song objects with instrument tracks | `OB.SONGS`, `OB.STATIONS` |\n193\t| `01-data-about.js` | the About & Credits pages and the exit-interview answers (Source Information, the three feedback blocks, the `aiHub` block); `build.mjs` writes `exit-interview.json` from it | `OB.ABOUT` |\n194\t| `10-road.js` | `OB.World`: builds an `E.Road` per stage (junctions, strips, grounds), scenery placement from kit rules, landmark placement, roadside object lists, `OB.World.info(stage, z, x)` for physics | `world.stage(id)`, `world.junction(id)`, `info()` |\n195\t| `02-shapes.js` | every `E.SHAPES` entry the game adds (cars in three layers, traffic, landmarks, roadside sets, request objects), ids prefixed `ob-`; it assigns `E.SHAPES[...]` and nothing else, so `engine/tools/asset-sheet.mjs` can load it alone | `E.SHAPES['ob-car-orchidea-over']` |\n196\t| `11-art.js` | boot-time pre-rendering of the shapes into sprite banks, recolouring, string sprites, glyph and UI icons | `OB.Art.car(model, paint, yawIdx, layer)`, `OB.Art.sprite(id, variant)` |\n197\t| `12-cast.js` | Humanoid rigs (driver, passengers and outfits, Bruno, rivals, spectators), seated drawing in cars, portraits, poses per event | `OB.Cast.rig(id)`, `OB.Cast.drawOccupants(…)` |\n198\t| `13-fx.js` | screen-space particles (smoke, petals, sparks, dust, spray, confetti, fireworks, lanterns), pixel bloom, lens flare, heat shimmer, sun-shower, aurora, speed lines, skid marks | `OB.FX.emit()`, `OB.FX.post(g, sky, o)` |\n199\t| `20-car.js` | `OB.CarSim`: speed, gears, steering, curve push, drift, slipstream, off-road, rails, crests, collisions | `car.step(dt, intent, info)` |\n200\t| `21-traffic.js` | `OB.Traffic`: seeded spawn per stage, lanes, lane changes, junction branch choice, ribbon pairs, collisions with cars, near-miss and overtake detection, slipstream source | `traffic.step()`, `traffic.near()` |\n201\t| `22-drivers.js` | `OB.Brain`: the AI driver used by rivals, attract mode, tests and bots (racing line, curve speeds, traffic avoidance, drift use, request targeting, fork choice) | `brain.intent(state, world)` |\n202\t| `23-race.js` | `OB.Race`: a run from a `RaceConfig`; stages, clock, checkpoints, forks, goal, scoring and Bloom chain, ghosts (record/replay), rules hooks, per-player state (split screen) | `OB.Race.create(cfg)`, `race.step(dt)`, `race.snapshot()` |\n203\t| `24-requests.js` | Love in Bloom director: schedules, spawns request objects, evaluates, petals, fork preferences, grade | `OB.Requests.types[type]` |\n204\t| `25-missions.js` | the 14 mission rule sets, ranks and rewards | `OB.Rules[kind](cfg)` → hooks |\n205\t| `26-progress.js` | the save profile (versioned, migrated), seeds, garden, career, records, ghosts, endings seen | `OB.Profile.load/save`, `grant()`, `buy()` |\n206\t| `30-synth.js` | instrument presets on the extended engine audio, the effects bus, `OB.EngineSound` per car, tyre and wind loops, `OB.Ambience` per kit | `OB.Synth.inst(name)`, `engineSound.update()` |\n207\t| `31-voice.js` | `OB.Babble`: text → syllables → formant synthesis, moods, barks; the timing the speech bubbles type in | `OB.Babble.say(text, who, mood)` → duration |\n208\t| `32-radio.js` | stations, preview, in-race switching, bloom layer, ducking under speech | `OB.Radio.tune(i)` |\n209\t| `40-ui.js` | canvas 10-foot UI kit: screens, focus graph (spatial navigation), mouse hover and click, widgets (tile, carousel, toggle, slider, list, postcard, bed, dial), transitions, banners, toasts, glyph prompts, idle tracking | `OB.UI.screen(spec)`, `OB.UI.prompt(g, x, y, act, text)` |\n210\t| `41-hud.js` | the race HUD per viewport: clock, speedo, gear, score, Bloom chain, mini-map, passenger panel, request card, banners, hints | `OB.HUD.draw(r, player)` |\n211\t| `50-front.js` | scenes: `title`, `menu`, `garage` (car, paint, driver, passenger), `radio`, `howto`, `about` | |\n212\t| `51-race-scenes.js` | scenes: `race` (every mode), `startline` cutscene (inside race), `ending`, `results`, `gameover`, initials entry | |\n213\t| `52-career.js` | scenes: `tour` (map), `brief`, `debrief`, `garden` | |\n214\t| `53-modes.js` | scenes: `trial` (setup), `versus` (join + route), and the `race` split config | |\n215\t| `60-attract.js` | idle detection hook-up and the attract director (playlist, cheats, overlay, exit) | |\n216\t| `61-dev.js` | dev panel (tabs: Tuning, Cheats, Warp, Audio, Save, Info), debug overlay | `OB.Dev.open()` |\n217\t| `70-hub.js` | AI Hub session: detect, connect, own card, start sync, ghost send/receive, progress/result/time-up/end, session rules | |\n218\t| `71-touch.js` | touch-only detection and the \"Controller or keyboard required\" notice | |\n219\t| `90-main.js` | settings definitions, Shell theme, boot steps, scene registry, deep links, `game.debug`, `game.stateHash`, `game.start` | |\n220\t\n221\t### 5.3 Scenes and flow\n222\t\n223\t```\n224\tboot ─► (AIHub.active) ─► hubcard ─► race[hub] ─► hubover\n225\t   └──► title ◄──────────────────────────────── (any input) ◄── attract ◄── (10 s idle on title/menu screens)\n226\t          └► menu ─┬─ Arcade Run ───────► garage ► radio ► race[arcade] ─► ending ► results ► menu\n227\t                   ├─ Love in Bloom ────► garage ► radio ► race[love]   ─► ending ► results ► menu\n228\t                   ├─ Coast 2 Coast Tour ► tour ⇄ brief ► garage* ► radio ► race[mission] ► debrief ► tour\n229\t                   ├─ Time Trial ───────► trial ► garage ► radio ► race[trial] ► results ► trial\n230\t                   ├─ Versus (2P) ──────► versus(join, route) ► radio ► race[versus, split] ► results\n231\t                   ├─ Garage Garden ────► garden\n232\t                   └─ Settings · How to Play · About & Credits\n233\trace ── time up ─► gameover ► results\n234\tpause (engine menu): Resume · Restart · Retire · Dev Mode · Settings · Controls · Full screen · Help · Quit to title\n235\t```\n236\t\n237\t`*` A mission may fix the car or the passenger, and the garage then shows only what the mission lets you change.\n238\t\n239\t`race` is **one scene for every mode**: `game.go('race', RaceConfig)`. A `RaceConfig` is\n240\t`{ mode, route, car, paint, driver, passenger, song, transmission, rules, rivals, traffic, ghost, players, seed, cheats, bot }`.\n241\t`route` is either `{ pyramid: true }` (forks open) or a fixed stage list ending in a goal, or in a sprint goal arch.\n242\t\n243\t### 5.4 Coordinates and units\n244\t\n245\t- **Road position.** `stage` (an id), `z` (world units along that stage's road from its start), `x` (across, in half\n246\t  roads: −1 to 1 is the asphalt).\n247\t- **Segments.** One segment is 200 units long. Half the road is 2000 units at width factor 1. The camera sits 1000\n248\t  units above the road, with a field of view of 100° and a draw distance of 220 segments.\n249\t- **Speed** is in km/h. Distance per second is `v × 12` units, so 290 km/h is 3480 units per second (the ROAD kit's\n250\t  scale).\n251\t- **Lanes.** For N lanes the centres are `x_i = −1 + (2i + 1)/N`. Car half-widths in x: the player 0.24, traffic\n252\t  0.17–0.30 by type.\n253\t- **Time.** The sim uses only `dt` (1/120 s steps). The hub's `AIHub.time()` is read only for the start sync and the\n254\t  reported result.\n255\t\n256\t---\n257\t\n258\t## 6. Engine changes (all in the dev copy; log each in `engine/CHANGES.md`)\n259\t\n260\tThe engine is ours to change. These are the planned changes, and each one is needed. Everything else lives in `src/`.\n261\t\n262\t| # | Change | Why |\n263\t|---|---|---|\n264\t| E1 | **Road junctions.** A segment may carry `split: { s, curveL, curveR }`, the branches' spread in half roads. `draw()` takes `branches: [{ segs, x0, curveAcc }, …]` (the two continuations after a junction) and draws two road surfaces per depth band, each with its own lane marks, rumble and accumulated curve. Grass is split at the median line: the left ground colour left of it, the right one right of it. The road's `cars` and `things` take a `branch` (0 or 1) so they draw on their own branch. `projectBranch(b, z, x, y)` projects onto a branch. | The fork is the source's defining structure. The current module draws one road only. |\n265\t| E2 | **Road dressing.** Per-segment `w` (width factor, eased), `lanes`, and per-side grounds `groundL`/`groundR` (`grass`, `sand`, `water` with foam and shimmer rows, `rows` for crop and flower rows in two colours, `cobble`, `snow`, `rock`). **Edge strips** `edgeL`/`edgeR` (`rail`, `wall`, `cliff`, `hedge`, `facade`, `stonewall`) of a given height, drawn as quads like the tunnel walls. **Overhead spans** `span` (`arch`, `bunting`, `canopy`, `gallery` tunnel with open side, `snowshed`). A per-segment `tint` (a multiply colour for tunnels and night) and `lit` (pools of street-lamp light). An `onBand(k, p1, p2, seg)` hook for painting on the asphalt (photo box, skid marks, request markers). | OutRun-style roads need sea on one side, rails, cliffs, arcades and arches. These are generic road-renderer features. |\n266\t| E3 | **Analog input.** `input.analog(action)`: the largest of the action's pad button values (`buttons[i].value`, the triggers 6/7), 1 for any held key, 0 otherwise. `input.padValues` holds the values, and `input.setAnalog(action, v)` sets one for tests and bots. | Throttle and brake on R2/L2 must be progressive. The engine reads pad buttons as on/off only. |\n267\t| E4 | **Hub combo guard.** When `window.AIHub && AIHub.active`, Options (Pad9) does not pause while Create (Pad8) is held. | The protocol reserves Create + Options for the hub's corner bar. |\n268\t| E5 | **Glyph setting.** The standard `glyphs` setting defaults to `'playstation'`, with the options PlayStation and Xbox. | TASK.md: PlayStation glyphs, with a Settings option for Xbox. |\n269\t| E6 | **Synth extension.** A track or voice may take `inst` (a preset name) or the explicit fields: `env {a, d, s, r}`, `filter {type, f, q, env, envAmt}`, `unison n` and `detune` cents, `fm {ratio, index, decay}`, `noise` mix, `pan`. Tokens add chords (`C4+E4+G4`), accents (`C4!`) and slides (`C4~`). The drum kit adds `p` clap, `r` rim, `g`/`G` conga low/high, `b` cowbell, `z` shaker, `m` timbale, `y` ride and `T` high tom. Each track may set `send {delay, reverb}`. There is one music effects bus: a tempo-synced delay and a convolution reverb from a generated impulse, with `audio.reverb(amount, fade)`. There is a separate **voice bus** with its own volume. | The series' music is lush jazz-funk and fusion that bare square waves can't carry, and the voices need their own mix. |\n270\t| E7 | **Continuous voices.** `audio.loop(spec)` returns `{ set(params, ramp), stop(fade) }` for oscillators and noise that run continuously (engine, tyres, wind, bees, ambience). They follow mute, volume, the effects volume and pause (gain to 0 while `game.paused`), and do nothing headless. | The engine has one-shot sounds only, and a car needs a living engine note. |\n271\t| E8 | **Shell theme for 10 feet.** CSS variables for base font size (clamp 22–34 px), focus ring width (4 px), minimum button height (64 px) and panel width (at most 900 px), applied by our `new E.Shell(game, { theme })`. | The standard pause and settings screens must read at TV distance. |\n272\t\n273\tImplemented in game code, not the engine: the sky (`OB.Sky` wraps an `E.Backdrop` plus our own painter layers), the\n274\tpixel bloom and other post-effects (they work on the buffer inside an overlay), sprite pre-rendering (`E.shapeSprite`\n275\twith a custom `E.View('chase', 'Chase', 0, 9)`), and every UI.\n276\t\n277\t---\n278\t\n279\t## 7. Systems\n280\t\n281\t### 7.1 Road world and route graph (`10-road.js`)\n282\t\n283\t- **Stages.** Each stage is one `E.Road` built from its `sections` (`[n, curve, hill, opts]`; `opts` sets `w`, `lanes`,\n284\t  grounds, edges, spans, tunnel, `tint`, `lit`, and `event` for weather or gusts). Stage length is **820–980 segments**,\n285\t  plus a **junction of 80 segments** at the end of rows 1–4. Row-5 stages end in a 60-segment **goal run-in** with the\n286\t  goal arch and crowd.\n287\t- **Junction (fork) rules.**\n288\t  1. Junction segments are flat. The first **150 segments of every stage are flat** too, so both branches share one\n289\t     height profile across the whole draw distance.\n290\t  2. The spread `s` eases from 0 to 2.4 half-roads over the junction (`easeInOut`). The left branch's centre is at\n291\t     `−s`, the right branch's at `+s`.\n292\t  3. In the median (gore) there are flower-pot crash cushions, then a big **fork sign** showing both stage names, an\n293\t     arrow each and a difficulty rating of 1–5 flowers (`OB.Art` draws it, the text in the pixel font).\n294\t- **The decision.** At the junction's **decision point** (`s ≥ 1.05`) the player's side `sign(x)` picks the branch.\n295\t  - From then on `x` is relative to that branch's centre. The camera follows the absolute position, so the chosen\n296\t    branch stays centred while the other slides away.\n297\t  - When the junction ends, the race switches to the next stage at `z = 0` with `x` unchanged (already relative). The\n298\t    test in §12 proves this is seamless: the road's centre projects to within 1 px across the switch.\n299\t- **Before the decision**, `draw()` gets `branches`: the remaining junction segments plus the first 140 segments of\n300\t  *both* next stages, each offset by its branch's lateral end position."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f95c51ac-ca98-4f0d-bbff-431b6595447e","uuid":"7be7cb88-64b9-4fa0-9a2e-7c2681fbbc6b","timestamp":"2026-11-04T11:32:02.308Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-043153__xe1b7e1/work/PLAN.md","content":"# OutBloom 2006: Coast 2 Coast\n\n> *One day. One road. Every flower in bloom.*\n\nThis is the build plan for a feature-complete demo inspired by **OutRun 2006: Coast 2 Coast**. Read it together with\n`TASK.md`; where the two seem to disagree, `TASK.md` wins and this plan bends.\n\n**Title.** It was picked the way `TASK.md` asks: fifty playful riffs on the source's name were brainstormed silently,\nthen Python's `secrets.randbelow(50)` drew #49, **OutBloom 2006: Coast 2 Coast**. The title is final, so don't re-roll it.\n\"Bloom\" carries three meanings and the whole design leans on them: flowers (the world), love in bloom (the passenger),\nand the over-bright 2000s *bloom lighting* the source era is remembered for (the look).\n\n**The pitch.** An arcade road racer on the engine's pixel-art pseudo-3D road. You drive an open-top dream car, with a\ndemanding passenger beside you, across the island of **Bellafiore** in a single day. You start on the Alba Coast at\nsunrise and race to the Vespera Coast before the sun sets. A run is five one-minute stages. At every checkpoint the road\nforks, so you choose your own route: 15 stages, 16 routes and 5 goals, each goal with its own ending. A clock counts down\nthe whole way, and traffic, drifts, slipstreams and your passenger's whims fill the minutes in between.\n\n---\n\n## Contents\n\n1. Quick facts\n2. What makes the source distinctive, and how OutBloom plays it\n3. Originality rules\n4. Deliverables, folders and setup\n5. Architecture\n6. Engine changes\n7. Systems (the full spec of every mechanic)\n8. Content (world, stages, goals, cars, cast, traffic, requests, missions, unlocks, music, writing)\n9. Art direction and asset recipes\n10. Build order and milestones\n11. Risks and how to de-risk them\n12. Testing\n13. Definition of done: every TASK.md requirement and where it lives\n- Appendices: A. tuning defaults (the dev sliders) · B. input map · C. glyphs · D. the debug handle and events · E. research notes\n\n---\n\n## 1. Quick facts\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Genre | Arcade road racing: an OutRun-style checkpoint racer. A passenger-request \"date\" layer (*Love in Bloom*) and a mission career (*Coast 2 Coast Tour*) sit on top. |\n| Perspective | Third-person chase camera behind the car, on a sprite-scaled pseudo-3D road (the engine's `road` module). Also a high chase and a bumper camera. Side-view vignettes for the start line, endings and the garage, and a stylised overhead map for the career. |\n| Players | 1 in every mode. 2 on one machine, split screen (*Versus*). 1–4 online in AI Hub *ghost* sessions. |\n| Engine | my-3D2dge **v0.85.2** handoff package (`dist/handoff`, commit `4390a98`), the full engine with every module, changed as listed in §6. |\n| Look | Engine-native pixel art at 480×270 (`expand: true`), with a pixel-safe bloom pass, lens flare and dithered fog. |\n| Audio | Everything is synthesised in code with the engine's chip synth, extended with instruments and effects (§6, §7.14–7.16). Voices are syllable-synth gibberish. |\n| Deliverable | One self-contained HTML file in this folder's root, plus `exit-interview.json` beside it. |\n\n**Names.** Take the model/effort/timestamp part from *your* `TASK.md`. With this `TASK.md` the names are:\n\n- GameTitle slug: `OutBloom2006Coast2Coast`. SOURCE slug: `OutRun2006Coast2Coast` (spaces and punctuation removed, words joined).\n- Dev folder: `OutBloom2006Coast2Coast-OutRun2006Coast2Coast-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174755/`\n- Bundle: `OutBloom2006Coast2Coast-OutRun2006Coast2Coast-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174755.html`, in the root, beside `TASK.md`.\n- Exit interview: `exit-interview.json`, in the root.\n- The title on screen and in `aiHub.name` reads exactly **OutBloom 2006: Coast 2 Coast**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What makes the source distinctive, and how OutBloom plays it\n\n| Source DNA | OutBloom mechanic | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sun-drenched scenery that changes every minute (beaches, cities, mountains, canyons) | 15 hand-designed stages from 13 biome kits. Time of day runs from dawn to night as the trip goes west. The bloom lighting makes the sun and the sea glare. | §7.2, §8.2 |\n| A branching route: choose a road at each checkpoint, with several goals | A five-row pyramid of stages. The road physically forks into two (a new renderer feature). Each goal has its own ending in three moods. | §7.1, §8.2–8.3 |\n| Racing the clock, with checkpoints that extend the time | The **daylight clock**: a countdown, extended at each checkpoint gate. When it runs out, \"the sun sets\" and the run ends. | §7.6 |\n| Weaving through civilian traffic | Lane traffic of 11 vehicle types with lane changes. Near misses and overtakes score. | §7.4 |\n| The power slide: tap the brake while turning to drift at full speed | The drift model: a brake tap (classic), a quick down-up shift (manual), or the Drift button. Its angle, counter-steer and score are all modelled. | §7.3 |\n| Slipstreaming the car ahead | Slipstream builds behind any vehicle and gives a slingshot when you pull out. | §7.3 |\n| The passenger and her on-the-fly requests (Heart Attack) | **Love in Bloom**: three original passengers with 14 request types. You earn **petals**, which give a grade (E to AAA \"Full Bloom\"), and the grade sets the ending's mood. | §7.7, §8.7 |\n| The Coast 2 Coast career: missions from characters, miles, unlocks | The **Coast 2 Coast Tour**: 14 postcard missions from four characters, with drift duels, draft dashes, a knockout and a rival-couple duel. You earn **seeds** and plant them in the **Garage Garden** to unlock cars, songs, colours and outfits. | §7.8–7.9, §8.8–8.9 |\n| Choosing the song on the car radio before the race | A radio dial with 5 stations of original songs (plus 2 to unlock), switchable during the race. | §7.14, §8.10 |\n| Dream cars from one famous marque | Five original open-top cars from the fictional marque **Fioretti**, from a 1962 roadster to a 2026 hypercar. | §8.4 |\n| The flag-waving starter at the start line | **Bruno Brezza**, a retired rally champion who waves his sunflower flag at every start and gives the racing missions. | §8.5 |\n| Time attack, online racing | Time Trial with ghosts, local split-screen Versus, and AI Hub ghost-mode online races. | §7.10–7.12 |\n| Tone: carefree, romantic, cheesy, sunny | Short, peppy, cheeky lines, big friendly banners, and endings that are comic and romantic. | §8.11 |\n\nThe core loop, which goes at the top of `src/00-core.js`: **drive fast through a minute of beautiful road, read the bend\nand drift it, thread the traffic, please your passenger, pick a fork, beat the clock to the next checkpoint, and do it\nfive times to reach the coast.**\n\n---\n\n## 3. Originality rules\n\n`TASK.md` forbids recreating the source. Hold to these:\n\n- **No real brands.** No Ferrari or any real maker, model, badge or prancing horse. Every car is a Fioretti, designed\n  here (§8.4).\n- **No source characters.** No characters or names from the source. The cast is original (§8.5), and there is no\n  \"Flagman\": Bruno is our own character.\n- **No source places or layouts.** No source stage names or layouts, and no copied course order. Bellafiore and its 15\n  stages are ours (§8.2).\n- **No source music.** No melodies, quotes or remixes of the series' songs. Every song is composed fresh in the styles\n  the series is known for (Latin fusion, Balearic pop-funk, shuffle, Eurobeat, city pop) (§8.10).\n- **No copied UI.** No source logo, HUD layout or typography. Our HUD and menus are designed here (§7.17–7.18).\n- **Our own mode names.** *Love in Bloom* (not \"Heart Attack\"), *Coast 2 Coast Tour*, *Seeds* and the *Garage Garden*\n  (not \"miles\"). The checkpoint banner reads **TIME EXTENDED!**, not the series' phrase.\n- **Reinterpreted mission types.** Mission types are new versions with their own names and rules: Drift Duel, Draft\n  Dash, Last One Wilts, Heartbreakers (§8.8).\n- **About screen disclaimer.** The About screen says: \"An original homage. Not affiliated with or endorsed by SEGA or\n  Sumo Digital.\"\n\n---\n\n## 4. Deliverables, folders and setup\n\n### 4.1 Working rules (from TASK.md, made concrete)\n\n- **Stay in your folders.** Work only inside this folder and the dev folder. Other agents share this machine and the\n  parent directories, so don't read other runs' folders.\n- **Mind the disk.** `/tmp` on this machine is a small, nearly full tmpfs. Keep clones, caches and screenshots inside\n  the dev folder (for example `dev/.cache/`), never in `/tmp`.\n- **Get only what you need.** Fetch the engine with a shallow clone into the dev folder, then copy `dist/handoff/*`:\n  `git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git .cache/my-3d2dge`. Pin to v0.85.2 (`4390a98`) and\n  note any newer version you take.\n- **Don't clone the AI Hub repo** (it is over 5 GB). Download only these four files with `curl -fsSL` from\n  `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/`: `session.js` (the client, pasted verbatim),\n  `harness.html`, `bridge.js` and `round.js` (the last three are for the conformance test only).\n- **Servers.** Use a server only for the hub harness. Bind 127.0.0.1 on an OS-picked port\n  (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`), read the port it prints, and confirm the page title is ours before\n  trusting a result. Stop it by the PID you started. Never use `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`.\n- **Prefer offline tools.** Use `file://` and the engine's headless tools for everything else.\n\n### 4.2 Dev folder layout\n\n```\nOutBloom2006Coast2Coast-OutRun2006Coast2Coast-…/      (the dev folder)\n  engine/                  copy of dist/handoff (engine, docs, tools, kits); my-3d2dge.js is CHANGED (see CHANGES.md)\n  engine/CHANGES.md        every engine change: what and why (feeds the exit interview)\n  vendor/aihub-session.js  AI Hub client 1.4.0, byte-for-byte as downloaded\n  vendor/hub/              harness.html, bridge.js, round.js (tests only, never bundled)\n  src/                     the game: plain scripts, one IIFE each, loaded in manifest order\n  src/manifest.json        ordered list of src files (single source of truth for page, bundle and tests)\n  index.html               dev page: loads the engine, then src files one by one (file:// works)\n  build.mjs                writes the single-file bundle into the root\n  tests/run.mjs            THE test command (§12)\n  tests/suites/*.mjs       headless suites;  tests/browser/*.mjs  Playwright suites\n  package.json             playwright 1.63.0 (as the handoff pins)\n  .cache/                  clones, screenshots, filmstrips (git-ignored, never bundled)\n```\n\n### 4.3 The bundle (`node build.mjs`)\n\n1. Start from the template in `index.html`. Put `<meta charset=\"utf-8\">` first, then **the session client inline as the\n   first script in `<head>`**, unmodified, as the hub requires.\n2. Add the hub tags: `hub:session` = `1`, `hub:session-players` = `1-4`, `hub:session-mode` = `ghost`,\n   `hub:session-goal` = \"Fastest to the Flower Sprint goal wins\", `hub:session-howto` = \"Steer stick/←→ · gas\n   R2/✕/W · brake L2/□/S · drift ○/Space\", and `hub:session-minutes` = `4`. Add the catalogue tags too: `hub:type` =\n   `Game` and `hub:players` = `1-4`.\n3. Add `<title>OutBloom 2006: Coast 2 Coast</title>`, the viewport tag and the page CSS from the handoff template.\n4. Put `E.bootSplash`-style markup at the start of `<body>` (our title, the sunset colours).\n5. Inline `engine/my-3d2dge.js`, then every `src/` file in manifest order, each as its own `<script>`.\n6. Write the file into the root, then lint it: no `TODO`/`TBD`/`FIXME`/`placeholder`/`lorem` (case-insensitive), no\n   `fetch(`/`WebSocket`/`RTCPeerConnection`/`XMLHttpRequest`/`alert(`/`confirm(`/`prompt(`/`window.open` outside the\n   verbatim session client, and no external URLs loaded. The bundle needs no network at all.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Architecture\n\n### 5.1 Runtime shape\n\n- **One game.** `new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: [480, 270], expand: true, input: OB.INPUT, menu: {...}, id: 'outbloom2006' })`\n  is created in `src/00-core.js` and exposed as `OB.game`.\n- **One namespace.** Every file is `(() => { 'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge, OB = window.OB || (window.OB = {}); … })();`.\n  Headless tests give the vm context a `window` (the context itself) and an `addEventListener` stand-in, as\n  `kits/test.mjs` does.\n- **Data is data.** Stages, cars, requests, missions, songs, lines and unlocks are plain objects in `src/01-data-*.js`,\n  validated by the content-lint suite. Designers extend the game by editing these files; the dev tools (§7.23) are the\n  in-game half of the \"normal designer tools\".\n- **The sim is pure and deterministic.** `OB.Race` (with `CarSim`, `Traffic`, `Rivals`, `Requests` and `Rules`) runs on\n  fixed `dt` steps from seeded streams. It knows nothing about drawing, and drawing only reads its state. Headless tests\n  drive the sim through `game.advance` and `input.tap/hold`, exactly as a player would.\n- **An event bus decouples everything.** `OB.bus.emit(name, data)` is the only channel from the sim to scoring, the\n  passenger, audio, particles, the HUD and mission rules. Tests subscribe to it too, and the event list is Appendix D.\n- **Randomness.** Gameplay never uses `Math.random`. Use `OB.rand.stream(label, seed)`, which is `E.rng`. In an AI Hub\n  session the traffic stream is `AIHub.rng('traffic')`. Cosmetic effects use their own stream, so that toggling effects\n  never changes a run.\n\n### 5.2 Source files (`src/manifest.json` order)\n\n| File | Owns | Key API |\n|---|---|---|\n| `00-core.js` | game creation, constants, units, `OB.bus`, `OB.rand`, `OB.tuning` (live) + `OB.TUNING_DEFAULTS`, env flags (`OB.env.hub`, `OB.env.touchOnly`, `OB.env.headless`) | `OB.tune(key)`, `OB.bus.on/off/emit`, `OB.rand.stream()` |\n| `01-data-world.js` | biome kits, 15 stages, route graph, 5 goals, hub/versus routes, sky presets | `OB.STAGES`, `OB.GOALS`, `OB.KITS`, `OB.ROUTES` |\n| `01-data-cars.js` | Fioretti cars, colours, traffic types | `OB.CARS`, `OB.PAINTS`, `OB.TRAFFIC` |\n| `01-data-play.js` | request types, mission list, unlock beds, economy constants | `OB.REQUESTS`, `OB.MISSIONS`, `OB.BEDS` |\n| `01-data-cast.js` | characters (rig specs, voice params, colours) and every line of text: barks, requests, reactions, endings, briefs, help, credits | `OB.CAST`, `OB.LINES`, `OB.line(key, vars)` |\n| `01-data-music.js` | songs (radio, themes, jingles) as engine song objects with instrument tracks | `OB.SONGS`, `OB.STATIONS` |\n| `01-data-about.js` | the About & Credits pages and the exit-interview answers (Source Information, the three feedback blocks, the `aiHub` block); `build.mjs` writes `exit-interview.json` from it | `OB.ABOUT` |\n| `10-road.js` | `OB.World`: builds an `E.Road` per stage (junctions, strips, grounds), scenery placement from kit rules, landmark placement, roadside object lists, `OB.World.info(stage, z, x)` for physics | `world.stage(id)`, `world.junction(id)`, `info()` |\n| `02-shapes.js` | every `E.SHAPES` entry the game adds (cars in three layers, traffic, landmarks, roadside sets, request objects), ids prefixed `ob-`; it assigns `E.SHAPES[...]` and nothing else, so `engine/tools/asset-sheet.mjs` can load it alone | `E.SHAPES['ob-car-orchidea-over']` |\n| `11-art.js` | boot-time pre-rendering of the shapes into sprite banks, recolouring, string sprites, glyph and UI icons | `OB.Art.car(model, paint, yawIdx, layer)`, `OB.Art.sprite(id, variant)` |\n| `12-cast.js` | Humanoid rigs (driver, passengers and outfits, Bruno, rivals, spectators), seated drawing in cars, portraits, poses per event | `OB.Cast.rig(id)`, `OB.Cast.drawOccupants(…)` |\n| `13-fx.js` | screen-space particles (smoke, petals, sparks, dust, spray, confetti, fireworks, lanterns), pixel bloom, lens flare, heat shimmer, sun-shower, aurora, speed lines, skid marks | `OB.FX.emit()`, `OB.FX.post(g, sky, o)` |\n| `20-car.js` | `OB.CarSim`: speed, gears, steering, curve push, drift, slipstream, off-road, rails, crests, collisions | `car.step(dt, intent, info)` |\n| `21-traffic.js` | `OB.Traffic`: seeded spawn per stage, lanes, lane changes, junction branch choice, ribbon pairs, collisions with cars, near-miss and overtake detection, slipstream source | `traffic.step()`, `traffic.near()` |\n| `22-drivers.js` | `OB.Brain`: the AI driver used by rivals, attract mode, tests and bots (racing line, curve speeds, traffic avoidance, drift use, request targeting, fork choice) | `brain.intent(state, world)` |\n| `23-race.js` | `OB.Race`: a run from a `RaceConfig`; stages, clock, checkpoints, forks, goal, scoring and Bloom chain, ghosts (record/replay), rules hooks, per-player state (split screen) | `OB.Race.create(cfg)`, `race.step(dt)`, `race.snapshot()` |\n| `24-requests.js` | Love in Bloom director: schedules, spawns request objects, evaluates, petals, fork preferences, grade | `OB.Requests.types[type]` |\n| `25-missions.js` | the 14 mission rule sets, ranks and rewards | `OB.Rules[kind](cfg)` → hooks |\n| `26-progress.js` | the save profile (versioned, migrated), seeds, garden, career, records, ghosts, endings seen | `OB.Profile.load/save`, `grant()`, `buy()` |\n| `30-synth.js` | instrument presets on the extended engine audio, the effects bus, `OB.EngineSound` per car, tyre and wind loops, `OB.Ambience` per kit | `OB.Synth.inst(name)`, `engineSound.update()` |\n| `31-voice.js` | `OB.Babble`: text → syllables → formant synthesis, moods, barks; the timing the speech bubbles type in | `OB.Babble.say(text, who, mood)` → duration |\n| `32-radio.js` | stations, preview, in-race switching, bloom layer, ducking under speech | `OB.Radio.tune(i)` |\n| `40-ui.js` | canvas 10-foot UI kit: screens, focus graph (spatial navigation), mouse hover and click, widgets (tile, carousel, toggle, slider, list, postcard, bed, dial), transitions, banners, toasts, glyph prompts, idle tracking | `OB.UI.screen(spec)`, `OB.UI.prompt(g, x, y, act, text)` |\n| `41-hud.js` | the race HUD per viewport: clock, speedo, gear, score, Bloom chain, mini-map, passenger panel, request card, banners, hints | `OB.HUD.draw(r, player)` |\n| `50-front.js` | scenes: `title`, `menu`, `garage` (car, paint, driver, passenger), `radio`, `howto`, `about` | |\n| `51-race-scenes.js` | scenes: `race` (every mode), `startline` cutscene (inside race), `ending`, `results`, `gameover`, initials entry | |\n| `52-career.js` | scenes: `tour` (map), `brief`, `debrief`, `garden` | |\n| `53-modes.js` | scenes: `trial` (setup), `versus` (join + route), and the `race` split config | |\n| `60-attract.js` | idle detection hook-up and the attract director (playlist, cheats, overlay, exit) | |\n| `61-dev.js` | dev panel (tabs: Tuning, Cheats, Warp, Audio, Save, Info), debug overlay | `OB.Dev.open()` |\n| `70-hub.js` | AI Hub session: detect, connect, own card, start sync, ghost send/receive, progress/result/time-up/end, session rules | |\n| `71-touch.js` | touch-only detection and the \"Controller or keyboard required\" notice | |\n| `90-main.js` | settings definitions, Shell theme, boot steps, scene registry, deep links, `game.debug`, `game.stateHash`, `game.start` | |\n\n### 5.3 Scenes and flow\n\n```\nboot ─► (AIHub.active) ─► hubcard ─► race[hub] ─► hubover\n   └──► title ◄──────────────────────────────── (any input) ◄── attract ◄── (10 s idle on title/menu screens)\n          └► menu ─┬─ Arcade Run ───────► garage ► radio ► race[arcade] ─► ending ► results ► menu\n                   ├─ Love in Bloom ────► garage ► radio ► race[love]   ─► ending ► results ► menu\n                   ├─ Coast 2 Coast Tour ► tour ⇄ brief ► garage* ► radio ► race[mission] ► debrief ► tour\n                   ├─ Time Trial ───────► trial ► garage ► radio ► race[trial] ► results ► trial\n                   ├─ Versus (2P) ──────► versus(join, route) ► radio ► race[versus, split] ► results\n                   ├─ Garage Garden ────► garden\n                   └─ Settings · How to Play · About & Credits\nrace ── time up ─► gameover ► results\npause (engine menu): Resume · Restart · Retire · Dev Mode · Settings · Controls · Full screen · Help · Quit to title\n```\n\n`*` A mission may fix the car or the passenger, and the garage then shows only what the mission lets you change.\n\n`race` is **one scene for every mode**: `game.go('race', RaceConfig)`. A `RaceConfig` is\n`{ mode, route, car, paint, driver, passenger, song, transmission, rules, rivals, traffic, ghost, players, seed, cheats, bot }`.\n`route` is either `{ pyramid: true }` (forks open) or a fixed stage list ending in a goal, or in a sprint goal arch.\n\n### 5.4 Coordinates and units\n\n- **Road position.** `stage` (an id), `z` (world units along that stage's road from its start), `x` (across, in half\n  roads: −1 to 1 is the asphalt).\n- **Segments.** One segment is 200 units long. Half the road is 2000 units at width factor 1. The camera sits 1000\n  units above the road, with a field of view of 100° and a draw distance of 220 segments.\n- **Speed** is in km/h. Distance per second is `v × 12` units, so 290 km/h is 3480 units per second (the ROAD kit's\n  scale).\n- **Lanes.** For N lanes the centres are `x_i = −1 + (2i + 1)/N`. Car half-widths in x: the player 0.24, traffic\n  0.17–0.30 by type.\n- **Time.** The sim uses only `dt` (1/120 s steps). The hub's `AIHub.time()` is read only for the start sync and the\n  reported result.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Engine changes (all in the dev copy; log each in `engine/CHANGES.md`)\n\nThe engine is ours to change. These are the planned changes, and each one is needed. Everything else lives in `src/`.\n\n| # | Change | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| E1 | **Road junctions.** A segment may carry `split: { s, curveL, curveR }`, the branches' spread in half roads. `draw()` takes `branches: [{ segs, x0, curveAcc }, …]` (the two continuations after a junction) and draws two road surfaces per depth band, each with its own lane marks, rumble and accumulated curve. Grass is split at the median line: the left ground colour left of it, the right one right of it. The road's `cars` and `things` take a `branch` (0 or 1) so they draw on their own branch. `projectBranch(b, z, x, y)` projects onto a branch. | The fork is the source's defining structure. The current module draws one road only. |\n| E2 | **Road dressing.** Per-segment `w` (width factor, eased), `lanes`, and per-side grounds `groundL`/`groundR` (`grass`, `sand`, `water` with foam and shimmer rows, `rows` for crop and flower rows in two colours, `cobble`, `snow`, `rock`). **Edge strips** `edgeL`/`edgeR` (`rail`, `wall`, `cliff`, `hedge`, `facade`, `stonewall`) of a given height, drawn as quads like the tunnel walls. **Overhead spans** `span` (`arch`, `bunting`, `canopy`, `gallery` tunnel with open side, `snowshed`). A per-segment `tint` (a multiply colour for tunnels and night) and `lit` (pools of street-lamp light). An `onBand(k, p1, p2, seg)` hook for painting on the asphalt (photo box, skid marks, request markers). | OutRun-style roads need sea on one side, rails, cliffs, arcades and arches. These are generic road-renderer features. |\n| E3 | **Analog input.** `input.analog(action)`: the largest of the action's pad button values (`buttons[i].value`, the triggers 6/7), 1 for any held key, 0 otherwise. `input.padValues` holds the values, and `input.setAnalog(action, v)` sets one for tests and bots. | Throttle and brake on R2/L2 must be progressive. The engine reads pad buttons as on/off only. |\n| E4 | **Hub combo guard.** When `window.AIHub && AIHub.active`, Options (Pad9) does not pause while Create (Pad8) is held. | The protocol reserves Create + Options for the hub's corner bar. |\n| E5 | **Glyph setting.** The standard `glyphs` setting defaults to `'playstation'`, with the options PlayStation and Xbox. | TASK.md: PlayStation glyphs, with a Settings option for Xbox. |\n| E6 | **Synth extension.** A track or voice may take `inst` (a preset name) or the explicit fields: `env {a, d, s, r}`, `filter {type, f, q, env, envAmt}`, `unison n` and `detune` cents, `fm {ratio, index, decay}`, `noise` mix, `pan`. Tokens add chords (`C4+E4+G4`), accents (`C4!`) and slides (`C4~`). The drum kit adds `p` clap, `r` rim, `g`/`G` conga low/high, `b` cowbell, `z` shaker, `m` timbale, `y` ride and `T` high tom. Each track may set `send {delay, reverb}`. There is one music effects bus: a tempo-synced delay and a convolution reverb from a generated impulse, with `audio.reverb(amount, fade)`. There is a separate **voice bus** with its own volume. | The series' music is lush jazz-funk and fusion that bare square waves can't carry, and the voices need their own mix. |\n| E7 | **Continuous voices.** `audio.loop(spec)` returns `{ set(params, ramp), stop(fade) }` for oscillators and noise that run continuously (engine, tyres, wind, bees, ambience). They follow mute, volume, the effects volume and pause (gain to 0 while `game.paused`), and do nothing headless. | The engine has one-shot sounds only, and a car needs a living engine note. |\n| E8 | **Shell theme for 10 feet.** CSS variables for base font size (clamp 22–34 px), focus ring width (4 px), minimum button height (64 px) and panel width (at most 900 px), applied by our `new E.Shell(game, { theme })`. | The standard pause and settings screens must read at TV distance. |\n\nImplemented in game code, not the engine: the sky (`OB.Sky` wraps an `E.Backdrop` plus our own painter layers), the\npixel bloom and other post-effects (they work on the buffer inside an overlay), sprite pre-rendering (`E.shapeSprite`\nwith a custom `E.View('chase', 'Chase', 0, 9)`), and every UI.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Systems\n\n### 7.1 Road world and route graph (`10-road.js`)\n\n- **Stages.** Each stage is one `E.Road` built from its `sections` (`[n, curve, hill, opts]`; `opts` sets `w`, `lanes`,\n  grounds, edges, spans, tunnel, `tint`, `lit`, and `event` for weather or gusts). Stage length is **820–980 segments**,\n  plus a **junction of 80 segments** at the end of rows 1–4. Row-5 stages end in a 60-segment **goal run-in** with the\n  goal arch and crowd.\n- **Junction (fork) rules.**\n  1. Junction segments are flat. The first **150 segments of every stage are flat** too, so both branches share one\n     height profile across the whole draw distance.\n  2. The spread `s` eases from 0 to 2.4 half-roads over the junction (`easeInOut`). The left branch's centre is at\n     `−s`, the right branch's at `+s`.\n  3. In the median (gore) there are flower-pot crash cushions, then a big **fork sign** showing both stage names, an\n     arrow each and a difficulty rating of 1–5 flowers (`OB.Art` draws it, the text in the pixel font).\n- **The decision.** At the junction's **decision point** (`s ≥ 1.05`) the player's side `sign(x)` picks the branch.\n  - From then on `x` is relative to that branch's centre. The camera follows the absolute position, so the chosen\n    branch stays centred while the other slides away.\n  - When the junction ends, the race switches to the next stage at `z = 0` with `x` unchanged (already relative). The\n    test in §12 proves this is seamless: the road's centre projects to within 1 px across the switch.\n- **Before the decision**, `draw()` gets `branches`: the remaining junction segments plus the first 140 segments of\n  *both* next stages, each offset by its branch's lateral end position.","numLines":300,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1758}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01M7PfbC23RJb62YJAAfZF8k","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
