{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011EMpuDnjoAhdXHhmHk6jAs","type":"tool_result","content":"300\t  *both* next stages, each offset by its branch's lateral end position.\n301\t- **Checkpoint gate.** It stands at the start of the next stage (segment 2): an arch of flowers with a \"CHECKPOINT\"\n302\t  banner. Passing it extends the clock and shows the stage card.\n303\t- **Route graph.** The pyramid is rows 1–5, with stage *(r, c)* forking to *(r+1, c)* on the left and *(r+1, c+1)* on\n304\t  the right. That makes **16 routes**, and every row-5 stage ends at its own goal (§8.2). Left branches are always the\n305\t  gentler road: fewer hard bends, less traffic, wider lanes.\n306\t- **Fixed routes.** Hub, Versus and missions use fixed stage lists. A fork on a fixed route has the other branch closed\n307\t  by a striped barrier and a \"ROAD CLOSED\" sign, drawn and solid. A fixed route may end at a **sprint goal arch** placed\n308\t  at the end of any stage.\n309\t- **Scenery from kit rules.** Each biome kit lists *placement rules*: `{ sprite, side: 'L' | 'R' | 'both', offset:\n310\t  [min, max] (half roads beyond the edge), every: segments, jitter, chance, cluster }` plus *runs* (dense rows such as a\n311\t  palm avenue or a fence line). Placement uses the stage's own seed, so the same stage always looks the same. Landmarks\n312\t  are stage data (`{ at: fraction, side, kind, offset }`). Nothing is placed within 0.15 half-roads of the asphalt\n313\t  unless it is a rail or a strip.\n314\t- **Physics queries.** `world.info(stage, z, x)` returns `{ curve, slopeY, curvY (for crests), w, lanes, onRoad,\n315\t  ground, edgeL, edgeR, tunnel, hits (scenery touching, from road.hits), cushion (in the gore), gust }` for the sim.\n316\t- **Mirror Roads.** An unlockable that builds every stage with its curves negated and its sides swapped. It is a\n317\t  build-time flag, and the same code paths run.\n318\t\n319\t### 7.2 Rendering a race frame (`51-race-scenes.js`, `13-fx.js`, `41-hud.js`)\n320\t\n321\tEach viewport is drawn in this order, all in screen pixels; on a split screen it repeats per `r.viewport`:\n322\t\n323\t1. **Sky.** `OB.Sky` for the stage and time of day: gradient, sun or moon, clouds, far layers (sea, hills, mountains,\n324\t   mesas, skyline, aurora, balloons, lanterns). It scrolls with `turn` (the curve times speed added up). Near the end of\n325\t   a junction it dissolves with an ordered dither over 1.5 s into the chosen stage's sky.\n326\t2. **Road.** `stage.road.draw(r, { z, x, h, turn, cars, branches })`. That draws, far to near: bands, grounds, strips\n327\t   and spans, with scenery, traffic, rivals, ghosts and request objects interleaved by segment. Fog colour and density\n328\t   come from the kit and the time of day.\n329\t3. **Asphalt marks** through `onBand`: skid marks (a ring buffer of 400 per stage), the photo box, headlight pools at\n330\t   night (additive stepped alpha) and street-lamp pools.\n331\t4. **The player's car** at `road.project(z + ahead, x)`: shadow, wheels, the under layer, occupants, the over layer, and\n332\t   the lights layer when braking or at night. Then the particles near the car: tyre smoke, sparks, dust or sand,\n333\t   spray, backfire.\n334\t5. **Screen effects.** Petals and rain, speed lines (above 260 km/h), heat shimmer in the desert and canyon, then the\n335\t   **pixel bloom** (below), then the **lens flare** when the sun is on screen and not in a tunnel.\n336\t6. **HUD** (§7.18), then dialogue bubbles, banners and toasts, then the debug overlay when it is on.\n337\t\n338\t**Pixel bloom** (`OB.FX.post`), which must look like pixel art and never like blur:\n339\t\n340\t1. Read the viewport's `ImageData`, then sum pixels brighter than a threshold (luma 200 by default, from the kit) into\n341\t   a ¼-resolution grid.\n342\t2. Blur that grid with two 3×3 box passes and quantise it to 4 levels.\n343\t3. Add it back at 4× (nearest neighbour), through a 4×4 Bayer dither and tinted toward the sun's colour.\n344\t4. Strength by time of day: dawn 0.55, noon 0.35, sunset 0.7, night 0.6 (neon). Reduce motion keeps it, as it is\n345\t   static.\n346\t\n347\tLens flare is 5 ghosts (hexagons and discs drawn with `px.blend('add')` at stepped alpha) along the line from the sun\n348\tthrough the screen's centre. Its strength scales with how centred the sun is.\n349\t\n350\t**Cameras.** The camera is a setting, and the △/C key cycles it in a race.\n351\t\n352\t| Camera | Height `h` | Car drawn | Notes |\n353\t|---|---|---|---|\n354\t| **Chase** (default) | 1000 | full size | — |\n355\t| **High** | 1350 | 0.8× | — |\n356\t| **Bumper** | 380 | not drawn | a bonnet strip and the cockpit edge; the occupants' heads aren't shown |\n357\t\n358\t- **Following.** Camera x is `0.85·x + 0.15·x_lagged`, where `x_lagged` follows `x` at 6/s. In a drift the lag rises\n359\t  so the car swings out on screen.\n360\t- **Speed.** The field of view widens from 100° to 108° at top speed.\n361\t- **Shake.** Rumble strips and off-road driving shake the camera through `game.shake`, which respects the comfort\n362\t  settings.\n363\t\n364\t**Night and tunnels.**\n365\t- **Tunnels.** The `tint` darkens bands and sprites (multiply). Sodium lamps along the walls flash past as stripes.\n366\t- **Night.** The player's headlights cast two additive cones on the asphalt ahead, and traffic shows red tail lights\n367\t  and white headlights. Neon is emissive, so it is drawn after the tint.\n368\t\n369\t### 7.3 Car handling (`20-car.js`)\n370\t\n371\tThe state is `{ z, x, vx, v (km/h), gear, rpm, drift: { on, dir, a, t, score }, slip, slingT, air, airT, crash: { t, kind },\n372\tbumpT, scrape, offroad }`. The intent is `{ steer −1..1 (analog), gas 0..1, brake 0..1, driftBtn, shiftUp, shiftDown }`.\n373\tEvery number below is a live tuning value (Appendix A), and per-car stats scale them (§8.4).\n374\t\n375\t1. **Engine.**\n376\t   - **Acceleration.** `dv = A0·(1 − (v/vtop)^1.6)·gas − drag − brake·240 − offroad`. Here `A0` is taken from the car's\n377\t     0–100 km/h time, and `drag = 18 + 0.00025·v²` km/h/s.\n378\t   - **Automatic** (the default) shifts at 18/34/50/66/82 % of `vtop`. `rpm` sweeps 1,500–8,000 inside each gear.\n379\t   - **Manual.** R1/E shifts up, L1/Q shifts down. There is a rev limiter at the top of each gear and weak pull below\n380\t     2,500 rpm. A down-then-up shift within 0.3 s, while steering above 120 km/h, starts a drift (classic input).\n381\t   - **Rev start.** Holding gas during the countdown moves `rpm`. If the needle is in the green zone (6,000–7,000) at\n382\t     GO, you get **PERFECT START** (+25 km/h). Above 7,500 the wheels spin and you get nothing.\n383\t2. **Steering.**\n384\t   - **Target.** The lateral-speed target is `vx* = steer · steerRate · min(1, v/70) · (1 − 0.22·v/vtop)`, in half-roads\n385\t     per second. `vx` approaches it at `steerResponse` (9/s).\n386\t   - **Keyboard.** Steering ramps from 0 to 1 over 0.12 s and returns in 0.08 s. The analog stick is used as read,\n387\t     after the dead zone.\n388\t   - **Steering assist** (setting: Off/Low/High). Off the road it nudges `vx` back toward the lane at 0/0.3/0.6 per\n389\t     second. On High it also brakes by itself before bends the car can't make.\n390\t3. **Curve push.** `vx −= curve · (v/vtop)² · curvePush · dt` (curvePush is 0.62 by default). Design targets, which the\n391\t   tests check:\n392\t   - A curve of 3 at full speed can be held without drifting.\n393\t   - A curve of 4 needs a lift or a drift above about 92 % of top speed.\n394\t   - Curves of 5–6 need a drift or braking. A clean drift takes a 6 at about 85 % of top speed.\n395\t4. **Drift.**\n396\t   - **Entry.** Any enabled input starts it while `v > driftMinSpeed` (120) and `|steer| > 0.4`:\n397\t     - **Brake tap:** brake above 0.3 for at most `driftTapWindow` (0.35 s), then released.\n398\t     - **Down-up shift.**\n399\t     - **The Drift button** (○/Space). Held, it keeps the drift deeper, like a handbrake.\n400\t     The drift's direction is `sign(steer)`, and the \"Drift input\" setting picks which inputs are on (default: all).\n401\t   - **Angle.** `a` approaches a target at 3.5 rad/s. The target is `dir · (0.35 + 0.40·|steer|)` when steering into the\n402\t     drift, `dir · 0.30` with the stick centred, and `dir · 0.12` when counter-steering past 0.5. It is capped at\n403\t     `driftAngleMax` (0.75 rad).\n404\t   - **Effects.**\n405\t     - The curve push is multiplied by `1 − driftGrip · min(1, |a|/0.5)` (driftGrip is 0.85).\n406\t     - The steering rate is multiplied by `driftSteerBoost` (1.3).\n407\t     - Speed drops by `driftSpeedLoss · |a|/0.75` km/h/s (16).\n408\t   - **Exit.** It ends when `|a| < 0.08` for 0.2 s, when `v < 90`, after a crash, or when the curve is below 0.5 with the\n409\t     stick centred for 0.4 s.\n410\t   - **Drift score.** It accumulates `v · |a| · dt · 10`. At the exit it emits `drift:end { score, length }`.\n411\t   - **Visuals.** The yaw frame is `vx·0.16 + a`. Smoke comes from both rear wheels, skid marks are laid, and the tyre\n412\t     squeal rises with `|a|`.\n413\t5. **Slipstream.**\n414\t   - **Building.** The nearest vehicle ahead with `300 < dz < slipRange` (1,800) and `|dx| < slipWidth` (0.22) builds\n415\t     `slip` at 1.25/s. Otherwise `slip` decays at 2.5/s.\n416\t   - **Effects.** Top speed rises by `slipTopBonus·slip` (+6 %) and drag drops by up to 50 %. Wind lines show at\n417\t     `slip > 0.5`.\n418\t   - **Slingshot.** Pulling out with `slip ≥ 0.8` gives `slingshotBoost` (+14 km/h, eased over 0.8 s) and emits\n419\t     `slingshot`.\n420\t6. **Off-road and edges.**\n421\t   - **Off-road** (`|x| > 1` on grass, sand, rows or snow): top speed falls to `offroadTop` (50 %), with an extra 120\n422\t     km/h/s of drag while above it, plus a bounce and dust or sand.\n423\t   - **Water** behind a rail can't be reached. Water with no rail (the causeway shallows): a big splash, then the car is\n424\t     put back like a crash.\n425\t   - **Rails, walls and cliffs.** `x` is held at the strip (±1.22). The car scrapes: −40 km/h/s, sparks, a screech, a\n426\t     rumble, no spin.\n427\t7. **Collisions.**\n428\t   - **With traffic, rivals or the other player** (`dz` under one car length, about 520 units, and `|dx|` under 90 % of\n429\t     the summed half-widths):\n430\t     - **Bump**, when the closing speed is below `spinThreshold` (70 km/h): `v = v_other·0.92 − 8`, the cars are pushed\n431\t       0.08 apart, and `car:bump` is emitted.\n432\t     - **Spin-out** otherwise: 1.3 s of a full 360° spin, `v` drops to 30 % (at least 40 km/h), the other car spins and\n433\t       slides aside, and `car:crash` is emitted.\n434\t     - A side contact (similar `z`) pushes the cars apart in `x` and costs 8 % of speed.\n435\t   - **Scenery** (`hits` beyond the edge): a spin-out above 110 km/h, below that a bump back to the asphalt at half\n436\t     speed.\n437\t   - **Gore cushions:** a bump, pushing the car toward the nearer branch.\n438\t   - **After a spin-out**, the car is put back at `x = ±0.75` facing ahead and blinks for 1 s. It can't crash again\n439\t     during that second.\n440\t8. **Crests and air.** When the road's vertical curvature at `z + ahead` falls below −k and `v > 200`, the car is\n441\t   airborne for about 0.3–0.6 s.\n442\t   - **In the air:** the sprite lifts, the shadow stays down, steering is cut to 30 % and there is no curve push.\n443\t   - **Landing:** the suspension bounces, sparks fly on a hard landing, and `air:land` is emitted. The passenger reacts.\n444\t9. **Gusts** (Seafoam Causeway). Stage events push `vx` with a lateral impulse over 1.2 s, warned by a wind-sock icon\n445\t   and spray 1 s ahead.\n446\t\n447\t### 7.4 Traffic (`21-traffic.js`)\n448\t\n449\t- **Spawning.** Vehicles spawn per stage from its traffic mix (§8.6), with a seeded stream per stage visit\n450\t  (`runSeed ⊕ stageIndex`, or `AIHub.rng('traffic')` in a hub session). That gives every hub player the same traffic.\n451\t  They spawn from `z + 600` to the end of the draw distance and despawn 3,000 units behind the slowest player.\n452\t  - **Density.** The stage's density (vehicles per 10,000 units per lane) × the `trafficDensity` tuning × the\n453\t    difficulty (Easy 0.75, Normal 1, Hard 1.3).\n454\t  - **Spacing.** At least 900 units apart in one lane. A gap of two free lanes is always left somewhere within any\n455\t    1,500 units, so the road is never walled off.\n456\t- **Behaviour.** Each type has a speed range, and vehicles keep their lane.\n457\t  - **Lane changes.** Blocked by a slower car within 1,200 units, a vehicle blinks for 0.8 s, then changes lane over\n458\t    1.2 s if the lane is free.\n459\t  - **Horn.** The horn (△/H) makes the vehicle ahead within 2,500 units in your lane change lanes if it can. It has a\n460\t    2 s cooldown, and the passenger may comment.\n461\t- **Junctions.** Each vehicle on a junction is assigned a branch from its stream, so they split naturally.\n462\t- **Detection for scoring and requests.**\n463\t  - **Overtake:** your `z` passes theirs while `|dx| < 0.9` and there was no contact in the last 0.5 s. Emits\n464\t    `overtake`.\n465\t  - **Near miss:** passing with an edge-to-edge gap `0 < |dx| − (w1 + w2) < 0.12` half-roads, a closing speed above\n466\t    50 km/h and no contact. Emits `nearmiss`.\n467\t  - **Slipstream source:** the vehicle ahead, per §7.3.\n468\t- **Ribbon pairs.** A Love in Bloom spawn is two vehicles side by side in adjacent lanes at the same speed, with a pink\n469\t  ribbon between them. Driving through the gap (`|x − mid| < 0.16`) as you pass snaps it.\n470\t- **Drawing.** The yaw frame is chosen from the vehicle's projected screen offset (perspective), plus its lane-change\n471\t  angle. Blinkers, brake lights (when slowing) and, at night, head and tail lights all come from the lights layer.\n472\t\n473\t### 7.5 Rivals, bots and the shared driver brain (`22-drivers.js`)\n474\t\n475\t`OB.Brain(params)` returns the same intent a player gives (`steer`, `gas`, `brake`, `driftBtn`), so rivals, the attract\n476\tdemo, test bots and the Versus \"CPU\" option all drive through `CarSim`, with no special physics.\n477\t\n478\t- **Line and speed.**\n479\t  - **Racing line.** Look ahead 2,500 units. The target is `x* = −0.35 · sign(curve)` into the bend.\n480\t  - **Speed.** The curve-safe speed is `vsafe = vtop · sqrt(steerCapacity / (|curve| · curvePush))`. The brain brakes\n481\t    when it is over `vsafe` and the drift isn't allowed.\n482\t  - **Drift use.** If `drift` is allowed and `|curve| ≥ 4`, it taps the brake when entering and steers into the bend.\n483\t- **Traffic.** Pick the free lane with the most room ahead. Prefer slipstreaming a vehicle when the next 3,000 units\n484\t  are straight (draft-seeking rivals).\n485\t- **Rubber band** (rivals only). The rival's top speed is multiplied by `1 + rivalRubber·clamp(−Δz/16000, −0.08, 0.10)`,\n486\t  where Δz is the rival's lead over the player. `rivalSkill` scales its top speed and its cornering.\n487\t- **Request targeting** (bots and attract). Objects to hit (balloons, gnomes, bouquets, arches, ribbon gaps) become\n488\t  `x*` targets ahead. Objects to avoid (bees, cones, wrong slalom sides) are pushed against in `x*`. For the photo box,\n489\t  brake to 40 km/h when 1,500 units out.\n490\t- **Forks.** The brain follows a given route plan (`'L'`/`'R'` for each fork), or the passenger's preference.\n491\t- **Cheats** (attract mode and the dev menu): `noCollide`, `infiniteTime`, `perfectRequests` (objects come to the car),\n492\t  `autoDrift`.\n493\t- **Rival personas** are parameter sets: Kiki (drift-happy), the Brambles (draft-seeking), the Lustres (block-and-weave),\n494\t  Old Ottavio (late braker).\n495\t\n496\t### 7.6 Run rules: clock, checkpoints, forks, goals, score and the Bloom chain (`23-race.js`)\n497\t\n498\t- **Start line** (S1, and any fixed route's first stage):\n499\t  1. The cars sit on a painted grid under a light gantry. Bruno stands at the roadside waving his flag.\n500\t  2. A 3-2-1 countdown on the gantry, the passenger's line, then \"GO!\". This is the rev start of §7.3.\n501\t  3. The cutscene can be skipped with ✕/Enter. It plays at most once per session in Arcade mode.\n502\t- **The daylight clock** counts down from `startTime` (75 s on Normal). The HUD shows the seconds, and below 10 they\n503\t  flash red and a heartbeat tick plays.\n504\t  - **Checkpoints.** Each gate adds `checkpointBonus[row]`, which is 58/57/56/55 s for entering rows 2–5.\n505\t  - **Difficulty** scales the time: Easy ×1.12, Normal ×1, Hard ×0.92.\n506\t  - **At zero** the run is over. The car coasts to a stop, the screen warms to sunset orange, and you see the\n507\t    passenger's sigh line and \"TIME UP — The sun set over {stage}.\" Then `gameover` leads to `results`.\n508\t- **Stage card at a checkpoint**, about 2.5 s and never covering the road's centre:\n509\t  - \"TIME EXTENDED! +57\", the new stage's name with its 1–5 flower difficulty, and the stage time just driven.\n510\t  - Your best split for that stage, when you have one.\n511\t- **Fork approach.**\n512\t  - **900 segments before the decision point:** the HUD's mini-map pulses both next stages, and a \"← LAVENDER RIDGE |\n513\t    LEMON CLIFFS →\" banner appears with the difficulty flowers.\n514\t  - **In Love in Bloom**, about 600 segments before it, the passenger states a preference (§7.7).\n515\t- **Goal.**\n516\t  1. Passing the goal arch triggers `goal`: 1 s of slow motion (`game.timeScale = .4`), confetti, the crowd and the goal\n517\t     fanfare.\n518\t  2. Then `ending` plays (§8.3) and `results` follows.\n519\t  3. The run's time, score and route (as a string of L/R, such as `LRRL`) are saved.\n520\t- **Score, shown in every mode except Time Trial.**\n521\t  - **Per second:** `v(km/h) × 2 × bloomLevel`.\n522\t  - **Events:** a near miss 500, an overtake 100, a slingshot 300, and on `drift:end` the drift's score. Each is\n523\t    multiplied by `bloomLevel`.\n524\t  - **At the goal:** the remaining seconds × 1,000, plus a route bonus of Σ stage difficulty × 5,000.\n525\t  - The **arcade table** (`E.Arcade`, mode `'arcade'`, and `'love'` for Love in Bloom) takes initials on a letter wheel,\n526\t    which also accepts typing.\n527\t- **The Bloom chain** is the style multiplier, from level 1 up to level 5, *Full Bloom*.\n528\t  - **Earning chain points:** drift time × angle, near misses (+400), slingshots (+300), overtakes (+120), and Perfect\n529\t    requests (+800).\n530\t  - **Levels** at 1,000 / 3,000 / 6,000 / 10,000 chain points.\n531\t  - **Decay.** With no style event for `bloomDecay` (4 s), the points drain 20 % per second. A crash halves them and\n532\t    drops a level.\n533\t  - **Showing it.** Level 3 and up: the car sheds petals. At level 5 a \"FULL BLOOM!\" banner appears, roadside flower\n534\t    sprites pop open as the car passes (a sprite swap with a burst), and the radio song's `sparkle` layer fades in.\n535\t- **Results screen** (big tiles, no tables):\n536\t  - **Score, time and route:** the pyramid with your path lit, plus the goal's name.\n537\t  - **Your style:** the best drift, near misses and the time spent in slipstream.\n538\t  - **Love in Bloom only:** the petals and grade, with a stamp animation.\n539\t  - **Rewards:** the seeds earned, and records (*NEW RECORD!*).\n540\t  - **Buttons:** Drive again, Change car, Menu.\n541\t\n542\t### 7.7 The passenger and Love in Bloom (`24-requests.js`, `12-cast.js`, `31-voice.js`)\n543\t\n544\t- **The passenger is always in the car.** In every mode the passenger reacts to events with a bark (babble + subtitle\n545\t  bubble) and a pose. How often depends on the \"Passenger chatter\" setting: Full (every event with 35 % chance), Less\n546\t  (10 %, requests and goals only) or Off (requests only, silent). In Love in Bloom the requests are the core.\n547\t- **Request life cycle** (data in `OB.REQUESTS`; each stage lists 2–3 request slots `{ at: fraction, type, params }`):\n548\t  1. **Intro (1.6 s):** the passenger turns to you and says the line. A big card appears (\"POP THE BALLOONS!\") with the\n549\t     type's icon (not colour alone) and the target count. The objects spawn 1,500–2,500 units ahead.\n550\t  2. **Active (its window, 180–260 segments, about 12–17 s):** a progress counter (\"7 / 12\") or a gauge, a window bar,\n551\t     live pops and sparkles on each success.\n552\t  3. **Rating:** Perfect, Great, Good or Miss, giving **5 / 3 / 2 / 0 petals**. A request type the passenger loves earns\n553\t     ×1.2, rounded up. The passenger reacts in tier with a line and a pose.\n554\t- **Fork preference** (Love in Bloom). At a fork the passenger may say \"Left! I want to see the lavender!\". Taking it\n555\t  gives +2 petals, refusing it −1 (\"Hmph.\").\n556\t- **Petal penalties:** a spin-out −2, a bump −1 (with a 3 s cooldown), and more than 2 s off-road −1.\n557\t- **Grade.** The grade compares petals to the run's maximum (the requests actually met on that route, plus the\n558\t  fork preferences): **AAA ≥ 92 % \"Full Bloom\", AA ≥ 82 %, A ≥ 70 %, B ≥ 55 %, C ≥ 40 %, D ≥ 25 %, E below that**. The\n559\t  ending's mood follows from it: **Bloom** (A or better), **Bud** (B–C), **Wilt** (D–E).\n560\t- **Mood face.** The passenger's panel portrait shows a mood face (happy, neutral or pouty) from the last 20 s of\n561\t  events, and the petal ring fills around it.\n562\t- **The request types (all 14)** are specified in §8.7. Each implements `{ spawn(ctx), step(dt, ctx), evaluate() →\n563\t  rating, hud(ctx), lines }`, and each is tested with scripted pass and fail runs (§12).\n564\t\n565\t### 7.8 The Coast 2 Coast Tour: the career (`52-career.js`, `25-missions.js`)\n566\t\n567\t- **The Tour map.** A stylised island of Bellafiore (east coast on the right, west on the left), its regions painted per\n568\t  biome and the pyramid drawn as roads.\n569\t  - **Postcards.** 14 postcards are pinned near the stages they use, each showing its giver's portrait.\n570\t  - **Navigation.** The stick or d-pad moves the focus spatially between postcards, the mouse hover focuses one and a\n571\t    click opens it, and ✕/Enter opens it too. The map pans to keep the focus in view.\n572\t  - **Locked postcards** show a padlock and say what opens them.\n573\t- **The brief** is one big postcard: the giver's portrait (with its idle animation), the title, one or two short lines\n574\t  in the giver's voice, the rules as icons plus one sentence, the reward seeds per rank, your best rank, and \"✕ Start\n575\t  ○ Back\".\n576\t- **Rules.** A mission runs `race` with `rules: OB.Rules[kind](params)`. Its hooks are `onStart`, `onStep(dt)`,\n577\t  `onEvent(e)`, `hud(r)`, `isOver()` and `result()`, which returns `{ cleared, rank: 'C' | 'B' | 'A' | 'AAA', stats }`.\n578\t  Clearing is rank C or better.\n579\t- **The debrief.** A rank stamp (AAA is the \"FULL BLOOM\" stamp), the seeds counted up, any unlock toasts (\"A new bed is\n580\t  ready in the Garage Garden: Gardenia GT\"), then Retry, Next postcard, or Map.\n581\t- **Unlocking.** Clearing a mission opens the giver's next postcard. The **Finale** opens once any 9 missions are\n582\t  cleared. Clearing the Finale plays the Tour's closing scene and the credits.\n583\t- **Story** (lines in §8.11):\n584\t  - **Opening.** Bruno's garage at dawn. He welcomes you to \"the Coast 2 Coast Tour, the sunniest race on Earth\".\n585\t  - **Chapters.** Each giver's first postcard opens with a 3-line intro, and each giver's last mission closes their\n586\t    little arc.\n587\t  - **Finale.** All four characters gather at the goal you reach for a group photo (Juni's camera flash), then the\n588\t    credits roll.\n589\t\n590\t### 7.9 The Garage Garden and the economy (`26-progress.js`, `52-career.js`)\n591\t\n592\t- **The screen.** A sunny garden beside Bruno's garage with flower beds in rows of three.\n593\t  - **Each bed is one unlock:** its icon, name, cost in seeds and status (locked behind a mission, plantable, or\n594\t    bloomed).\n595\t  - **Planting** costs seeds. The bed animates (sprout, bud, bloom with a petal burst) and the item is unlocked.\n596\t  - **Navigation:** the d-pad or stick, mouse hover and click. The seed balance sits large at the top.\n597\t- **Earning seeds.**\n598\t  - **Missions:** the base reward × the rank multiplier (C ×1, B ×1.25, A ×1.5, AAA ×2). A mission pays out only the\n599\t    improvement over the best rank already paid for it.\n600\t  - **Arcade and Love in Bloom runs:** `floor(score / 20000)`, plus 50 for reaching a goal and 100 the first time you\n601\t    see each goal ending (with the tier, to make the 15 vignettes worth collecting).\n602\t- **Costs and balance** are in §8.9. The economy test (§12) proves everything can be bought with the seeds from all 14\n603\t  missions at rank A plus at most 6 arcade runs.\n604\t\n605\t### 7.10 Time Trial and ghosts\n606\t\n607\t- **Setup.** Pick any of the 15 stages, or a *Full Coast* run with the forks open. Traffic is Off by default and can be\n608\t  turned On. There is no passenger request layer, but the passenger still rides and reacts. The clock counts **up**, and\n609\t  checkpoints show splits against your best (green ahead, red behind, with a sign, never colour alone).\n610\t- **Ghost.**\n611\t  - **Recording.** Your best run is sampled at 10 Hz as `[stageIndex, z, x, yawFrame]`, delta-encoded and kept in the\n612\t    profile (at most 6,000 samples per run).\n613\t  - **Playback.** It is drawn as a translucent car (a 50 % ordered dither in the paint's tones) with a \"BEST\" tag.\n614\t- **Medals.** Bronze, Silver, Gold and Bloom per stage, from the reference bot time × 1.25 / 1.12 / 1.04 / 0.98. These\n615\t  are computed by the test suite at build time and stored in the data (`OB.STAGES[id].par`), never computed live.\n616\t\n617\t### 7.11 Versus: two players on one screen (`53-modes.js`)\n618\t\n619\t1. **Join.** The Versus screen calls `game.allowJoin({ max: 2 })`. P2 joins with Start on a second pad, or with 2 on the\n620\t   keyboard (which splits it: P1 WASD with F/G/H, P2 the arrows with `.` `/` `,`).\n621\t2. **Pick.** Each player picks a car and paint with their own focus, side by side. Then pick a route: Flower Sprint\n622\t   (S1 → S2 → S5), Cliff Sprint (S1 → S3 → S6) or Full Coast (S1 → S2 → S5 → S9 → S13 → Goal C). A lone player may set\n623\t   P2 to \"CPU\" (the `OB.Brain` at Normal skill).\n624\t3. **Race.** `race` runs with `players: 2` and `game.splitScreen(2, { layout: 'rows' })`, and the scene's `res` is\n625\t   `[640, 360]` so each half gets 640×180.\n626\t   - **World.** One shared world and traffic. The cars collide and slipstream each other.\n627\t   - **Screens.** Each viewport draws its own camera and its own HUD (clock, speed, place \"1st/2nd\", the gap).\n628\t     `scene.hud(r)` draws the centre divider and the countdown.\n629\t   - **Engine sounds.** Both players' engine sounds play, each at 70 %.\n630\t4. **Win.** The first to the goal wins. If the clock runs out, the player further along wins. Results show both players\n631\t   and offer Rematch, Change route or Menu.\n632\t\n633\t### 7.12 AI Hub online (`70-hub.js`)\n634\t\n635\tFollow `lib/hub/README.md` (protocol 1, client 1.4.0) exactly. The mode is **ghost**: each copy runs its own race, and\n636\tthe copies send each other only their own car.\n637\t\n638\t1. **Detect.** `AIHub.active` is true from the first line.\n639\t   - Skip the title, menus, attract mode and the touch notice.\n640\t   - Hide **Quit to title**, Restart and Retire (pass `menu.quit: false` at construction), and hide Dev Mode.\n641\t   - The garage and radio are skipped: the car is the Orchidea Spider in seat colour, the song is station 1, the\n642\t     transmission automatic, and the passenger Marisol (chatter Less).\n643\t2. **Connect.** `await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [1, 4], card: true })`. If it resolves to `null`, boot to\n644\t   the title as normal.\n645\t3. **Prepare.** Build the race for the fixed **Flower Sprint** route (S1 → S2 → S5, ending at a sprint goal arch). Take\n646\t   traffic from `AIHub.rng('traffic')`. Call `AIHub.ready()` as soon as the boot steps finish, without waiting for a key.\n647\t4. **Our own card (`hubcard` scene).**\n648\t   - **It shows** the title logo over the start line at dawn, the players lined up as cars on the grid in their seat\n649\t     colours with a READY tick, the goal line (\"Fastest to the Flower Sprint goal wins\"), the controls for the last-used\n650\t     device (glyphs), and the countdown from `card.left`.\n651\t   - **Answers.** Enter or ✕ (button 0) or a click on READY → `AIHub.answer('ready')`. △ (button 3), T, or a click on\n652\t     MORE TIME → `AIHub.answer('more')`.\n653\t   - **Limits.** The card draws from `AIHub.rng('card')` only. It has no settings or choices.\n654\t5. **Start.** On `start`, run the gantry countdown so that GO lands when `AIHub.time() ≥ 0`. The sim holds the cars\n655\t   still until then.\n656\t6. **During the race.**\n657\t   - **Clock.** In a session the clock counts **up** (race time). There is no time-out, and the hub's limit ends the\n658\t     round.\n659\t   - **Ghost send, every 100 ms:** `AIHub.send({ t: 'g', area: stageId, z, x, v, yaw, drift }, { reliable: false })`.\n660\t   - **Progress, once a second:** `AIHub.progress({ score: metres along the route, label: 'Stage 2/3 · 64%' })`. Start\n661\t     both the sends and the progress at `start`, even while the score is still 0: the harness only pokes random keys,\n662\t     yet checks that every copy sends and reports.\n663\t   - **Ghost cars.** Others are drawn as translucent cars in their seat colour with name tags (the pixel font; any\n664\t     glyph it lacks becomes `?`; never HTML), only while they share our `area`, smoothed toward their last state. A\n665\t     standings strip on the HUD shows everyone's progress.\n666\t7. **Goal.** `AIHub.result({ time: Math.round(AIHub.time()) })`, plus `send({ t: 'done', time })` and\n667\t   `stats: { 'Best drift': …, 'Near misses': … }`. Then keep cruising behind a \"Waiting for the others…\" banner while\n668\t   the ghosts keep coming.\n669\t8. **Endings.**\n670\t   - **`time-up`:** if the race is still going, `AIHub.result({ score: metres })` at once.\n671\t   - **`end`:** stop taking input, `stopMusic`, fade the engine loops and mute all audio, freeze the sim, and show\n672\t     \"Round over\" until the hub removes the page.\n673\t   - **`leave`:** drop that player's ghost.\n674\t9. **Hard rules in a session.** No network except through the hub. No `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, `window.open` or\n675\t   navigation. The pause menu offers Resume, Settings and Controls only. Options is ignored while Create is held (E4).\n676\t   The empty storage the client provides means a fresh profile, which is fine.\n677\t\n678\t### 7.13 Characters and animation (`12-cast.js`)\n679\t\n680\tAll characters are engine `E.Humanoid` rigs dressed per §8.5, animated by the rig's own system: poses, expressions,\n681\t`point`/`aim`, procedural breathing, and hair. Each one has a portrait from `rig.drawPortrait`.\n682\t\n683\t- **In the car.**\n684\t  - **Seating.** The driver (left seat) and passenger (right seat) are drawn between the car's *under* and *over*\n685\t    layers, so the bodywork hides their lower bodies. They are rendered seated (`down: .35`, legs hidden), sized to the\n686\t    car's scale, and placed on the seat anchors of the car frame shown.\n687\t  - **Hair and scarves** stream back with speed: the rig's hair plus a two-segment ribbon for a scarf or long hair,\n688\t    whose angle follows speed and steering.\n689\t- **Poses**, triggered by bus events:\n690\t\n691\t| Event | Driver | Passenger |\n692\t|---|---|---|\n693\t| steering | leans into the turn (`lean`), head tilts | sways the other way |\n694\t| request intro | — | turns to the driver, `expr: 'shout'`, points ahead (`point`) |\n695\t| success | — | `pose: 'cheer'`, `expr: 'smile'`, petals burst over the car |\n696\t| fail | — | arms crossed (`pose: 'hips'` while seated), `expr: 'angry'` |\n697\t| near miss / big air | — | covers her face (`pose: 'guard'`, `expr: 'wince'`) |\n698\t| spin-out | head shake | both hands up (`pose: 'cheer'` with `expr: 'shout'` reads as a scream), \"Kyaaa!\" bark |\n699\t| fork preference | — | points left or right (`point` + `aim`, the rig turned toward that side) |\n700\t| goal | one arm up | `pose: 'wave'` at the crowd |\n701\t| Juni's photo moments | — | raises her camera (a held sprite), with a flash particle |\n702\t\n703\t- **Bruno.** At the start line he waves his flag: a flag sprite on `rig.hand()`, the arm driven by an `E.MOVES`-style\n704\t  spec looping `overhead`, with the flag cloth a 3-segment ribbon. At goals and debriefs he cheers, gives a thumbs-up\n705\t  (`point` + `pose: 'hips'`) or facepalms (a hand at his head, `expr: 'wince'`).\n706\t- **Endings and garage** use side-view vignettes at size 1.6. The couple step out of the car (a walk cycle from the\n707\t  velocity) and play their scene's poses.\n708\t- **Spectators** are pre-rendered Humanoid sprites in 3 poses × 4 palettes, waving through two frames, shown at\n709\t  checkpoints, goals, beaches and the city.\n710\t- **Car animation.**\n711\t  - **Frames.** 33 yaw frames covering −90° to +90° (steering and drift) and 32 frames for a full spin.\n712\t  - **Body.** It bobs on its suspension (a spring on bumps, rumble strips and landings) and rolls 1 px against the\n713\t    wheels when cornering.\n714\t  - **Wheels and lights.** The tread alternates between two phases by speed. Brake and reverse lights, blinkers and\n715\t    headlights come from the lights layer.\n716\t  - **Effects.** Backfire flames on lift-off above 6,000 rpm, and tyre smoke, skid marks, sparks, sand and spray as\n717\t    particles.\n718\t- **Traffic animation:** lane-change blinkers, brake lights, a spin when hit, and a honk \"♪\" when you cut one off.\n719\t- **Request objects** (§8.7): balloons bob and burst into confetti; gnomes wobble, then fly off with googly eyes popping\n720\t  out; bees buzz in sine paths; ribbons flutter and snap; cones tumble; flower arches sway and burst into petals;\n721\t  bouquets spin and sparkle.\n722\t- **UI animation:** focus pops (a scale-and-bounce tween), wipes with blowing petals between screens, the logo shine\n723\t  sweeping across, and garden beds blooming.\n724\t\n725\t### 7.14 Music and the radio (`01-data-music.js`, `30-synth.js`, `32-radio.js`)\n726\t\n727\t- **Code only.** All music is engine song objects (`{ bpm, steps, tracks }`) on the extended synth (E6). There are no\n728\t  audio files.\n729\t- **The radio screen** is a car-radio faceplate.\n730\t  - **Stations.** Five presets, plus two more once unlocked (§8.10). Each station tile shows its frequency (\"88.6\n731\t    PETAL FM\") and the song title.\n732\t  - **Tuning.** Left/right tunes. A 0.3 s burst of static plays between stations, then the song starts from its intro.\n733\t  - **Choosing.** The mouse hovers and clicks a preset. ✕/Enter or the gas confirms.\n734\t  - **Transmission.** △ or T toggles AUTO/MANUAL, shown big.\n735\t- **During a race.** D-pad ↑ or R switches station, crossfading over 1 s with a \"♪ Coastline Crush · 91.2 SUN FM\" card.\n736\t  Music ducks to 60 % under the passenger's speech and to 35 % when paused (the engine does that).\n737\t- **Song form.** Every radio song is 64–96 bars, about 2–3 minutes, then loops.\n738\t  - **Shape:** intro (4 bars) → A → B → chorus → A′ → bridge → chorus′ → turnaround.\n739\t  - **Tracks:** at least 6 named tracks (`drums`, `perc`, `bass`, `keys` or `gtr`, `lead`, `pad`) and a `sparkle` layer\n740\t    (`on: false`) that Full Bloom brings in.\n741\t  - **Mix:** bass below 250 Hz, chords at 300–2,000 Hz, lead at 1–4 kHz, `sends` on keys and lead.\n742\t- **Instrument presets** (`OB.Synth.inst`), built from E6 fields:\n743\t\n744\t| Preset | Recipe |\n745\t|---|---|\n746\t| `epiano` | FM, ratio 1, index 1.8 decaying, plus a soft triangle |\n747\t| `slapbass` | saw and square, lowpass with fast envelope, accents |\n748\t| `fingerbass` | triangle and sine, short attack |\n749\t| `brass` | 3-voice saw unison, filter swell |\n750\t| `stab` | short saw chord |\n751\t| `steeldrum` | FM, ratio 3.5 |\n752\t| `marimba` | sine, fast decay |\n753\t| `lead` | pulse with vibrato after 0.15 s and slide |\n754\t| `sax` | saw with a bandpass formant and vibrato |\n755\t| `pad` | 4-voice detuned saw, slow attack, lowpass |\n756\t| `organ` | sines at 1×/2×/3× |\n757\t| `pluck` | noise burst into a resonant lowpass (a Karplus-like pluck) |\n758\t| `chorusgtr` | 2 detuned square chords, panned |\n759\t\n760\t- **Composition rules**, because builders can't listen:\n761\t  - Diatonic melodies on the stated key's scale, and chord tones on strong beats.\n762\t  - The bass plays chord roots or fifths on beat 1.\n763\t  - No two melodic tracks in the same octave at once.\n764\t  - The lead rests at least 25 % of the steps.\n765\t  - Drums always run under everything.\n766\t  - The progressions, keys, tempos and hooks are listed per song in §8.10. The audio-lint test (§12) checks levels and\n767\t    clipping, and the spectrum test checks the mix's bands.\n768\t\n769\t### 7.15 Sound effects, the engine note and ambience\n770\t\n771\t- **Engine** (`OB.EngineSound`, on E7 loops), one per player car:\n772\t  - **Sources.** Two saw oscillators at the firing frequency `f = rpm/60 × cylinders/2` and at 2f, plus a square sub at\n773\t    f/2, through a lowpass whose cutoff follows throttle (600–4,000 Hz) and a soft-clip shaper.\n774\t  - **Character.** Each car has its own cylinder count (4/6/8/12) and filter colour (§8.4).\n775\t  - **Gear changes** dip the pitch with a short \"chuff\". Lift-off above 6,000 rpm adds backfire crackles (noise bursts).\n776\t  - **Rivals** get one shared quieter loop for the nearest rival.\n777\t  - **Traffic** gets a passing \"whoosh\": noise rising with closing speed and panned by side.\n778\t- **Tyres.** A squeal from 2–3 triangle oscillators near 900–1,400 Hz with a fast random wobble; its gain follows the\n779\t  drift angle and hard cornering. Rumble strips give a 30 Hz pulse train. Gravel and sand give lowpass noise crunch.\n780\t- **Wind** is bandpass noise rising with v². In the slipstream it hushes (lowpass), then pops back on the slingshot\n781\t  whoosh.\n782\t- **Crashes** are soft and funny, never harsh: a bump \"thunk\" (sine drop with noise), a spin (squeal sweep plus the\n783\t  passenger's \"Kyaaa!\"), and a scrape (filtered noise with sparkle ticks).\n784\t- **Request sounds:**\n785\t\n786\t| Event | Sound |\n787\t|---|---|\n788\t| balloon pop | noise click and a rising chirp |\n789\t| gnome bonk | \"boink\": triangle 300→900 Hz with a squeak |\n790\t| bee buzz | a loop, saw at 220 Hz with 30 Hz amplitude wobble, panned by position |\n791\t| ribbon snap | noise and a pitch drop |\n792\t| cone | a hollow \"tok\" |\n793\t| arch | harp gliss (pluck arpeggio) |\n794\t| bouquet | chime |\n795\t| photo | shutter click and flash zap |\n796\t| request success | rising chimes |\n797\t| request fail | deflating trombone (saw slide down with vibrato) |\n798\t\n799\t- **Race events:**\n800\t\n801\t| Event | Sound |\n802\t|---|---|\n803\t| countdown | beeps, then GO |\n804\t| checkpoint | whoosh and a bright chord |\n805\t| time warning | tick |\n806\t| near miss | a short \"zing\" |\n807\t| Full Bloom | sparkle |\n808\t| goal fanfare | — |\n809\t| crowd cheer | layered noise bursts and a few babble \"yay\"s |\n810\t| seagulls (beach) | — |\n811\t| cicadas (vineyard and canyon) | — |\n812\t| birds (fields) | — |\n813\t| waves | lowpass noise swells |\n814\t| city night hum | — |\n815\t| fireworks | thump, then crackle |\n816\t| horn | per car: a two-tone square pair |\n817\t\n818\t- **Ambience** (`OB.Ambience`) is one quiet loop set per kit. It crossfades at junctions and sits under the music.\n819\t  Tunnels raise `audio.reverb` from 0.15 to 0.6 and add an echo-y wash to the engine.\n820\t- **UI sounds:** focus tick, confirm, back, error, toggle, slider tick, and planting a seed (a soft pop and a bloom\n821\t  sparkle).\n822\t\n823\t### 7.16 Voices: Animalese and Simlish-style babble (`31-voice.js`)\n824\t\n825\t- **Text to syllables.** Lower-case the text and split it into syllables: a consonant onset plus a vowel nucleus.\n826\t  Punctuation becomes a pause (comma 80 ms, full stop 160 ms, \"…\" 300 ms). A run of capitals is shouted (+3 dB, +2\n827\t  semitones).\n828\t- **Synthesis per syllable** (60–110 ms, on the voice bus):\n829\t  - **Source:** a saw and a pulse at `f0`, through two parallel bandpass formants. The vowel sets F1/F2: a 800/1200,\n830\t    e 500/1900, i 300/2300, o 500/900, u 350/800 (Hz). Each character scales them by its own factor.\n831\t  - **Onsets:** plosives (p b t d k g) get an 8–15 ms noise burst at 2–4 kHz. Fricatives (s z f v h, sh) get 30–50 ms\n832\t    of filtered noise (high for s, low for f and h). Nasals and liquids glide `f0`.\n833\t  - **Pitch:** `f0` follows an intonation contour. Statements fall at the end, questions rise, and \"!\" lifts the start.\n834\t    A per-syllable jitter comes from an rng seeded by the text, so a line always sounds the same.\n835\t- **Moods:**\n836\t\n837\t| Mood | Pitch | Speed | Other |\n838\t|---|---|---|---|\n839\t| happy | +3 semitones | — | wider contour |\n840\t| excited | +5 | +20 % | — |\n841\t| angry | −2 | — | louder, clipped decays |\n842\t| sad | −4 | −20 % | breathy noise mix |\n843\t| scared | +6 | — | tremolo |\n844\t\n845\t- **Per-character voices** (data in `OB.CAST`):\n846\t\n847\t| Character | Base f0 | Formant × | Rate (syllables/s) | Wave | Quirk |\n848\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n849\t| Marisol | 290 Hz | 1.12 | 13 | pulse 25 % | giggle \"hee\" barks |\n850\t| Vivi | 230 Hz | 1.0 | 11 | saw | long \"Daaarling\" vowels, dramatic swoops |\n851\t| Juni | 320 Hz | 1.18 | 15 | pulse 12 % | raspy noise mix |\n852\t| Bruno | 110 Hz | 0.8 | 10 | saw | booming, with a laugh bark \"Ho ho!\" |\n853\t| Kiki | 270 Hz | 1.1 | 14 | — | — |\n854\t| Lance | 140 Hz | — | — | — | — |\n855\t| Lacey | 250 Hz | — | — | — | — |\n856\t| Bram/Basil | 125 and 135 Hz | — | — | — | — |\n857\t| Ottavio | 100 Hz | — | slow, wavering | — | — |\n858\t| Announcer | 150 Hz | — | — | — | big reverb send, used for \"CHECKPOINT!\" and \"GOAL!\" |\n859\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f95c51ac-ca98-4f0d-bbff-431b6595447e","uuid":"b95bade8-86ad-487b-9c4f-b36500797fb7","timestamp":"2026-11-04T11:32:08.541Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-043153__xe1b7e1/work/PLAN.md","content":"  *both* next stages, each offset by its branch's lateral end position.\n- **Checkpoint gate.** It stands at the start of the next stage (segment 2): an arch of flowers with a \"CHECKPOINT\"\n  banner. Passing it extends the clock and shows the stage card.\n- **Route graph.** The pyramid is rows 1–5, with stage *(r, c)* forking to *(r+1, c)* on the left and *(r+1, c+1)* on\n  the right. That makes **16 routes**, and every row-5 stage ends at its own goal (§8.2). Left branches are always the\n  gentler road: fewer hard bends, less traffic, wider lanes.\n- **Fixed routes.** Hub, Versus and missions use fixed stage lists. A fork on a fixed route has the other branch closed\n  by a striped barrier and a \"ROAD CLOSED\" sign, drawn and solid. A fixed route may end at a **sprint goal arch** placed\n  at the end of any stage.\n- **Scenery from kit rules.** Each biome kit lists *placement rules*: `{ sprite, side: 'L' | 'R' | 'both', offset:\n  [min, max] (half roads beyond the edge), every: segments, jitter, chance, cluster }` plus *runs* (dense rows such as a\n  palm avenue or a fence line). Placement uses the stage's own seed, so the same stage always looks the same. Landmarks\n  are stage data (`{ at: fraction, side, kind, offset }`). Nothing is placed within 0.15 half-roads of the asphalt\n  unless it is a rail or a strip.\n- **Physics queries.** `world.info(stage, z, x)` returns `{ curve, slopeY, curvY (for crests), w, lanes, onRoad,\n  ground, edgeL, edgeR, tunnel, hits (scenery touching, from road.hits), cushion (in the gore), gust }` for the sim.\n- **Mirror Roads.** An unlockable that builds every stage with its curves negated and its sides swapped. It is a\n  build-time flag, and the same code paths run.\n\n### 7.2 Rendering a race frame (`51-race-scenes.js`, `13-fx.js`, `41-hud.js`)\n\nEach viewport is drawn in this order, all in screen pixels; on a split screen it repeats per `r.viewport`:\n\n1. **Sky.** `OB.Sky` for the stage and time of day: gradient, sun or moon, clouds, far layers (sea, hills, mountains,\n   mesas, skyline, aurora, balloons, lanterns). It scrolls with `turn` (the curve times speed added up). Near the end of\n   a junction it dissolves with an ordered dither over 1.5 s into the chosen stage's sky.\n2. **Road.** `stage.road.draw(r, { z, x, h, turn, cars, branches })`. That draws, far to near: bands, grounds, strips\n   and spans, with scenery, traffic, rivals, ghosts and request objects interleaved by segment. Fog colour and density\n   come from the kit and the time of day.\n3. **Asphalt marks** through `onBand`: skid marks (a ring buffer of 400 per stage), the photo box, headlight pools at\n   night (additive stepped alpha) and street-lamp pools.\n4. **The player's car** at `road.project(z + ahead, x)`: shadow, wheels, the under layer, occupants, the over layer, and\n   the lights layer when braking or at night. Then the particles near the car: tyre smoke, sparks, dust or sand,\n   spray, backfire.\n5. **Screen effects.** Petals and rain, speed lines (above 260 km/h), heat shimmer in the desert and canyon, then the\n   **pixel bloom** (below), then the **lens flare** when the sun is on screen and not in a tunnel.\n6. **HUD** (§7.18), then dialogue bubbles, banners and toasts, then the debug overlay when it is on.\n\n**Pixel bloom** (`OB.FX.post`), which must look like pixel art and never like blur:\n\n1. Read the viewport's `ImageData`, then sum pixels brighter than a threshold (luma 200 by default, from the kit) into\n   a ¼-resolution grid.\n2. Blur that grid with two 3×3 box passes and quantise it to 4 levels.\n3. Add it back at 4× (nearest neighbour), through a 4×4 Bayer dither and tinted toward the sun's colour.\n4. Strength by time of day: dawn 0.55, noon 0.35, sunset 0.7, night 0.6 (neon). Reduce motion keeps it, as it is\n   static.\n\nLens flare is 5 ghosts (hexagons and discs drawn with `px.blend('add')` at stepped alpha) along the line from the sun\nthrough the screen's centre. Its strength scales with how centred the sun is.\n\n**Cameras.** The camera is a setting, and the △/C key cycles it in a race.\n\n| Camera | Height `h` | Car drawn | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Chase** (default) | 1000 | full size | — |\n| **High** | 1350 | 0.8× | — |\n| **Bumper** | 380 | not drawn | a bonnet strip and the cockpit edge; the occupants' heads aren't shown |\n\n- **Following.** Camera x is `0.85·x + 0.15·x_lagged`, where `x_lagged` follows `x` at 6/s. In a drift the lag rises\n  so the car swings out on screen.\n- **Speed.** The field of view widens from 100° to 108° at top speed.\n- **Shake.** Rumble strips and off-road driving shake the camera through `game.shake`, which respects the comfort\n  settings.\n\n**Night and tunnels.**\n- **Tunnels.** The `tint` darkens bands and sprites (multiply). Sodium lamps along the walls flash past as stripes.\n- **Night.** The player's headlights cast two additive cones on the asphalt ahead, and traffic shows red tail lights\n  and white headlights. Neon is emissive, so it is drawn after the tint.\n\n### 7.3 Car handling (`20-car.js`)\n\nThe state is `{ z, x, vx, v (km/h), gear, rpm, drift: { on, dir, a, t, score }, slip, slingT, air, airT, crash: { t, kind },\nbumpT, scrape, offroad }`. The intent is `{ steer −1..1 (analog), gas 0..1, brake 0..1, driftBtn, shiftUp, shiftDown }`.\nEvery number below is a live tuning value (Appendix A), and per-car stats scale them (§8.4).\n\n1. **Engine.**\n   - **Acceleration.** `dv = A0·(1 − (v/vtop)^1.6)·gas − drag − brake·240 − offroad`. Here `A0` is taken from the car's\n     0–100 km/h time, and `drag = 18 + 0.00025·v²` km/h/s.\n   - **Automatic** (the default) shifts at 18/34/50/66/82 % of `vtop`. `rpm` sweeps 1,500–8,000 inside each gear.\n   - **Manual.** R1/E shifts up, L1/Q shifts down. There is a rev limiter at the top of each gear and weak pull below\n     2,500 rpm. A down-then-up shift within 0.3 s, while steering above 120 km/h, starts a drift (classic input).\n   - **Rev start.** Holding gas during the countdown moves `rpm`. If the needle is in the green zone (6,000–7,000) at\n     GO, you get **PERFECT START** (+25 km/h). Above 7,500 the wheels spin and you get nothing.\n2. **Steering.**\n   - **Target.** The lateral-speed target is `vx* = steer · steerRate · min(1, v/70) · (1 − 0.22·v/vtop)`, in half-roads\n     per second. `vx` approaches it at `steerResponse` (9/s).\n   - **Keyboard.** Steering ramps from 0 to 1 over 0.12 s and returns in 0.08 s. The analog stick is used as read,\n     after the dead zone.\n   - **Steering assist** (setting: Off/Low/High). Off the road it nudges `vx` back toward the lane at 0/0.3/0.6 per\n     second. On High it also brakes by itself before bends the car can't make.\n3. **Curve push.** `vx −= curve · (v/vtop)² · curvePush · dt` (curvePush is 0.62 by default). Design targets, which the\n   tests check:\n   - A curve of 3 at full speed can be held without drifting.\n   - A curve of 4 needs a lift or a drift above about 92 % of top speed.\n   - Curves of 5–6 need a drift or braking. A clean drift takes a 6 at about 85 % of top speed.\n4. **Drift.**\n   - **Entry.** Any enabled input starts it while `v > driftMinSpeed` (120) and `|steer| > 0.4`:\n     - **Brake tap:** brake above 0.3 for at most `driftTapWindow` (0.35 s), then released.\n     - **Down-up shift.**\n     - **The Drift button** (○/Space). Held, it keeps the drift deeper, like a handbrake.\n     The drift's direction is `sign(steer)`, and the \"Drift input\" setting picks which inputs are on (default: all).\n   - **Angle.** `a` approaches a target at 3.5 rad/s. The target is `dir · (0.35 + 0.40·|steer|)` when steering into the\n     drift, `dir · 0.30` with the stick centred, and `dir · 0.12` when counter-steering past 0.5. It is capped at\n     `driftAngleMax` (0.75 rad).\n   - **Effects.**\n     - The curve push is multiplied by `1 − driftGrip · min(1, |a|/0.5)` (driftGrip is 0.85).\n     - The steering rate is multiplied by `driftSteerBoost` (1.3).\n     - Speed drops by `driftSpeedLoss · |a|/0.75` km/h/s (16).\n   - **Exit.** It ends when `|a| < 0.08` for 0.2 s, when `v < 90`, after a crash, or when the curve is below 0.5 with the\n     stick centred for 0.4 s.\n   - **Drift score.** It accumulates `v · |a| · dt · 10`. At the exit it emits `drift:end { score, length }`.\n   - **Visuals.** The yaw frame is `vx·0.16 + a`. Smoke comes from both rear wheels, skid marks are laid, and the tyre\n     squeal rises with `|a|`.\n5. **Slipstream.**\n   - **Building.** The nearest vehicle ahead with `300 < dz < slipRange` (1,800) and `|dx| < slipWidth` (0.22) builds\n     `slip` at 1.25/s. Otherwise `slip` decays at 2.5/s.\n   - **Effects.** Top speed rises by `slipTopBonus·slip` (+6 %) and drag drops by up to 50 %. Wind lines show at\n     `slip > 0.5`.\n   - **Slingshot.** Pulling out with `slip ≥ 0.8` gives `slingshotBoost` (+14 km/h, eased over 0.8 s) and emits\n     `slingshot`.\n6. **Off-road and edges.**\n   - **Off-road** (`|x| > 1` on grass, sand, rows or snow): top speed falls to `offroadTop` (50 %), with an extra 120\n     km/h/s of drag while above it, plus a bounce and dust or sand.\n   - **Water** behind a rail can't be reached. Water with no rail (the causeway shallows): a big splash, then the car is\n     put back like a crash.\n   - **Rails, walls and cliffs.** `x` is held at the strip (±1.22). The car scrapes: −40 km/h/s, sparks, a screech, a\n     rumble, no spin.\n7. **Collisions.**\n   - **With traffic, rivals or the other player** (`dz` under one car length, about 520 units, and `|dx|` under 90 % of\n     the summed half-widths):\n     - **Bump**, when the closing speed is below `spinThreshold` (70 km/h): `v = v_other·0.92 − 8`, the cars are pushed\n       0.08 apart, and `car:bump` is emitted.\n     - **Spin-out** otherwise: 1.3 s of a full 360° spin, `v` drops to 30 % (at least 40 km/h), the other car spins and\n       slides aside, and `car:crash` is emitted.\n     - A side contact (similar `z`) pushes the cars apart in `x` and costs 8 % of speed.\n   - **Scenery** (`hits` beyond the edge): a spin-out above 110 km/h, below that a bump back to the asphalt at half\n     speed.\n   - **Gore cushions:** a bump, pushing the car toward the nearer branch.\n   - **After a spin-out**, the car is put back at `x = ±0.75` facing ahead and blinks for 1 s. It can't crash again\n     during that second.\n8. **Crests and air.** When the road's vertical curvature at `z + ahead` falls below −k and `v > 200`, the car is\n   airborne for about 0.3–0.6 s.\n   - **In the air:** the sprite lifts, the shadow stays down, steering is cut to 30 % and there is no curve push.\n   - **Landing:** the suspension bounces, sparks fly on a hard landing, and `air:land` is emitted. The passenger reacts.\n9. **Gusts** (Seafoam Causeway). Stage events push `vx` with a lateral impulse over 1.2 s, warned by a wind-sock icon\n   and spray 1 s ahead.\n\n### 7.4 Traffic (`21-traffic.js`)\n\n- **Spawning.** Vehicles spawn per stage from its traffic mix (§8.6), with a seeded stream per stage visit\n  (`runSeed ⊕ stageIndex`, or `AIHub.rng('traffic')` in a hub session). That gives every hub player the same traffic.\n  They spawn from `z + 600` to the end of the draw distance and despawn 3,000 units behind the slowest player.\n  - **Density.** The stage's density (vehicles per 10,000 units per lane) × the `trafficDensity` tuning × the\n    difficulty (Easy 0.75, Normal 1, Hard 1.3).\n  - **Spacing.** At least 900 units apart in one lane. A gap of two free lanes is always left somewhere within any\n    1,500 units, so the road is never walled off.\n- **Behaviour.** Each type has a speed range, and vehicles keep their lane.\n  - **Lane changes.** Blocked by a slower car within 1,200 units, a vehicle blinks for 0.8 s, then changes lane over\n    1.2 s if the lane is free.\n  - **Horn.** The horn (△/H) makes the vehicle ahead within 2,500 units in your lane change lanes if it can. It has a\n    2 s cooldown, and the passenger may comment.\n- **Junctions.** Each vehicle on a junction is assigned a branch from its stream, so they split naturally.\n- **Detection for scoring and requests.**\n  - **Overtake:** your `z` passes theirs while `|dx| < 0.9` and there was no contact in the last 0.5 s. Emits\n    `overtake`.\n  - **Near miss:** passing with an edge-to-edge gap `0 < |dx| − (w1 + w2) < 0.12` half-roads, a closing speed above\n    50 km/h and no contact. Emits `nearmiss`.\n  - **Slipstream source:** the vehicle ahead, per §7.3.\n- **Ribbon pairs.** A Love in Bloom spawn is two vehicles side by side in adjacent lanes at the same speed, with a pink\n  ribbon between them. Driving through the gap (`|x − mid| < 0.16`) as you pass snaps it.\n- **Drawing.** The yaw frame is chosen from the vehicle's projected screen offset (perspective), plus its lane-change\n  angle. Blinkers, brake lights (when slowing) and, at night, head and tail lights all come from the lights layer.\n\n### 7.5 Rivals, bots and the shared driver brain (`22-drivers.js`)\n\n`OB.Brain(params)` returns the same intent a player gives (`steer`, `gas`, `brake`, `driftBtn`), so rivals, the attract\ndemo, test bots and the Versus \"CPU\" option all drive through `CarSim`, with no special physics.\n\n- **Line and speed.**\n  - **Racing line.** Look ahead 2,500 units. The target is `x* = −0.35 · sign(curve)` into the bend.\n  - **Speed.** The curve-safe speed is `vsafe = vtop · sqrt(steerCapacity / (|curve| · curvePush))`. The brain brakes\n    when it is over `vsafe` and the drift isn't allowed.\n  - **Drift use.** If `drift` is allowed and `|curve| ≥ 4`, it taps the brake when entering and steers into the bend.\n- **Traffic.** Pick the free lane with the most room ahead. Prefer slipstreaming a vehicle when the next 3,000 units\n  are straight (draft-seeking rivals).\n- **Rubber band** (rivals only). The rival's top speed is multiplied by `1 + rivalRubber·clamp(−Δz/16000, −0.08, 0.10)`,\n  where Δz is the rival's lead over the player. `rivalSkill` scales its top speed and its cornering.\n- **Request targeting** (bots and attract). Objects to hit (balloons, gnomes, bouquets, arches, ribbon gaps) become\n  `x*` targets ahead. Objects to avoid (bees, cones, wrong slalom sides) are pushed against in `x*`. For the photo box,\n  brake to 40 km/h when 1,500 units out.\n- **Forks.** The brain follows a given route plan (`'L'`/`'R'` for each fork), or the passenger's preference.\n- **Cheats** (attract mode and the dev menu): `noCollide`, `infiniteTime`, `perfectRequests` (objects come to the car),\n  `autoDrift`.\n- **Rival personas** are parameter sets: Kiki (drift-happy), the Brambles (draft-seeking), the Lustres (block-and-weave),\n  Old Ottavio (late braker).\n\n### 7.6 Run rules: clock, checkpoints, forks, goals, score and the Bloom chain (`23-race.js`)\n\n- **Start line** (S1, and any fixed route's first stage):\n  1. The cars sit on a painted grid under a light gantry. Bruno stands at the roadside waving his flag.\n  2. A 3-2-1 countdown on the gantry, the passenger's line, then \"GO!\". This is the rev start of §7.3.\n  3. The cutscene can be skipped with ✕/Enter. It plays at most once per session in Arcade mode.\n- **The daylight clock** counts down from `startTime` (75 s on Normal). The HUD shows the seconds, and below 10 they\n  flash red and a heartbeat tick plays.\n  - **Checkpoints.** Each gate adds `checkpointBonus[row]`, which is 58/57/56/55 s for entering rows 2–5.\n  - **Difficulty** scales the time: Easy ×1.12, Normal ×1, Hard ×0.92.\n  - **At zero** the run is over. The car coasts to a stop, the screen warms to sunset orange, and you see the\n    passenger's sigh line and \"TIME UP — The sun set over {stage}.\" Then `gameover` leads to `results`.\n- **Stage card at a checkpoint**, about 2.5 s and never covering the road's centre:\n  - \"TIME EXTENDED! +57\", the new stage's name with its 1–5 flower difficulty, and the stage time just driven.\n  - Your best split for that stage, when you have one.\n- **Fork approach.**\n  - **900 segments before the decision point:** the HUD's mini-map pulses both next stages, and a \"← LAVENDER RIDGE |\n    LEMON CLIFFS →\" banner appears with the difficulty flowers.\n  - **In Love in Bloom**, about 600 segments before it, the passenger states a preference (§7.7).\n- **Goal.**\n  1. Passing the goal arch triggers `goal`: 1 s of slow motion (`game.timeScale = .4`), confetti, the crowd and the goal\n     fanfare.\n  2. Then `ending` plays (§8.3) and `results` follows.\n  3. The run's time, score and route (as a string of L/R, such as `LRRL`) are saved.\n- **Score, shown in every mode except Time Trial.**\n  - **Per second:** `v(km/h) × 2 × bloomLevel`.\n  - **Events:** a near miss 500, an overtake 100, a slingshot 300, and on `drift:end` the drift's score. Each is\n    multiplied by `bloomLevel`.\n  - **At the goal:** the remaining seconds × 1,000, plus a route bonus of Σ stage difficulty × 5,000.\n  - The **arcade table** (`E.Arcade`, mode `'arcade'`, and `'love'` for Love in Bloom) takes initials on a letter wheel,\n    which also accepts typing.\n- **The Bloom chain** is the style multiplier, from level 1 up to level 5, *Full Bloom*.\n  - **Earning chain points:** drift time × angle, near misses (+400), slingshots (+300), overtakes (+120), and Perfect\n    requests (+800).\n  - **Levels** at 1,000 / 3,000 / 6,000 / 10,000 chain points.\n  - **Decay.** With no style event for `bloomDecay` (4 s), the points drain 20 % per second. A crash halves them and\n    drops a level.\n  - **Showing it.** Level 3 and up: the car sheds petals. At level 5 a \"FULL BLOOM!\" banner appears, roadside flower\n    sprites pop open as the car passes (a sprite swap with a burst), and the radio song's `sparkle` layer fades in.\n- **Results screen** (big tiles, no tables):\n  - **Score, time and route:** the pyramid with your path lit, plus the goal's name.\n  - **Your style:** the best drift, near misses and the time spent in slipstream.\n  - **Love in Bloom only:** the petals and grade, with a stamp animation.\n  - **Rewards:** the seeds earned, and records (*NEW RECORD!*).\n  - **Buttons:** Drive again, Change car, Menu.\n\n### 7.7 The passenger and Love in Bloom (`24-requests.js`, `12-cast.js`, `31-voice.js`)\n\n- **The passenger is always in the car.** In every mode the passenger reacts to events with a bark (babble + subtitle\n  bubble) and a pose. How often depends on the \"Passenger chatter\" setting: Full (every event with 35 % chance), Less\n  (10 %, requests and goals only) or Off (requests only, silent). In Love in Bloom the requests are the core.\n- **Request life cycle** (data in `OB.REQUESTS`; each stage lists 2–3 request slots `{ at: fraction, type, params }`):\n  1. **Intro (1.6 s):** the passenger turns to you and says the line. A big card appears (\"POP THE BALLOONS!\") with the\n     type's icon (not colour alone) and the target count. The objects spawn 1,500–2,500 units ahead.\n  2. **Active (its window, 180–260 segments, about 12–17 s):** a progress counter (\"7 / 12\") or a gauge, a window bar,\n     live pops and sparkles on each success.\n  3. **Rating:** Perfect, Great, Good or Miss, giving **5 / 3 / 2 / 0 petals**. A request type the passenger loves earns\n     ×1.2, rounded up. The passenger reacts in tier with a line and a pose.\n- **Fork preference** (Love in Bloom). At a fork the passenger may say \"Left! I want to see the lavender!\". Taking it\n  gives +2 petals, refusing it −1 (\"Hmph.\").\n- **Petal penalties:** a spin-out −2, a bump −1 (with a 3 s cooldown), and more than 2 s off-road −1.\n- **Grade.** The grade compares petals to the run's maximum (the requests actually met on that route, plus the\n  fork preferences): **AAA ≥ 92 % \"Full Bloom\", AA ≥ 82 %, A ≥ 70 %, B ≥ 55 %, C ≥ 40 %, D ≥ 25 %, E below that**. The\n  ending's mood follows from it: **Bloom** (A or better), **Bud** (B–C), **Wilt** (D–E).\n- **Mood face.** The passenger's panel portrait shows a mood face (happy, neutral or pouty) from the last 20 s of\n  events, and the petal ring fills around it.\n- **The request types (all 14)** are specified in §8.7. Each implements `{ spawn(ctx), step(dt, ctx), evaluate() →\n  rating, hud(ctx), lines }`, and each is tested with scripted pass and fail runs (§12).\n\n### 7.8 The Coast 2 Coast Tour: the career (`52-career.js`, `25-missions.js`)\n\n- **The Tour map.** A stylised island of Bellafiore (east coast on the right, west on the left), its regions painted per\n  biome and the pyramid drawn as roads.\n  - **Postcards.** 14 postcards are pinned near the stages they use, each showing its giver's portrait.\n  - **Navigation.** The stick or d-pad moves the focus spatially between postcards, the mouse hover focuses one and a\n    click opens it, and ✕/Enter opens it too. The map pans to keep the focus in view.\n  - **Locked postcards** show a padlock and say what opens them.\n- **The brief** is one big postcard: the giver's portrait (with its idle animation), the title, one or two short lines\n  in the giver's voice, the rules as icons plus one sentence, the reward seeds per rank, your best rank, and \"✕ Start\n  ○ Back\".\n- **Rules.** A mission runs `race` with `rules: OB.Rules[kind](params)`. Its hooks are `onStart`, `onStep(dt)`,\n  `onEvent(e)`, `hud(r)`, `isOver()` and `result()`, which returns `{ cleared, rank: 'C' | 'B' | 'A' | 'AAA', stats }`.\n  Clearing is rank C or better.\n- **The debrief.** A rank stamp (AAA is the \"FULL BLOOM\" stamp), the seeds counted up, any unlock toasts (\"A new bed is\n  ready in the Garage Garden: Gardenia GT\"), then Retry, Next postcard, or Map.\n- **Unlocking.** Clearing a mission opens the giver's next postcard. The **Finale** opens once any 9 missions are\n  cleared. Clearing the Finale plays the Tour's closing scene and the credits.\n- **Story** (lines in §8.11):\n  - **Opening.** Bruno's garage at dawn. He welcomes you to \"the Coast 2 Coast Tour, the sunniest race on Earth\".\n  - **Chapters.** Each giver's first postcard opens with a 3-line intro, and each giver's last mission closes their\n    little arc.\n  - **Finale.** All four characters gather at the goal you reach for a group photo (Juni's camera flash), then the\n    credits roll.\n\n### 7.9 The Garage Garden and the economy (`26-progress.js`, `52-career.js`)\n\n- **The screen.** A sunny garden beside Bruno's garage with flower beds in rows of three.\n  - **Each bed is one unlock:** its icon, name, cost in seeds and status (locked behind a mission, plantable, or\n    bloomed).\n  - **Planting** costs seeds. The bed animates (sprout, bud, bloom with a petal burst) and the item is unlocked.\n  - **Navigation:** the d-pad or stick, mouse hover and click. The seed balance sits large at the top.\n- **Earning seeds.**\n  - **Missions:** the base reward × the rank multiplier (C ×1, B ×1.25, A ×1.5, AAA ×2). A mission pays out only the\n    improvement over the best rank already paid for it.\n  - **Arcade and Love in Bloom runs:** `floor(score / 20000)`, plus 50 for reaching a goal and 100 the first time you\n    see each goal ending (with the tier, to make the 15 vignettes worth collecting).\n- **Costs and balance** are in §8.9. The economy test (§12) proves everything can be bought with the seeds from all 14\n  missions at rank A plus at most 6 arcade runs.\n\n### 7.10 Time Trial and ghosts\n\n- **Setup.** Pick any of the 15 stages, or a *Full Coast* run with the forks open. Traffic is Off by default and can be\n  turned On. There is no passenger request layer, but the passenger still rides and reacts. The clock counts **up**, and\n  checkpoints show splits against your best (green ahead, red behind, with a sign, never colour alone).\n- **Ghost.**\n  - **Recording.** Your best run is sampled at 10 Hz as `[stageIndex, z, x, yawFrame]`, delta-encoded and kept in the\n    profile (at most 6,000 samples per run).\n  - **Playback.** It is drawn as a translucent car (a 50 % ordered dither in the paint's tones) with a \"BEST\" tag.\n- **Medals.** Bronze, Silver, Gold and Bloom per stage, from the reference bot time × 1.25 / 1.12 / 1.04 / 0.98. These\n  are computed by the test suite at build time and stored in the data (`OB.STAGES[id].par`), never computed live.\n\n### 7.11 Versus: two players on one screen (`53-modes.js`)\n\n1. **Join.** The Versus screen calls `game.allowJoin({ max: 2 })`. P2 joins with Start on a second pad, or with 2 on the\n   keyboard (which splits it: P1 WASD with F/G/H, P2 the arrows with `.` `/` `,`).\n2. **Pick.** Each player picks a car and paint with their own focus, side by side. Then pick a route: Flower Sprint\n   (S1 → S2 → S5), Cliff Sprint (S1 → S3 → S6) or Full Coast (S1 → S2 → S5 → S9 → S13 → Goal C). A lone player may set\n   P2 to \"CPU\" (the `OB.Brain` at Normal skill).\n3. **Race.** `race` runs with `players: 2` and `game.splitScreen(2, { layout: 'rows' })`, and the scene's `res` is\n   `[640, 360]` so each half gets 640×180.\n   - **World.** One shared world and traffic. The cars collide and slipstream each other.\n   - **Screens.** Each viewport draws its own camera and its own HUD (clock, speed, place \"1st/2nd\", the gap).\n     `scene.hud(r)` draws the centre divider and the countdown.\n   - **Engine sounds.** Both players' engine sounds play, each at 70 %.\n4. **Win.** The first to the goal wins. If the clock runs out, the player further along wins. Results show both players\n   and offer Rematch, Change route or Menu.\n\n### 7.12 AI Hub online (`70-hub.js`)\n\nFollow `lib/hub/README.md` (protocol 1, client 1.4.0) exactly. The mode is **ghost**: each copy runs its own race, and\nthe copies send each other only their own car.\n\n1. **Detect.** `AIHub.active` is true from the first line.\n   - Skip the title, menus, attract mode and the touch notice.\n   - Hide **Quit to title**, Restart and Retire (pass `menu.quit: false` at construction), and hide Dev Mode.\n   - The garage and radio are skipped: the car is the Orchidea Spider in seat colour, the song is station 1, the\n     transmission automatic, and the passenger Marisol (chatter Less).\n2. **Connect.** `await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [1, 4], card: true })`. If it resolves to `null`, boot to\n   the title as normal.\n3. **Prepare.** Build the race for the fixed **Flower Sprint** route (S1 → S2 → S5, ending at a sprint goal arch). Take\n   traffic from `AIHub.rng('traffic')`. Call `AIHub.ready()` as soon as the boot steps finish, without waiting for a key.\n4. **Our own card (`hubcard` scene).**\n   - **It shows** the title logo over the start line at dawn, the players lined up as cars on the grid in their seat\n     colours with a READY tick, the goal line (\"Fastest to the Flower Sprint goal wins\"), the controls for the last-used\n     device (glyphs), and the countdown from `card.left`.\n   - **Answers.** Enter or ✕ (button 0) or a click on READY → `AIHub.answer('ready')`. △ (button 3), T, or a click on\n     MORE TIME → `AIHub.answer('more')`.\n   - **Limits.** The card draws from `AIHub.rng('card')` only. It has no settings or choices.\n5. **Start.** On `start`, run the gantry countdown so that GO lands when `AIHub.time() ≥ 0`. The sim holds the cars\n   still until then.\n6. **During the race.**\n   - **Clock.** In a session the clock counts **up** (race time). There is no time-out, and the hub's limit ends the\n     round.\n   - **Ghost send, every 100 ms:** `AIHub.send({ t: 'g', area: stageId, z, x, v, yaw, drift }, { reliable: false })`.\n   - **Progress, once a second:** `AIHub.progress({ score: metres along the route, label: 'Stage 2/3 · 64%' })`. Start\n     both the sends and the progress at `start`, even while the score is still 0: the harness only pokes random keys,\n     yet checks that every copy sends and reports.\n   - **Ghost cars.** Others are drawn as translucent cars in their seat colour with name tags (the pixel font; any\n     glyph it lacks becomes `?`; never HTML), only while they share our `area`, smoothed toward their last state. A\n     standings strip on the HUD shows everyone's progress.\n7. **Goal.** `AIHub.result({ time: Math.round(AIHub.time()) })`, plus `send({ t: 'done', time })` and\n   `stats: { 'Best drift': …, 'Near misses': … }`. Then keep cruising behind a \"Waiting for the others…\" banner while\n   the ghosts keep coming.\n8. **Endings.**\n   - **`time-up`:** if the race is still going, `AIHub.result({ score: metres })` at once.\n   - **`end`:** stop taking input, `stopMusic`, fade the engine loops and mute all audio, freeze the sim, and show\n     \"Round over\" until the hub removes the page.\n   - **`leave`:** drop that player's ghost.\n9. **Hard rules in a session.** No network except through the hub. No `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, `window.open` or\n   navigation. The pause menu offers Resume, Settings and Controls only. Options is ignored while Create is held (E4).\n   The empty storage the client provides means a fresh profile, which is fine.\n\n### 7.13 Characters and animation (`12-cast.js`)\n\nAll characters are engine `E.Humanoid` rigs dressed per §8.5, animated by the rig's own system: poses, expressions,\n`point`/`aim`, procedural breathing, and hair. Each one has a portrait from `rig.drawPortrait`.\n\n- **In the car.**\n  - **Seating.** The driver (left seat) and passenger (right seat) are drawn between the car's *under* and *over*\n    layers, so the bodywork hides their lower bodies. They are rendered seated (`down: .35`, legs hidden), sized to the\n    car's scale, and placed on the seat anchors of the car frame shown.\n  - **Hair and scarves** stream back with speed: the rig's hair plus a two-segment ribbon for a scarf or long hair,\n    whose angle follows speed and steering.\n- **Poses**, triggered by bus events:\n\n| Event | Driver | Passenger |\n|---|---|---|\n| steering | leans into the turn (`lean`), head tilts | sways the other way |\n| request intro | — | turns to the driver, `expr: 'shout'`, points ahead (`point`) |\n| success | — | `pose: 'cheer'`, `expr: 'smile'`, petals burst over the car |\n| fail | — | arms crossed (`pose: 'hips'` while seated), `expr: 'angry'` |\n| near miss / big air | — | covers her face (`pose: 'guard'`, `expr: 'wince'`) |\n| spin-out | head shake | both hands up (`pose: 'cheer'` with `expr: 'shout'` reads as a scream), \"Kyaaa!\" bark |\n| fork preference | — | points left or right (`point` + `aim`, the rig turned toward that side) |\n| goal | one arm up | `pose: 'wave'` at the crowd |\n| Juni's photo moments | — | raises her camera (a held sprite), with a flash particle |\n\n- **Bruno.** At the start line he waves his flag: a flag sprite on `rig.hand()`, the arm driven by an `E.MOVES`-style\n  spec looping `overhead`, with the flag cloth a 3-segment ribbon. At goals and debriefs he cheers, gives a thumbs-up\n  (`point` + `pose: 'hips'`) or facepalms (a hand at his head, `expr: 'wince'`).\n- **Endings and garage** use side-view vignettes at size 1.6. The couple step out of the car (a walk cycle from the\n  velocity) and play their scene's poses.\n- **Spectators** are pre-rendered Humanoid sprites in 3 poses × 4 palettes, waving through two frames, shown at\n  checkpoints, goals, beaches and the city.\n- **Car animation.**\n  - **Frames.** 33 yaw frames covering −90° to +90° (steering and drift) and 32 frames for a full spin.\n  - **Body.** It bobs on its suspension (a spring on bumps, rumble strips and landings) and rolls 1 px against the\n    wheels when cornering.\n  - **Wheels and lights.** The tread alternates between two phases by speed. Brake and reverse lights, blinkers and\n    headlights come from the lights layer.\n  - **Effects.** Backfire flames on lift-off above 6,000 rpm, and tyre smoke, skid marks, sparks, sand and spray as\n    particles.\n- **Traffic animation:** lane-change blinkers, brake lights, a spin when hit, and a honk \"♪\" when you cut one off.\n- **Request objects** (§8.7): balloons bob and burst into confetti; gnomes wobble, then fly off with googly eyes popping\n  out; bees buzz in sine paths; ribbons flutter and snap; cones tumble; flower arches sway and burst into petals;\n  bouquets spin and sparkle.\n- **UI animation:** focus pops (a scale-and-bounce tween), wipes with blowing petals between screens, the logo shine\n  sweeping across, and garden beds blooming.\n\n### 7.14 Music and the radio (`01-data-music.js`, `30-synth.js`, `32-radio.js`)\n\n- **Code only.** All music is engine song objects (`{ bpm, steps, tracks }`) on the extended synth (E6). There are no\n  audio files.\n- **The radio screen** is a car-radio faceplate.\n  - **Stations.** Five presets, plus two more once unlocked (§8.10). Each station tile shows its frequency (\"88.6\n    PETAL FM\") and the song title.\n  - **Tuning.** Left/right tunes. A 0.3 s burst of static plays between stations, then the song starts from its intro.\n  - **Choosing.** The mouse hovers and clicks a preset. ✕/Enter or the gas confirms.\n  - **Transmission.** △ or T toggles AUTO/MANUAL, shown big.\n- **During a race.** D-pad ↑ or R switches station, crossfading over 1 s with a \"♪ Coastline Crush · 91.2 SUN FM\" card.\n  Music ducks to 60 % under the passenger's speech and to 35 % when paused (the engine does that).\n- **Song form.** Every radio song is 64–96 bars, about 2–3 minutes, then loops.\n  - **Shape:** intro (4 bars) → A → B → chorus → A′ → bridge → chorus′ → turnaround.\n  - **Tracks:** at least 6 named tracks (`drums`, `perc`, `bass`, `keys` or `gtr`, `lead`, `pad`) and a `sparkle` layer\n    (`on: false`) that Full Bloom brings in.\n  - **Mix:** bass below 250 Hz, chords at 300–2,000 Hz, lead at 1–4 kHz, `sends` on keys and lead.\n- **Instrument presets** (`OB.Synth.inst`), built from E6 fields:\n\n| Preset | Recipe |\n|---|---|\n| `epiano` | FM, ratio 1, index 1.8 decaying, plus a soft triangle |\n| `slapbass` | saw and square, lowpass with fast envelope, accents |\n| `fingerbass` | triangle and sine, short attack |\n| `brass` | 3-voice saw unison, filter swell |\n| `stab` | short saw chord |\n| `steeldrum` | FM, ratio 3.5 |\n| `marimba` | sine, fast decay |\n| `lead` | pulse with vibrato after 0.15 s and slide |\n| `sax` | saw with a bandpass formant and vibrato |\n| `pad` | 4-voice detuned saw, slow attack, lowpass |\n| `organ` | sines at 1×/2×/3× |\n| `pluck` | noise burst into a resonant lowpass (a Karplus-like pluck) |\n| `chorusgtr` | 2 detuned square chords, panned |\n\n- **Composition rules**, because builders can't listen:\n  - Diatonic melodies on the stated key's scale, and chord tones on strong beats.\n  - The bass plays chord roots or fifths on beat 1.\n  - No two melodic tracks in the same octave at once.\n  - The lead rests at least 25 % of the steps.\n  - Drums always run under everything.\n  - The progressions, keys, tempos and hooks are listed per song in §8.10. The audio-lint test (§12) checks levels and\n    clipping, and the spectrum test checks the mix's bands.\n\n### 7.15 Sound effects, the engine note and ambience\n\n- **Engine** (`OB.EngineSound`, on E7 loops), one per player car:\n  - **Sources.** Two saw oscillators at the firing frequency `f = rpm/60 × cylinders/2` and at 2f, plus a square sub at\n    f/2, through a lowpass whose cutoff follows throttle (600–4,000 Hz) and a soft-clip shaper.\n  - **Character.** Each car has its own cylinder count (4/6/8/12) and filter colour (§8.4).\n  - **Gear changes** dip the pitch with a short \"chuff\". Lift-off above 6,000 rpm adds backfire crackles (noise bursts).\n  - **Rivals** get one shared quieter loop for the nearest rival.\n  - **Traffic** gets a passing \"whoosh\": noise rising with closing speed and panned by side.\n- **Tyres.** A squeal from 2–3 triangle oscillators near 900–1,400 Hz with a fast random wobble; its gain follows the\n  drift angle and hard cornering. Rumble strips give a 30 Hz pulse train. Gravel and sand give lowpass noise crunch.\n- **Wind** is bandpass noise rising with v². In the slipstream it hushes (lowpass), then pops back on the slingshot\n  whoosh.\n- **Crashes** are soft and funny, never harsh: a bump \"thunk\" (sine drop with noise), a spin (squeal sweep plus the\n  passenger's \"Kyaaa!\"), and a scrape (filtered noise with sparkle ticks).\n- **Request sounds:**\n\n| Event | Sound |\n|---|---|\n| balloon pop | noise click and a rising chirp |\n| gnome bonk | \"boink\": triangle 300→900 Hz with a squeak |\n| bee buzz | a loop, saw at 220 Hz with 30 Hz amplitude wobble, panned by position |\n| ribbon snap | noise and a pitch drop |\n| cone | a hollow \"tok\" |\n| arch | harp gliss (pluck arpeggio) |\n| bouquet | chime |\n| photo | shutter click and flash zap |\n| request success | rising chimes |\n| request fail | deflating trombone (saw slide down with vibrato) |\n\n- **Race events:**\n\n| Event | Sound |\n|---|---|\n| countdown | beeps, then GO |\n| checkpoint | whoosh and a bright chord |\n| time warning | tick |\n| near miss | a short \"zing\" |\n| Full Bloom | sparkle |\n| goal fanfare | — |\n| crowd cheer | layered noise bursts and a few babble \"yay\"s |\n| seagulls (beach) | — |\n| cicadas (vineyard and canyon) | — |\n| birds (fields) | — |\n| waves | lowpass noise swells |\n| city night hum | — |\n| fireworks | thump, then crackle |\n| horn | per car: a two-tone square pair |\n\n- **Ambience** (`OB.Ambience`) is one quiet loop set per kit. It crossfades at junctions and sits under the music.\n  Tunnels raise `audio.reverb` from 0.15 to 0.6 and add an echo-y wash to the engine.\n- **UI sounds:** focus tick, confirm, back, error, toggle, slider tick, and planting a seed (a soft pop and a bloom\n  sparkle).\n\n### 7.16 Voices: Animalese and Simlish-style babble (`31-voice.js`)\n\n- **Text to syllables.** Lower-case the text and split it into syllables: a consonant onset plus a vowel nucleus.\n  Punctuation becomes a pause (comma 80 ms, full stop 160 ms, \"…\" 300 ms). A run of capitals is shouted (+3 dB, +2\n  semitones).\n- **Synthesis per syllable** (60–110 ms, on the voice bus):\n  - **Source:** a saw and a pulse at `f0`, through two parallel bandpass formants. The vowel sets F1/F2: a 800/1200,\n    e 500/1900, i 300/2300, o 500/900, u 350/800 (Hz). Each character scales them by its own factor.\n  - **Onsets:** plosives (p b t d k g) get an 8–15 ms noise burst at 2–4 kHz. Fricatives (s z f v h, sh) get 30–50 ms\n    of filtered noise (high for s, low for f and h). Nasals and liquids glide `f0`.\n  - **Pitch:** `f0` follows an intonation contour. Statements fall at the end, questions rise, and \"!\" lifts the start.\n    A per-syllable jitter comes from an rng seeded by the text, so a line always sounds the same.\n- **Moods:**\n\n| Mood | Pitch | Speed | Other |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| happy | +3 semitones | — | wider contour |\n| excited | +5 | +20 % | — |\n| angry | −2 | — | louder, clipped decays |\n| sad | −4 | −20 % | breathy noise mix |\n| scared | +6 | — | tremolo |\n\n- **Per-character voices** (data in `OB.CAST`):\n\n| Character | Base f0 | Formant × | Rate (syllables/s) | Wave | Quirk |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Marisol | 290 Hz | 1.12 | 13 | pulse 25 % | giggle \"hee\" barks |\n| Vivi | 230 Hz | 1.0 | 11 | saw | long \"Daaarling\" vowels, dramatic swoops |\n| Juni | 320 Hz | 1.18 | 15 | pulse 12 % | raspy noise mix |\n| Bruno | 110 Hz | 0.8 | 10 | saw | booming, with a laugh bark \"Ho ho!\" |\n| Kiki | 270 Hz | 1.1 | 14 | — | — |\n| Lance | 140 Hz | — | — | — | — |\n| Lacey | 250 Hz | — | — | — | — |\n| Bram/Basil | 125 and 135 Hz | — | — | — | — |\n| Ottavio | 100 Hz | — | slow, wavering | — | — |\n| Announcer | 150 Hz | — | — | — | big reverb send, used for \"CHECKPOINT!\" and \"GOAL!\" |\n","numLines":560,"startLine":300,"totalLines":1758}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_011EMpuDnjoAhdXHhmHk6jAs","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
