{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghpAWvnnQnL1M1TSsAue","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019YgKDULaMdWkZowFhHiibM","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-043153__xe1b7e1/work/PLAN.md","offset":860,"limit":420},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19001,"cache_read_input_tokens":39094,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":19001},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f95c51ac-ca98-4f0d-bbff-431b6595447e","uuid":"fa4b7f52-73de-43d8-ab18-4d14353fce46","timestamp":"2026-11-04T11:32:12.319Z","request_id":"req_011CghpAWdgwshYGCsCg7w9b","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_019YgKDULaMdWkZowFhHiibM":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-043153__xe1b7e1/work/PLAN.md","offset":860,"limit":420}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019YgKDULaMdWkZowFhHiibM","type":"tool_result","content":"860\t- **Subtitles always show.** Speech bubbles type the exact line at the babble's pace (the planner returns per-syllable\n861\t  times), and the portrait's mouth moves while it types. The voice bus has its own volume (Settings, Sound, Voices).\n862\t- **The planner is pure:** `plan(text, who, mood) → { syllables: [{ t, dur, f0, F1, F2, onset }], duration }`. It is\n863\t  tested headless (counts, durations, determinism) without Web Audio.\n864\t\n865\t### 7.17 UI and UX for 10 feet: screens and the UI kit (`40-ui.js`, `50-front.js`)\n866\t\n867\t- **Sizes.** Design at 480×270, which is 4× at 1080p and 8× at 4K.\n868\t  - **Body text** is never below the 5×7 font at scale 1 (28 px tall at 1080p). Don't use `font: 'tiny'` for anything a\n869\t    player reads.\n870\t  - **Menu items** are scale 2, headings scale 3–4 (`E.font.title`), with an outline or shadow on everything over the\n871\t    world.\n872\t- **Density.**\n873\t  - At most 7 focusable items per screen, and one main column or carousel. No scrolling lists: page them instead.\n874\t  - Every screen has a footer of prompts (\"✕ Select ○ Back\") and a way back (○, Esc, Backspace or a right-click).\n875\t- **Focus.** One thick focus frame per screen: a 2 px outline in a pulsing sun-yellow plus the pop tween.\n876\t  - **Spatial navigation:** the d-pad, stick, arrows or WASD move to the nearest item in that direction, with\n877\t    `input.repeat` for held directions.\n878\t  - **Mouse:** moving over an item focuses it, and a left click activates it. Mouse movement shows the pointer, and it\n879\t    hides after 2 s still in races.\n880\t- **Screens.**\n881\t  - **Title.**\n882\t    - **Logo:** \"OutBloom\" extruded in a pink → orange → gold gradient, a \"2006\" badge, and \"COAST 2 COAST\" on a road\n883\t      stripe.\n884\t    - **Scene:** Sunrise Strand at dawn, the Orchidea parked, petals drifting, the sea glittering with bloom.\n885\t    - **Prompt:** \"PRESS ✕\" or \"PRESS ENTER\" by device, plus the version label (`E.versionLabel()`).\n886\t    - **Music:** \"OutBloom!\".\n887\t    - **Idle:** after 10 s, the attract mode starts.\n888\t  - **Main menu.** A horizontal carousel of six big mode cards, each a small live illustration with a one-line\n889\t    description: ARCADE RUN, LOVE IN BLOOM, COAST 2 COAST TOUR, TIME TRIAL, VERSUS 2P and GARAGE GARDEN. Below it, a row\n890\t    of three: SETTINGS, HOW TO PLAY, ABOUT & CREDITS.\n891\t  - **Garage** (one screen, four rows):\n892\t    - **Car:** a carousel turning the car on a turntable through its yaw frames, with 3 stat bars (Speed, Acceleration,\n893\t      Handling) and a class badge.\n894\t    - **Paint:** swatches.\n895\t    - **Driver:** Leo or Lea.\n896\t    - **Passenger:** Marisol, Vivi or Juni. Her portrait and a one-line bio show, and her favourite requests as icons.\n897\t    - Locked items show a padlock and \"Plant it in the Garage Garden\".\n898\t  - **Radio:** see §7.14.\n899\t  - **How to Play:** six pages (Driving, Drifting, Slipstream & near misses, Forks & checkpoints, Love in Bloom, Tour &\n900\t    Garden). Each page has a live mini-demo (the race renderer in a framed box driven by the bot) and two sentences\n901\t    with glyph prompts.\n902\t  - **About & Credits:** paged (§7.25).\n903\t- **Transitions** use `game.go(…, { fade: 'wipe' })` with a petal overlay. Reduce motion makes them a plain fade.\n904\t- **Engine screens.** The pause menu and the Settings, Controls and Help screens are the engine's standard HTML\n905\t  (`E.Shell`). Theme them with the sunset palette and the E8 sizes, and give the Help screen our keys and sections (§7.19).\n906\t\n907\t### 7.18 The race HUD (`41-hud.js`)\n908\t\n909\tThe HUD anchors to `r.W`/`r.H` per viewport. Each corner holds one cluster, and the road's centre always stays clear.\n910\t\n911\t| Place | Content |\n912\t|---|---|\n913\t| top centre | **TIME** (label scale 1, digits scale 4, a little sun icon). \"+57\" flies in at checkpoints. Red and flashing below 10 s. |\n914\t| top left | **SCORE** at scale 2, and under it the **Bloom chain**: five flower icons filling, \"×3\". |\n915\t| top right | stage name and stage time (scale 1), and under them the **mini-map**: 15 dots in the pyramid with your path lit and a pulsing dot for you. In a hub session, the standings strip. |\n916\t| bottom right | **speed** digits at scale 3 with the unit (km/h or mph), a tachometer arc (green zone, red line), the gear (\"A3\" or \"M4\") |\n917\t| bottom left | Love in Bloom only: the passenger portrait (40 px), mood face, petal ring and count, and above it the **request card** (icon, call-out, progress, window bar) |\n918\t| centre (brief) | banners: request call-outs, CHECKPOINT, TIME EXTENDED!, FORK arrows, SLIPSTREAM!, NEAR MISS +500, FULL BLOOM!, PERFECT START, GOAL! (scale 3, outlined, at most 2.5 s, never two at once: they queue) |\n919\t| near the car | the passenger's speech bubble (pixel font, scale 1, at most 2 lines of 34 characters), above the car's right seat |\n920\t\n921\t**Hints.** While the Hints setting is on, they appear at most once each per profile, with glyphs: \"Hold R2 to\n922\taccelerate\", \"Tap ○ while steering to drift\", \"Tuck in behind a car to slipstream\", \"Steer left or right at the fork\",\n923\tand \"Rev in the green at GO\". Rebinding updates them.\n924\t\n925\t### 7.19 Input and prompts (`00-core.js`, `40-ui.js`; Appendix B)\n926\t\n927\t- **The preset.** `OB.INPUT` keeps the engine's menu actions (`up down left right start pause confirm cancel`) and adds\n928\t  `gas`, `brake`, `drift`, `shiftUp`, `shiftDown`, `horn`, `camera` and `radio`. Throttle and brake read `input.analog`\n929\t  (E3).\n930\t- **Controls screen.** It names every action and lets the player rebind them (the engine's Controls screen).\n931\t- **Prompts.** `OB.UI.prompt(g, x, y, action, text, { scale })` draws the glyph for `input.lastDevice`:\n932\t  - **Controller:** PlayStation by default. The icons are drawn as sprites: ✕ blue, ○ red, □ pink, △ green; L1, R1, L2\n933\t    and R2 as pills; Options and Create as small ovals; the d-pad and sticks. Settings, Controller, Button symbols:\n934\t    **Xbox** switches to A (green), B (red), X (blue), Y (yellow), LB, RB, LT, RT and Menu/View.\n935\t  - **Keyboard:** keycaps showing the bound key (W, ↑, Space, Esc…).\n936\t  - **Mouse:** left and right button and wheel icons.\n937\t  - **Labels** come from `input.label(action)` after rebinding. We map them to icons (the font has no ○ □ △).\n938\t- **Live switching.** Every prompt (HUD hints, menu footers, the hub card, How to Play) follows the device used last,\n939\t  from the next frame.\n940\t- **Controller hot-plug.** The engine pauses a race when the pad in use is unplugged. Its banner names the player.\n941\t\n942\t### 7.20 Settings (ours, beside the engine's standard ones)\n943\t\n944\tGroup \"Game\" (with `E.Shell` settings items):\n945\t\n946\t| Setting | Options | Default |\n947\t|---|---|---|\n948\t| Transmission | Automatic / Manual | Automatic |\n949\t| Drift input | All / Brake tap / Drift button | All |\n950\t| Steering assist | Off / Low / High | Low |\n951\t| Difficulty | Easy / Normal / Hard | Normal |\n952\t| Speed units | km/h / mph | km/h |\n953\t| Camera | Chase / High / Bumper | Chase |\n954\t| Passenger chatter | Full / Less / Off | Full |\n955\t| HUD size | Large / Extra large (×1.25 scale steps) | Large |\n956\t| Hints | On / Off | On |\n957\t\n958\tUnder Sound: **Voices** (0–100 %). Under Controller: button symbols PlayStation or Xbox (E5). The engine's reduce\n959\tmotion, shake and flash settings cut the petals and speed lines, shake and flashes (bloom stays). Settings are saved per\n960\tgame (`id: 'outbloom2006'`).\n961\t\n962\t### 7.21 Touch-only devices (`71-touch.js`)\n963\t\n964\t- **Detection.** A device is touch-only when it has touch (`navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0` or `ontouchstart`), has no\n965\t  fine pointer (`!matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches`), no gamepad is connected, and no `keydown` has been seen.\n966\t  It is re-checked on `gamepadconnected` and on any `keydown`; either one switches to normal play at once.\n967\t- **Behaviour.**\n968\t  - Pass `touch: []` on every scene and set `input.touchEnabled = false`, so the engine shows no touch buttons.\n969\t  - The title shows a large panel: **\"Controller or keyboard required\"** with a pad and a keyboard icon, plus a big\n970\t    **SETTINGS** button (a tap opens `game.menu('settings')`). The engine's corner menu button stays.\n971\t  - **The attract mode runs anyway.** It starts after 10 s as usual. Taps don't count as \"input\" for leaving it, since\n972\t    they can't play. A tap only flashes the notice, except a tap on SETTINGS. The notice stays over the demo, dimmed.\n973\t- **Test:** `tools/check.mjs --size 390x844@3` (an emulated phone) shows the notice and no touch buttons, and the\n974\t  attract scene starts.\n975\t\n976\t### 7.22 Attract mode (`60-attract.js`)\n977\t\n978\t- **The idle clock.** `OB.UI` tracks the last input: any action press, a stick past the dead zone, a mouse move over\n979\t  4 px, a wheel or a click. On the **title, menu, garage, radio, tour, garden, trial and versus** screens, 10 s with\n980\t  none starts `attract`. The engine's HTML screens (settings, controls) don't count.\n981\t- **The playlist.** Each cycle is about 95 s in five segments, and each cycle picks different stages, rotating through\n982\t  all 15 on a seeded order:\n983\t  1. **Arcade:** the start line, Bruno's flag, the countdown, a perfect start, drifting the first bends, slipstreaming a\n984\t     bus (25 s).\n985\t  2. **A fork:** the signs, the bot picking a branch, the sky dissolving, the checkpoint and its stage card (15 s).\n986\t  3. **Love in Bloom:** a request (balloons, gnomes or ribbons, rotating), the passenger's call-out, a Perfect, petals\n987\t     (20 s).\n988\t  4. **Tour:** a Drift Duel moment against Kiki, or the Versus split screen with two bots, alternating each cycle\n989\t     (20 s).\n990\t  5. **A goal ending:** 10 s of a vignette, then the arcade table with initials and \"PRESS START\" over the title logo\n991\t     (5 s).\n992\t- **Cheats on:** `noCollide`, `infiniteTime`, `perfectRequests` and `autoDrift`. The bot (`OB.Brain`) drives.\n993\t- **On screen:** a \"DEMO PLAY\" tag and a blinking \"PRESS ✕ / PRESS ENTER\" (by the last device) over every segment. The\n994\t  game's music plays, with sound effects at 60 %.\n995\t- **Leaving.** Any input (as defined above) ends the attract mode at once and goes back to the screen and focus it came\n996\t  from. The input that woke it is consumed and does nothing else.\n997\t- **Never in a hub session.** It never touches the profile: there is no saving, and no records or seeds.\n998\t\n999\t### 7.23 Dev mode (`61-dev.js`)\n1000\t\n1001\t- **How to reach it.** From the pause menu's **Dev Mode** item (outside hub sessions), or with F9 while paused. The\n1002\t  panel opens over the frozen race (`timeScale = 0` while open), and Back returns to the pause menu.\n1003\t- **Looks.** It is a canvas panel in the UI kit, large and pad-navigable (L1/R1 or Q/E switch tabs, the mouse drags\n1004\t  sliders).\n1005\t- **Tabs:**\n1006\t  1. **Tuning:** a live slider for every Appendix A value, in groups (Car, Drift, Slipstream, Traffic, Clock, Rivals,\n1007\t     Love in Bloom, Camera, World). Each row shows its value, a reset-this-row button and a ● when it differs from the\n1008\t     default. **RESET ALL TO DEFAULTS** sits at the top. Changes apply on the next step. With \"Keep after reload\" on,\n1009\t     values are saved to `E.store('outbloom:dev')`.\n1010\t  2. **Cheats:** infinite time, no collisions, perfect requests, auto drive (the bot drives you), auto drift, freeze\n1011\t     traffic, no traffic, invincible mood, show hitboxes, show the debug overlay (stage, segment, z, x, v, rpm, gear,\n1012\t     curve, drift a and score, slip, Bloom points, request state, traffic count, frames per second), hide the HUD,\n1013\t     slow motion ×0.25 and fast ×2.\n1014\t  3. **Warp:** jump to any of the 15 stages at 0/25/50/75/95 %, the next checkpoint or the fork, a forced fork side,\n1015\t     play any goal ending at any tier, trigger any request type now, spawn a traffic car or ribbon pair ahead, spawn a\n1016\t     rival, set the time of day and weather, play the attract demo, play the credits, start any mission.\n1017\t  4. **Audio:** a jukebox (every song with its layers toggled), every sound effect, every character's voice with a\n1018\t     sample line in each mood, and the reverb amount.\n1019\t  5. **Save:** give 1,000 seeds, unlock everything, clear the career, set every mission's rank, reset the profile (asks\n1020\t     first, in the panel), and show the profile as JSON in a read-only, paged view.\n1021\t  6. **Info:** version, seed, route, last 20 bus events, error count.\n1022\t\n1023\t### 7.24 Saves and progression data (`26-progress.js`)\n1024\t\n1025\tOne profile in `E.store('outbloom:profile')`, versioned with `v: 1` and a `migrate(from)` table. It is written after\n1026\teach run, mission, purchase and settings change, never on page close. Where storage is blocked, the game plays on for\n1027\tthe visit.\n1028\t\n1029\t```js\n1030\t{ v: 1, seeds: 0, seenIntro: false,\n1031\t  unlocked: { cars: ['primavera', 'orchidea'], editions: [], paints: ['base'], songs: [0,1,2,3,4], outfits: { marisol: [0], vivi: [0], juni: [0] }, mirror: false },\n1032\t  last: { car: 'orchidea', paint: 0, driver: 'leo', passenger: 'marisol', station: 0, transmission: 'auto', camera: 'chase' },\n1033\t  career: { M1: { cleared: true, rank: 'A', best: 41250 }, … },\n1034\t  goals: { A: { bloom: true, bud: false, wilt: false }, … },\n1035\t  records: { stage: { S1: { ms, ghost } }, route: { 'LRRL': { score, ms } }, medals: { S1: 'gold' } },\n1036\t  hints: { drift: true, … } }\n1037\t```\n1038\t\n1039\tThe arcade tables use `E.Arcade` (ids `'outbloom-arcade'` and `'outbloom-love'`).\n1040\t\n1041\t### 7.25 About and Credits\n1042\t\n1043\tThe About & Credits screen has big pages, turned with ◀ ▶ (or a click on the arrows), one heading and at most about 8\n1044\tshort lines each:\n1045\t\n1046\t1. **Credits:** the game; built on the my-3D2dge engine (MIT); AI Hub session protocol; the asset-library credits for\n1047\t   any store set used (with each set's `credit` line); \"Inspired by OutRun 2006: Coast 2 Coast (SEGA / Sumo Digital,\n1048\t   2006). An original homage. Not affiliated.\"\n1049\t2. **Source Information**, the 14 fields of `TASK.md` in its words, with \"unknown\" where it can't be confirmed:\n1050\t   Date, Source, Plan (`PLAN.md`), Genre and Perspective, Tech, Art Direction, Animation, Audio, Online, Testing,\n1051\t   Research, Environment, Model and Usage.\n1052\t3. **Testing Feedback** (4 answers). 4. **Engine Feedback** (6 answers). 5. **Prompt Feedback** (4 answers).\n1053\t\n1054\tThese pages read from one data object, `OB.ABOUT`, which `build.mjs` also writes into `exit-interview.json`. That keeps\n1055\tthe in-game text and the JSON identical. Fill in the real answers at the end of the build (§10, M9).\n1056\t\n1057\t---\n1058\t\n1059\t## 8. Content\n1060\t\n1061\t### 8.1 Bellafiore and the story\n1062\t\n1063\t- **The island.** Bellafiore is a sun-soaked island country shaped like a leaf, with an eastern **Alba Coast**\n1064\t  (sunrise) and a western **Vespera Coast** (sunset). Between them lie lavender ridges, lemon cliffs, sunflower\n1065\t  prairies, a mountain lake, a red canyon, alpine passes in cherry blossom, an old walled town, wine country, a pink\n1066\t  lagoon, a jungle, a neon riviera, a starry desert and the windy highlands.\n1067\t- **The festival.** Every spring, the whole island blooms in a single week, the *Settimana dei Fiori*.\n1068\t- **The premise.** It is the first morning of the festival. You (Leo or Lea), a young driver with a borrowed Fioretti\n1069\t  and big dreams, promised your passenger the perfect day: \"coast to coast before the sun goes down\". Bruno Brezza, a\n1070\t  rally legend who now runs the island's festival tour, waves you off from the Sunrise Strand.\n1071\t- **Story beats** (lines in §8.11):\n1072\t\n1073\t| Beat | Mode | What happens |\n1074\t|---|---|---|\n1075\t| Dawn start | Arcade, Love | Bruno: \"Morning, champ! The west coast is waiting, and so is the sunset!\" The passenger answers with her own opener. |\n1076\t| Each fork | Love | the passenger's preference, with a reason in character |\n1077\t| Each checkpoint | all | a line about the next region (\"Lavender! Smell that?\") |\n1078\t| Time running low | all | the passenger worries in character |\n1079\t| Goal | all | the ending vignette for that goal and mood (§8.3) |\n1080\t| Tour opening | Tour | Bruno's garage. He hands you the Tour's postcard book. |\n1081\t| Giver intros and outros | Tour | 3 lines each, opening and closing every giver's arc |\n1082\t| Finale | Tour | the group photo at the goal, Bruno's toast (\"To the road, and to whoever's beside you on it!\"), then the credits |\n1083\t\n1084\t### 8.2 The stages (all 15)\n1085\t\n1086\tShared rules for every stage:\n1087\t- 820–980 segments long, with a junction or goal run-in after that.\n1088\t- The first 150 segments are flat.\n1089\t- Two or three request slots (in Love in Bloom), never inside the first 120 segments or the junction.\n1090\t- 4–8 scenery rules and at least 2 landmarks.\n1091\t- A target time of 52–64 s for the reference bot with the Orchidea on Normal. The test checks every stage falls in\n1092\t  48–70 s.\n1093\t\n1094\tCurve values run from −6 to 6 (+ bends right). Hills are in units over the section.\n1095\t\n1096\t| ID | Name (row/col) | Kit · time of day | Lanes · difficulty | Layout beats (in order) | Landmarks and set pieces | Traffic mix | Love in Bloom slots |\n1097\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n1098\t| S1 | **Sunrise Strand** (1/1) | beach · dawn | 3 · ●○○○○ | the start grid; a long straight; 3 gentle S-bends (curves of 2–3); a palm avenue whose fronds meet overhead (a span); a seawall bend (4); a rise and a dip to the junction | the pier with its Ferris wheel (left, mid-stage); pastel beach huts; lifeguard towers; the start gantry with Bruno | hatch 3, van 1, icecream 0.4, bus 0.6 | balloons (12) at 18 %; pass (8) at 62 % |\n1099\t| S2 | **Lavender Ridge** (2/1) | fields (lavender) · morning | 3 · ●○○○○ | 6 rolling crests (hills of ±1500, small air on 2); long sweepers (2–3); one 4 with a farmhouse on the outside | hot-air balloons in the sky; a stone farmhouse with a water tower; beehive rows (the bees' home) | hatch 2, van 2, pickup 1, camper 1 | bees (8) at 25 %; drift (target 2 long drifts) at 65 % |\n1100\t| S3 | **Lemon Cliffs** (2/2) | cliffs · morning | 2 · ●●●○○ | the cliff wall on the right (edge `cliff`), a rail and the sea drop on the left; tunnel 1 (60 segments); bends of 4–5; a gallery tunnel open to the sea (light stripes); a 6 hairpin before the junction | a terraced pastel village across the bay; lemon groves; a cliff chapel | scooter 2, hatch 2, van 1, bus 0.5 | cones (10) at 20 %; close (5) at 60 % |\n1101\t| S4 | **Sunflower Prairie** (3/1) | fields (sunflower) · late morning | 4 · ●○○○○ | very flat; two 30-second straights; wide sweepers (1–2); a level crossing at 50 % with a train in the backdrop | red barns and silos; wind turbines with turning blades; a giant hay-bale sculpture of a sunflower | tractor 1.5, pickup 2, sedan 2, truck 1 | draft (6 s) at 20 %; speed (≥ 250) at 60 % |\n1102\t| S5 | **Lake Serenata** (3/2) | lake · noon | 3 · ●●○○○ | lake water on the left, pines on the right; a long timber truss bridge (rails both sides, shadow stripes); a waterfall bend (4); a sun-shower event from 55 to 75 % (wet shine, spray, a rainbow in the sky) | a paddle steamer moving on the lake; boathouses; the waterfall | sedan 2, camper 2, van 1, bus 0.5 | arches (7) at 22 %; bouquets (12) at 66 % |\n1103\t| S6 | **Rosa Canyon** (3/3) | canyon · noon | 2 · ●●●●○ | big dips and crests (±4000, air ×3); bends of 5–6; 3 rock arches (short tunnels); heat shimmer | mesas; a natural rock arch over the road; flowering cacti; a gnome souvenir stand | pickup 2, truck 1, camper 1 | gnomes (10) at 30 %; drift at 70 % |\n1104\t| S7 | **Cherry Pass** (4/1) | alpine · afternoon | 3 · ●●○○○ | a climb of +6000 overall; 3 hairpin sequences (6, then 5); 2 snow sheds; a petal storm from 40 to 70 %; the descent to the junction | snowy peaks; chalets; cherry trees in bloom; cows in meadows | sedan 2, camper 1, bus 1, van 1 | drift at 25 %; clean at 60 % |\n1105\t| S8 | **Old Town Fiorenza** (4/2) | town · afternoon | 3 (2 in the gate) · ●●●○○ | cobbles; facades on both sides; bunting spans; a city-gate tunnel; four sharp corners (6, short); a piazza where the road widens to 4 around a fountain sweep | the cathedral dome (photo spot); a flower market; café awnings; a fountain | taxi 2, scooter 3, van 1, bus 1 | photo (cathedral) at 35 %; pass (10) at 70 % |\n1106\t| S9 | **Vineyard Way** (4/3) | vineyard · late afternoon | 3 · ●●●○○ | rolling hills (±2000); a cypress avenue (dense runs both sides); sweepers (3–4); one 5 below the castle | a hilltop castle; villas; grape rows (ground `rows`); barrel stacks | tractor 1, pickup 2, van 1, sedan 1 | gnomes (10) at 20 %; ribbons (5) at 62 % |\n1107\t| S10 | **Seafoam Causeway** (4/4) | lagoon · late afternoon | 2 · ●●●●● | water on both sides, behind low rails; long and fast; 5 crosswind gusts; a lift-bridge hump (air); salt pans with pink water | a flamingo flock taking off as you pass; salt pyramids; the lift bridge | sedan 2, truck 2, camper 1 | balloons in the wind (12, drifting sideways) at 25 %; speed (≥ 240) at 65 % |\n1108\t| S11 | **Golden Bay** (5/1) | beach · sunset | 3 · ●○○○○ | the sea on the right this time; a promenade with lantern strings (lit); gentle sweepers; the run-in to the goal | sailboats; beach volleyball nets; a lantern-festival stage → **Goal A** | hatch 2, icecream 0.5, bus 1, van 1 | bouquets (12) at 25 %; balloons (14) at 62 % |\n1109\t| S12 | **Orchid Jungle** (5/2) | jungle · sunset | 3 · ●●○○○ | canopy spans (light shafts); a wet road; bends of 3–5; a vine bridge with rails; ruin arches | waterfalls; ancient stone ruins; glowing orchids (emissive); parrots | pickup 2, camper 1, van 1 | arches (8) at 22 %; bees (8) at 60 % → **Goal B** |\n1110\t| S13 | **Moonlit Riviera** (5/3) | night city · night | 4 · ●●●○○ | a marina promenade; wide sweepers; 2 tunnels under the city with sodium lamps; fireworks over the water from 60 % | the casino with its giant neon flower; yachts with lights; a funicular | taxi 2, sedan 3, bus 1, scooter 2 | close (6) at 30 %; ribbons (6) at 65 % → **Goal C** |\n1111\t| S14 | **Starlight Desert** (5/4) | night desert · night | 3 · ●●●●○ | long fast straights; big crests (air ×4); 2 bends of 5 | night-blooming cacti (emissive white); a neon motel; a radio-telescope dish; shooting stars | truck 2, pickup 2, camper 1 | draft (6 s) at 25 %; cones (12) at 60 % → **Goal D** |\n1112\t| S15 | **Aurora Highlands** (5/5) | highlands · twilight | 2 · ●●●●● | stone walls on both edges; blind crests; bends of 5–6 in pairs; a narrow stone bridge | the aurora overhead; standing stones; a castle ruin; heather | sedan 1, camper 1, truck 1, pickup 1 | drift at 20 %; clean at 58 % → **Goal E** |\n1113\t\n1114\t**Forks** (left/right): S1→S2/S3 · S2→S4/S5 · S3→S5/S6 · S4→S7/S8 · S5→S8/S9 · S6→S9/S10 · S7→S11/S12 ·\n1115\tS8→S12/S13 · S9→S13/S14 · S10→S14/S15. Each row-5 stage ends at its own goal: S11→A, S12→B, S13→C, S14→D, S15→E.\n1116\t\n1117\t**S1 in full, the worked example** (`[segments, curve, hill]`):\n1118\t\n1119\t```\n1120\t[150,0,0]  start grid at seg 20 (gantry, Bruno at x = −1.6), flat by rule\n1121\t[70,2,0] [40,0,0] [70,-3,0] [60,0,800] [50,3,-800]\n1122\t[90,0,0] {span: 'palms', 90 segs}\n1123\t[60,-2,0] [50,4,0] {edgeL: 'stonewall', groundL: 'water' (the seawall bend)}\n1124\t[80,0,1200] [70,0,-1200] [60,-3,0]\n1125\t[40,0,0]   → junction (80) to S2 | S3\n1126\t```\n1127\t\n1128\tThat totals 890 segments plus the junction. Ground: sea on the left (`water` with foam rows) and sand on the right\n1129\t(`sand`). Scenery rules: palm (R, offset 0.4–1.6, every 4, jitter 2), umbrella clusters (R, 0.6–2.2, every 9, cluster\n1130\t3), beach huts (R, 2.4, every 26), lifeguard tower (R, 1.8, every 60), surfboard racks (R, every 33), buoys on the water\n1131\t(L, every 12, 1.5–4.0).\n1132\t\n1133\t### 8.3 Goals and endings (5 goals × 3 moods)\n1134\t\n1135\tEach ending is a 10–14 s side-view vignette. It has a backdrop (the goal's own sky), props, the car arriving and\n1136\tparking, the couple stepping out, a local character or the crowd, and **3 beats**: an arrival line, the passenger's line\n1137\tfor this mood (one per passenger, so 5 × 3 × 3 = 45 lines), and a caption card. Arcade mode picks the mood from the time\n1138\tleft: Bloom at 20 s or more, Bud at 5 s or more, Wilt below. Love in Bloom picks it from the grade (§7.7).\n1139\t\n1140\t| Goal | Place | Bloom | Bud | Wilt |\n1141\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n1142\t| A | **Lantern Pier**, Porto Lucciola | a thousand lanterns rise; the pier cheers; she kisses your cheek; fireworks | the two of you release one lantern together | the lanterns are already gone, so she sulks: \"You owe me gelato.\" |\n1143\t| B | **Petal Falls Chapel** | you crash a wedding by accident, she catches the bouquet and winks: \"Don't get any ideas. Yet.\" | the guests wave you in for cake | soaked by the waterfall spray, you run off laughing |\n1144\t| C | **Grand Hotel Vespera** | red carpet, flashbulbs, fireworks over the bay | the valet takes the keys with a bow | the valet won't park \"that dusty thing\", so you eat at the noodle cart and she secretly loves it |\n1145\t| D | **Stargazer Observatory** | the dome opens to a meteor shower and you both make wishes | one shooting star | clouds roll in; the astronomer offers cocoa |\n1146\t| E | **Finisterra Lighthouse** | the green flash at sunset: \"Best. Day. Ever.\" | the last sliver of sun | the sun is gone and the wind is cold, so you share your jacket |\n1147\t\n1148\tCaption card: \"GOAL C · GRAND HOTEL VESPERA\". Underneath is the mood title: \"BLOOM: A Star Arrives\" / \"BUD: Valet\n1149\tParking\" / \"WILT: Noodles Under the Neon\". Write all 15 titles in that pattern.\n1150\t\n1151\t### 8.4 The cars: Fioretti, five open-tops\n1152\t\n1153\t| Id | Name | Look (recipe, §9.3) | Top km/h | 0–100 s | Steer | Drift grip | Weight | Engine | Class · how to get it |\n1154\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n1155\t| `primavera` | **Primavera 300 Spider** (1962) | round fenders, chrome bumpers, tiny fins, a wood steering wheel; cream | 245 | 5.8 | 2.35 | 0.92 (easy) | 0.85 | 4 cylinders, warm | Novice · start |\n1156\t| `orchidea` | **Orchidea Spider** (2004), the default | a modern wedge-soft body, twin roll hoops, round tail lamps; coral red | 272 | 4.5 | 2.20 | 0.85 | 1.00 | V8 | Novice+ · start |\n1157\t| `gardenia` | **Gardenia GT Targa** (1987) | a sharp wedge, pop-up lamps, side strakes, a targa bar; pearl white with pink pinstripes | 292 | 4.0 | 2.00 | 0.78 (hard) | 1.05 | flat-12, rasp | Intermediate · garden, 600 |\n1158\t| `magnolia` | **Magnolia V12 Barchetta** (1998) | a long bonnet, no roof at all, twin humps; deep blue | 305 | 4.2 | 1.95 | 0.84 | 1.20 | V12, smooth | Intermediate+ · garden, 900 |\n1159\t| `girasole` | **Girasole Hyper Spider** (2026) | a low canopy-less hypercar, a sunflower-petal rear diffuser, LED strips; sunflower yellow | 330 | 3.0 | 1.85 | 0.80 (tricky) | 1.10 | V12 hybrid with an electric whine | Expert · garden, 1500 (needs the Finale cleared) |\n1160\t\n1161\t- **Bloom Editions** of the Primavera (400) and the Orchidea (500): +8 km/h, −0.3 s to 100, +0.1 steer, +0.04 drift\n1162\t  grip, petal decals and a gold trim colour.\n1163\t- **Paints.** Each car has 4 base paints. Four packs come from the garden (100 each): Pastel, Metallic, Retro and Neon,\n1164\t  4 paints each.\n1165\t- **Recolouring.** Paint is a 5-tone ramp swapped with `E.recolor` on the pre-rendered frames; the art uses a key ramp\n1166\t  (§9.3).\n1167\t- **Unlock flow.** A car's garden bed only appears once its mission reward has \"delivered the seed packet\": Gardenia\n1168\t  after B2, Magnolia after V2, Girasole after the Finale. Until then the bed is locked with that text.\n1169\t\n1170\t### 8.5 The cast\n1171\t\n1172\t| Who | Role | Look (Humanoid options) | Personality, and the requests she loves |\n1173\t|---|---|---|---|\n1174\t| **Leo** / **Lea** | you, the driver | `build: 'heroic'`, short quiff or ponytail, aviator shades (a face sprite), open-collar shirt; 3 palettes | silent; reacts with body language |\n1175\t| **Marisol Fontaine** | passenger, florist, giver | long wavy auburn hair, a sunflower clip, a sundress (`outfit: 'robe'`, short sleeves), heart sunglasses | sunny, playful, giggly; loves **balloons, gnomes, bouquets, arches** |\n1176\t| **Vivienne \"Vivi\" Montclair** | passenger, socialite, giver | sleek black bob, a silk scarf (ribbon), a cream blazer (`outfit: 'coat'`), pearl trim | dramatic, demanding, secretly soft; loves **clean, speed, photo, pass** |\n1177\t| **Juniper \"Juni\" Park** | passenger, stunt photographer, giver | short spiky pink hair, cap backwards (`hat: 'cap'`), cargo jacket, a camera strap | thrill-seeking, fast-talking; loves **close, drift, ribbons, draft** |\n1178\t| **Bruno Brezza** | the starter, mentor, giver | `build: 'bulky'`, a big grey moustache, panama hat, floral shirt, the sunflower flag | booming, warm, nostalgic: \"In my day…\" |\n1179\t| **Kiki Rapido** | rival, the drift queen | short green hair, racing gloves; drives a lime Gardenia | cheeky; taunts with a wink |\n1180\t| **Bram and Basil Bramble** | rivals, brothers who love the draft | twin beards, overalls; drive a hot-rod pickup | bicker with each other |\n1181\t| **Lance and Lacey Lustre** | the rival couple | matching gold outfits, sunglasses at night; drive a gold Magnolia | smug glamour |\n1182\t| **Old Ottavio** | rival, a Knockout surprise | white hair, beret; drives a burgundy Primavera | sweet grandpa, late braker |\n1183\t| **Locals** | ending extras | a lantern-seller, the wedding party, a valet, an astronomer, the lighthouse keeper | one line each |\n1184\t\n1185\t**Outfits** (garden, 150 each): Marisol \"Picnic Gingham\", Vivi \"Gala Gown\" (with sequins), Juni \"Rally Suit\".\n1186\t\n1187\t### 8.6 Traffic and the roadside\n1188\t\n1189\t**Traffic types** (`OB.TRAFFIC`). Half-width is in half-roads.\n1190\t\n1191\t| Id | Vehicle | km/h | Half-width | Notes |\n1192\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n1193\t| `hatch` | small hatchback | 95–120 | 0.18 | 5 paints |\n1194\t| `sedan` | family sedan | 100–130 | 0.20 | — |\n1195\t| `taxi` | city taxi | 90–115 | 0.20 | roof light |\n1196\t| `van` | delivery van | 85–110 | 0.22 | flower decals |\n1197\t| `pickup` | pickup | 90–115 | 0.21 | crates in the bed |\n1198\t| `camper` | camper van | 70–95 | 0.23 | surfboard on top |\n1199\t| `bus` | tour bus | 70–90 | 0.27 | long: 2 lengths |\n1200\t| `truck` | flower lorry | 65–85 | 0.30 | potted plants; 2 lengths |\n1201\t| `tractor` | tractor | 35–50 | 0.24 | farm stages only; blinking orange light |\n1202\t| `scooter` | scooter | 80–100 | 0.10 | rider rig |\n1203\t| `icecream` | ice-cream truck | 60–75 | 0.24 | plays its jingle within 3,000 units |\n1204\t\n1205\tDensities by stage come from §8.2's mix weights × the base density 0.32 (vehicles per 10,000 units per lane). Hard\n1206\tstages add 0.05.\n1207\t\n1208\t**Roadside sets per kit.** These are sprites. Core props are `E.prop(name)`, store and own shapes are `E.shapeSprite`,\n1209\tand ★ marks what we draw new.\n1210\t\n1211\t| Kit | Ground and strips | Scenery |\n1212\t|---|---|---|\n1213\t| beach | sand and water | palm, ★umbrella, ★beach hut, ★lifeguard tower, ★surf rack, rock, ★buoy, ★Ferris-wheel pier |\n1214\t| fields | `rows` in lavender or sunflower colours | ★cypress, bush, ★farmhouse, ★beehives, ★silo, ★barn, ★wind turbine (animated), ★hay bale, fence |\n1215\t| cliffs | cliff edge and rail | rock (store `kenney-nature` cliff pieces), ★lemon tree, ★terraced house cluster, ★chapel, sign |\n1216\t| lake | water and pines | pine, ★boathouse, ★paddle steamer (moving), ★truss bridge (strip and spans), ★waterfall (animated) |\n1217\t| canyon | rock and sand | ★mesa pillar, ★rock arch (span), cactus in flower (★ a variant of the core cactus), rock, ★souvenir stand |\n1218\t| alpine | snow and grass | ★cherry tree (blossom, petals), pine, ★chalet, ★cow, ★snow shed (span), rock |\n1219\t| town | cobble and facades | ★arcade facade (strip), awning, ★market stall, lantern, streetlamp, ★fountain, ★cathedral dome (landmark), banners (bunting span) |\n1220\t| vineyard | `rows` of grapes | ★cypress, ★villa, barrel, ★castle on a hill (landmark), ★wine cart, fence |\n1221\t| lagoon | water both sides, low rails | ★flamingo (flock animation), ★salt pyramid, ★lift bridge, lamp |\n1222\t| jungle | grass and wet | palm, ★giant leaf, ★fern, ★ruin arch (span), ★orchid (emissive), ★waterfall, ★parrot |\n1223\t| night city | facades and a neon strip | ★neon sign set (6 messages), ★yacht, ★casino flower (landmark), streetlamp (lit), ★funicular |\n1224\t| night desert | sand | cactus, ★night-bloom cactus (emissive), ★motel sign, ★radio dish, rock |\n1225\t| highlands | heather and stone walls | ★standing stone, ★castle ruin, rock, bush (heather tint), ★sheep |\n1226\t\n1227\t**Request objects** (★ all): heart balloon (3 colours), gnome (googly eyes), bee swarm (Blobs with `wings: true`), ribbon\n1228\t(a pink strip between 2 cars), cone, flower arch, bouquet, photo box (asphalt paint) with a photographer rig, gate\n1229\tflowers in 5 colours (Memory Lane).\n1230\t\n1231\t**Signs** (★): the fork sign (two names, arrows, flowers), CHECKPOINT and GOAL arches, ROAD CLOSED barriers, the\n1232\twind-sock warning, the stage name boards, and billboards with original fake ads: \"Gelato Gelatissimo\", \"Fioretti — Born\n1233\tto Bloom\", \"Radio Petal FM\", \"Hotel Vespera\". Billboards are written in the pixel font.\n1234\t\n1235\t### 8.7 Love in Bloom requests (all 14)\n1236\t\n1237\tThresholds are scaled by the `requestDifficulty` tuning. A miss gives 0 petals and a mood drop.\n1238\t\n1239\t| Type | Call-out (card) | Setup in the window | Measure | Perfect / Great / Good |\n1240\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n1241\t| `pass` | PASS {n} CARS! | traffic ×1.6; n is 6–10 | clean overtakes | ≥ n / ≥ .75n / ≥ .5n |\n1242\t| `drift` | DRIFT FOR ME! | the window covers the stage's twistiest part | drift score against target T (2 long drifts) | ≥ T / ≥ .7T / ≥ .4T |\n1243\t| `ribbons` | CUT THE RIBBONS! | k = 4–6 ribbon pairs | snapped / k | 100 % / ≥ 70 % / ≥ 40 % |\n1244\t| `balloons` | POP THE BALLOONS! | 10–16 balloons at car height in lines and zigzags | popped / total | ≥ 90 % / ≥ 70 % / ≥ 45 % |\n1245\t| `gnomes` | BONK THE GNOMES! | 8–12 gnomes standing on the road | bonked / total | ≥ 90 % / ≥ 70 % / ≥ 45 % |\n1246\t| `bees` | DODGE THE BEES! | 6–10 swarms crossing lanes on sine paths | hits (a hit stings: a wobble and a mood drop) | 0 / 1 / 2 |\n1247\t| `cones` | SLALOM THE CONES! | 8–12 cones on a line | cones passed on the correct alternating side | ≥ 90 % / ≥ 70 % / ≥ 45 % |\n1248\t| `arches` | THROUGH THE ARCHES! | 6–8 one-lane flower arches that step between lanes | passed / total | 100 % / ≥ 75 % / ≥ 50 % |\n1249\t| `speed` | KEEP IT OVER {v}! | v is 230–260 (in the player's units) | share of the window above v | ≥ 95 % / ≥ 80 % / ≥ 60 % |\n1250\t| `clean` | NOT A SCRATCH! | a window of about 15 s with dense traffic | contacts (bumps, crashes, scrapes) | 0 / — / 1 |\n1251\t| `draft` | STAY IN THE DRAFT! | traffic queues to draft behind | seconds of slipstream above 0.5 | ≥ 6 / ≥ 4 / ≥ 2 |\n1252\t| `close` | CLOSE CALLS! | denser traffic | near misses | ≥ n / ≥ .7n / ≥ .4n (n is 5–6) |\n1253\t| `bouquets` | GRAB THE BOUQUETS! | 10–14 bouquets, some on the inside of bends and beside traffic | collected / total | ≥ 90 % / ≥ 70 % / ≥ 45 % |\n1254\t| `photo` | STOP FOR A PHOTO! | a painted box, 2 segments long, at a landmark, with a photographer | **Perfect:** below 50 km/h with the car wholly inside the box and under 3.0 s lost. **Great:** stopped in the box. **Good:** through the box below 100 km/h. | — |\n1255\t\n1256\tThe photo stop costs clock time, a deliberate risk against the reward. Juni and Vivi say a different line for it.\n1257\t\n1258\t### 8.8 The Coast 2 Coast Tour missions (14)\n1259\t\n1260\t| # | Giver · title | Route | Rules (`OB.Rules` kind) | C / B / A / AAA | Seeds (base) and unlock |\n1261\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n1262\t| B1 | Bruno · **First Light Sprint** | S1 to its checkpoint | `sprint`: reach it with the clock from 52 s | 0 s / 5 s / 9 s / 13 s left | 150 |\n1263\t| B2 | Bruno · **Drift Duel** | S2 | `driftduel` against Kiki: more drift points by the stage's end. Live points bar for both. | win / +10 % / +25 % / +45 % margin | 200 · the Gardenia bed |\n1264\t| B3 | Bruno · **Draft Dash** | S4 | `draftdash` against the Bramble Brothers: slipstream points (seconds × speed) plus finishing first | win / +10 % / +25 % / +40 % | 250 |\n1265\t| B4 | Bruno · **Last One Wilts** | S5 → S8 → S12, then Goal B | `knockout`: 5 racers (you, Kiki, Brambles, Lustres, Ottavio). The last at each checkpoint is out; finish first. | top 2 / 1st / 1st +2 s / 1st +5 s | 300 · the \"Lemon Drop Rush\" bed |\n1266\t| M1 | Marisol · **Balloon Bonanza** | S1 | `collect` balloons: 40 spread over the stage | 20 / 26 / 32 / 38 | 150 |\n1267\t| M2 | Marisol · **Gnome Patrol** | S9 | `collect` gnomes (24) with bees: a bee hit costs 2 | 10 / 14 / 18 / 22 net | 200 · the Picnic Gingham outfit |\n1268\t| M3 | Marisol · **Memory Lane** | S2 | `memory`: she shows a 5-colour flower sequence (2 s per colour), then 5 gate sets with 3 coloured gates each; drive through the right colour | 3 / 4 / 5 / 5 with no hints shown | 250 · the \"Moonflower\" bed |\n1269\t| V1 | Vivi · **Not a Scratch** | S8 | `clean`: zero contacts, ranked by time | under par +12 s / +6 s / +2 s / under par | 200 |\n1270\t| V2 | Vivi · **Fashionably Fast** | S10 | `speedkeep`: above 240 km/h for the stage, in crosswinds | 70 % / 80 % / 88 % / 95 % | 250 · the Magnolia bed |\n1271\t| V3 | Vivi · **Heartbreakers** | S11 → Goal A | `heartbreak` against Lance and Lacey: both couples start with 20 petals. Every 2 s behind them drains 1, every pass steals 2 (both ways), and theirs drain the same. End with more than 0, or make theirs 0. | > 0 / ≥ 10 / ≥ 18 / theirs at 0 | 300 · the Gala Gown outfit |\n1272\t| J1 | Juni · **Close Calls** | S3 | `count` near misses | 8 / 12 / 15 / 20 | 150 |\n1273\t| J2 | Juni · **Ribbon Cutter** | S7 | `count` ribbons: 16 pairs | 8 / 11 / 14 / 16 | 200 · the Rally Suit outfit |\n1274\t| J3 | Juni · **The Perfect Shot** | S6 | `photo3`: 3 photo boxes; the score is the sum of photo ratings | 1 Good each / 2 Great / 3 Great / 3 Perfect | 250 · the Mirror Roads bed |\n1275\t| F | Bruno and all · **Coast 2 Coast** | the full pyramid, forks open | `love`: Love in Bloom rules with all request types, and the passenger you choose | grade B / A / AA / AAA at any goal | 500 · the Girasole bed, the credits |\n1276\t\n1277\tEvery rival mission shows the rival's portrait and a live gap on the HUD.\n1278\t\n1279\t### 8.9 The Garage Garden: beds and costs"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f95c51ac-ca98-4f0d-bbff-431b6595447e","uuid":"6b893673-ca4e-4c5d-9d9e-58de051c176e","timestamp":"2026-11-04T11:32:12.461Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-043153__xe1b7e1/work/PLAN.md","content":"- **Subtitles always show.** Speech bubbles type the exact line at the babble's pace (the planner returns per-syllable\n  times), and the portrait's mouth moves while it types. The voice bus has its own volume (Settings, Sound, Voices).\n- **The planner is pure:** `plan(text, who, mood) → { syllables: [{ t, dur, f0, F1, F2, onset }], duration }`. It is\n  tested headless (counts, durations, determinism) without Web Audio.\n\n### 7.17 UI and UX for 10 feet: screens and the UI kit (`40-ui.js`, `50-front.js`)\n\n- **Sizes.** Design at 480×270, which is 4× at 1080p and 8× at 4K.\n  - **Body text** is never below the 5×7 font at scale 1 (28 px tall at 1080p). Don't use `font: 'tiny'` for anything a\n    player reads.\n  - **Menu items** are scale 2, headings scale 3–4 (`E.font.title`), with an outline or shadow on everything over the\n    world.\n- **Density.**\n  - At most 7 focusable items per screen, and one main column or carousel. No scrolling lists: page them instead.\n  - Every screen has a footer of prompts (\"✕ Select ○ Back\") and a way back (○, Esc, Backspace or a right-click).\n- **Focus.** One thick focus frame per screen: a 2 px outline in a pulsing sun-yellow plus the pop tween.\n  - **Spatial navigation:** the d-pad, stick, arrows or WASD move to the nearest item in that direction, with\n    `input.repeat` for held directions.\n  - **Mouse:** moving over an item focuses it, and a left click activates it. Mouse movement shows the pointer, and it\n    hides after 2 s still in races.\n- **Screens.**\n  - **Title.**\n    - **Logo:** \"OutBloom\" extruded in a pink → orange → gold gradient, a \"2006\" badge, and \"COAST 2 COAST\" on a road\n      stripe.\n    - **Scene:** Sunrise Strand at dawn, the Orchidea parked, petals drifting, the sea glittering with bloom.\n    - **Prompt:** \"PRESS ✕\" or \"PRESS ENTER\" by device, plus the version label (`E.versionLabel()`).\n    - **Music:** \"OutBloom!\".\n    - **Idle:** after 10 s, the attract mode starts.\n  - **Main menu.** A horizontal carousel of six big mode cards, each a small live illustration with a one-line\n    description: ARCADE RUN, LOVE IN BLOOM, COAST 2 COAST TOUR, TIME TRIAL, VERSUS 2P and GARAGE GARDEN. Below it, a row\n    of three: SETTINGS, HOW TO PLAY, ABOUT & CREDITS.\n  - **Garage** (one screen, four rows):\n    - **Car:** a carousel turning the car on a turntable through its yaw frames, with 3 stat bars (Speed, Acceleration,\n      Handling) and a class badge.\n    - **Paint:** swatches.\n    - **Driver:** Leo or Lea.\n    - **Passenger:** Marisol, Vivi or Juni. Her portrait and a one-line bio show, and her favourite requests as icons.\n    - Locked items show a padlock and \"Plant it in the Garage Garden\".\n  - **Radio:** see §7.14.\n  - **How to Play:** six pages (Driving, Drifting, Slipstream & near misses, Forks & checkpoints, Love in Bloom, Tour &\n    Garden). Each page has a live mini-demo (the race renderer in a framed box driven by the bot) and two sentences\n    with glyph prompts.\n  - **About & Credits:** paged (§7.25).\n- **Transitions** use `game.go(…, { fade: 'wipe' })` with a petal overlay. Reduce motion makes them a plain fade.\n- **Engine screens.** The pause menu and the Settings, Controls and Help screens are the engine's standard HTML\n  (`E.Shell`). Theme them with the sunset palette and the E8 sizes, and give the Help screen our keys and sections (§7.19).\n\n### 7.18 The race HUD (`41-hud.js`)\n\nThe HUD anchors to `r.W`/`r.H` per viewport. Each corner holds one cluster, and the road's centre always stays clear.\n\n| Place | Content |\n|---|---|\n| top centre | **TIME** (label scale 1, digits scale 4, a little sun icon). \"+57\" flies in at checkpoints. Red and flashing below 10 s. |\n| top left | **SCORE** at scale 2, and under it the **Bloom chain**: five flower icons filling, \"×3\". |\n| top right | stage name and stage time (scale 1), and under them the **mini-map**: 15 dots in the pyramid with your path lit and a pulsing dot for you. In a hub session, the standings strip. |\n| bottom right | **speed** digits at scale 3 with the unit (km/h or mph), a tachometer arc (green zone, red line), the gear (\"A3\" or \"M4\") |\n| bottom left | Love in Bloom only: the passenger portrait (40 px), mood face, petal ring and count, and above it the **request card** (icon, call-out, progress, window bar) |\n| centre (brief) | banners: request call-outs, CHECKPOINT, TIME EXTENDED!, FORK arrows, SLIPSTREAM!, NEAR MISS +500, FULL BLOOM!, PERFECT START, GOAL! (scale 3, outlined, at most 2.5 s, never two at once: they queue) |\n| near the car | the passenger's speech bubble (pixel font, scale 1, at most 2 lines of 34 characters), above the car's right seat |\n\n**Hints.** While the Hints setting is on, they appear at most once each per profile, with glyphs: \"Hold R2 to\naccelerate\", \"Tap ○ while steering to drift\", \"Tuck in behind a car to slipstream\", \"Steer left or right at the fork\",\nand \"Rev in the green at GO\". Rebinding updates them.\n\n### 7.19 Input and prompts (`00-core.js`, `40-ui.js`; Appendix B)\n\n- **The preset.** `OB.INPUT` keeps the engine's menu actions (`up down left right start pause confirm cancel`) and adds\n  `gas`, `brake`, `drift`, `shiftUp`, `shiftDown`, `horn`, `camera` and `radio`. Throttle and brake read `input.analog`\n  (E3).\n- **Controls screen.** It names every action and lets the player rebind them (the engine's Controls screen).\n- **Prompts.** `OB.UI.prompt(g, x, y, action, text, { scale })` draws the glyph for `input.lastDevice`:\n  - **Controller:** PlayStation by default. The icons are drawn as sprites: ✕ blue, ○ red, □ pink, △ green; L1, R1, L2\n    and R2 as pills; Options and Create as small ovals; the d-pad and sticks. Settings, Controller, Button symbols:\n    **Xbox** switches to A (green), B (red), X (blue), Y (yellow), LB, RB, LT, RT and Menu/View.\n  - **Keyboard:** keycaps showing the bound key (W, ↑, Space, Esc…).\n  - **Mouse:** left and right button and wheel icons.\n  - **Labels** come from `input.label(action)` after rebinding. We map them to icons (the font has no ○ □ △).\n- **Live switching.** Every prompt (HUD hints, menu footers, the hub card, How to Play) follows the device used last,\n  from the next frame.\n- **Controller hot-plug.** The engine pauses a race when the pad in use is unplugged. Its banner names the player.\n\n### 7.20 Settings (ours, beside the engine's standard ones)\n\nGroup \"Game\" (with `E.Shell` settings items):\n\n| Setting | Options | Default |\n|---|---|---|\n| Transmission | Automatic / Manual | Automatic |\n| Drift input | All / Brake tap / Drift button | All |\n| Steering assist | Off / Low / High | Low |\n| Difficulty | Easy / Normal / Hard | Normal |\n| Speed units | km/h / mph | km/h |\n| Camera | Chase / High / Bumper | Chase |\n| Passenger chatter | Full / Less / Off | Full |\n| HUD size | Large / Extra large (×1.25 scale steps) | Large |\n| Hints | On / Off | On |\n\nUnder Sound: **Voices** (0–100 %). Under Controller: button symbols PlayStation or Xbox (E5). The engine's reduce\nmotion, shake and flash settings cut the petals and speed lines, shake and flashes (bloom stays). Settings are saved per\ngame (`id: 'outbloom2006'`).\n\n### 7.21 Touch-only devices (`71-touch.js`)\n\n- **Detection.** A device is touch-only when it has touch (`navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0` or `ontouchstart`), has no\n  fine pointer (`!matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches`), no gamepad is connected, and no `keydown` has been seen.\n  It is re-checked on `gamepadconnected` and on any `keydown`; either one switches to normal play at once.\n- **Behaviour.**\n  - Pass `touch: []` on every scene and set `input.touchEnabled = false`, so the engine shows no touch buttons.\n  - The title shows a large panel: **\"Controller or keyboard required\"** with a pad and a keyboard icon, plus a big\n    **SETTINGS** button (a tap opens `game.menu('settings')`). The engine's corner menu button stays.\n  - **The attract mode runs anyway.** It starts after 10 s as usual. Taps don't count as \"input\" for leaving it, since\n    they can't play. A tap only flashes the notice, except a tap on SETTINGS. The notice stays over the demo, dimmed.\n- **Test:** `tools/check.mjs --size 390x844@3` (an emulated phone) shows the notice and no touch buttons, and the\n  attract scene starts.\n\n### 7.22 Attract mode (`60-attract.js`)\n\n- **The idle clock.** `OB.UI` tracks the last input: any action press, a stick past the dead zone, a mouse move over\n  4 px, a wheel or a click. On the **title, menu, garage, radio, tour, garden, trial and versus** screens, 10 s with\n  none starts `attract`. The engine's HTML screens (settings, controls) don't count.\n- **The playlist.** Each cycle is about 95 s in five segments, and each cycle picks different stages, rotating through\n  all 15 on a seeded order:\n  1. **Arcade:** the start line, Bruno's flag, the countdown, a perfect start, drifting the first bends, slipstreaming a\n     bus (25 s).\n  2. **A fork:** the signs, the bot picking a branch, the sky dissolving, the checkpoint and its stage card (15 s).\n  3. **Love in Bloom:** a request (balloons, gnomes or ribbons, rotating), the passenger's call-out, a Perfect, petals\n     (20 s).\n  4. **Tour:** a Drift Duel moment against Kiki, or the Versus split screen with two bots, alternating each cycle\n     (20 s).\n  5. **A goal ending:** 10 s of a vignette, then the arcade table with initials and \"PRESS START\" over the title logo\n     (5 s).\n- **Cheats on:** `noCollide`, `infiniteTime`, `perfectRequests` and `autoDrift`. The bot (`OB.Brain`) drives.\n- **On screen:** a \"DEMO PLAY\" tag and a blinking \"PRESS ✕ / PRESS ENTER\" (by the last device) over every segment. The\n  game's music plays, with sound effects at 60 %.\n- **Leaving.** Any input (as defined above) ends the attract mode at once and goes back to the screen and focus it came\n  from. The input that woke it is consumed and does nothing else.\n- **Never in a hub session.** It never touches the profile: there is no saving, and no records or seeds.\n\n### 7.23 Dev mode (`61-dev.js`)\n\n- **How to reach it.** From the pause menu's **Dev Mode** item (outside hub sessions), or with F9 while paused. The\n  panel opens over the frozen race (`timeScale = 0` while open), and Back returns to the pause menu.\n- **Looks.** It is a canvas panel in the UI kit, large and pad-navigable (L1/R1 or Q/E switch tabs, the mouse drags\n  sliders).\n- **Tabs:**\n  1. **Tuning:** a live slider for every Appendix A value, in groups (Car, Drift, Slipstream, Traffic, Clock, Rivals,\n     Love in Bloom, Camera, World). Each row shows its value, a reset-this-row button and a ● when it differs from the\n     default. **RESET ALL TO DEFAULTS** sits at the top. Changes apply on the next step. With \"Keep after reload\" on,\n     values are saved to `E.store('outbloom:dev')`.\n  2. **Cheats:** infinite time, no collisions, perfect requests, auto drive (the bot drives you), auto drift, freeze\n     traffic, no traffic, invincible mood, show hitboxes, show the debug overlay (stage, segment, z, x, v, rpm, gear,\n     curve, drift a and score, slip, Bloom points, request state, traffic count, frames per second), hide the HUD,\n     slow motion ×0.25 and fast ×2.\n  3. **Warp:** jump to any of the 15 stages at 0/25/50/75/95 %, the next checkpoint or the fork, a forced fork side,\n     play any goal ending at any tier, trigger any request type now, spawn a traffic car or ribbon pair ahead, spawn a\n     rival, set the time of day and weather, play the attract demo, play the credits, start any mission.\n  4. **Audio:** a jukebox (every song with its layers toggled), every sound effect, every character's voice with a\n     sample line in each mood, and the reverb amount.\n  5. **Save:** give 1,000 seeds, unlock everything, clear the career, set every mission's rank, reset the profile (asks\n     first, in the panel), and show the profile as JSON in a read-only, paged view.\n  6. **Info:** version, seed, route, last 20 bus events, error count.\n\n### 7.24 Saves and progression data (`26-progress.js`)\n\nOne profile in `E.store('outbloom:profile')`, versioned with `v: 1` and a `migrate(from)` table. It is written after\neach run, mission, purchase and settings change, never on page close. Where storage is blocked, the game plays on for\nthe visit.\n\n```js\n{ v: 1, seeds: 0, seenIntro: false,\n  unlocked: { cars: ['primavera', 'orchidea'], editions: [], paints: ['base'], songs: [0,1,2,3,4], outfits: { marisol: [0], vivi: [0], juni: [0] }, mirror: false },\n  last: { car: 'orchidea', paint: 0, driver: 'leo', passenger: 'marisol', station: 0, transmission: 'auto', camera: 'chase' },\n  career: { M1: { cleared: true, rank: 'A', best: 41250 }, … },\n  goals: { A: { bloom: true, bud: false, wilt: false }, … },\n  records: { stage: { S1: { ms, ghost } }, route: { 'LRRL': { score, ms } }, medals: { S1: 'gold' } },\n  hints: { drift: true, … } }\n```\n\nThe arcade tables use `E.Arcade` (ids `'outbloom-arcade'` and `'outbloom-love'`).\n\n### 7.25 About and Credits\n\nThe About & Credits screen has big pages, turned with ◀ ▶ (or a click on the arrows), one heading and at most about 8\nshort lines each:\n\n1. **Credits:** the game; built on the my-3D2dge engine (MIT); AI Hub session protocol; the asset-library credits for\n   any store set used (with each set's `credit` line); \"Inspired by OutRun 2006: Coast 2 Coast (SEGA / Sumo Digital,\n   2006). An original homage. Not affiliated.\"\n2. **Source Information**, the 14 fields of `TASK.md` in its words, with \"unknown\" where it can't be confirmed:\n   Date, Source, Plan (`PLAN.md`), Genre and Perspective, Tech, Art Direction, Animation, Audio, Online, Testing,\n   Research, Environment, Model and Usage.\n3. **Testing Feedback** (4 answers). 4. **Engine Feedback** (6 answers). 5. **Prompt Feedback** (4 answers).\n\nThese pages read from one data object, `OB.ABOUT`, which `build.mjs` also writes into `exit-interview.json`. That keeps\nthe in-game text and the JSON identical. Fill in the real answers at the end of the build (§10, M9).\n\n---\n\n## 8. Content\n\n### 8.1 Bellafiore and the story\n\n- **The island.** Bellafiore is a sun-soaked island country shaped like a leaf, with an eastern **Alba Coast**\n  (sunrise) and a western **Vespera Coast** (sunset). Between them lie lavender ridges, lemon cliffs, sunflower\n  prairies, a mountain lake, a red canyon, alpine passes in cherry blossom, an old walled town, wine country, a pink\n  lagoon, a jungle, a neon riviera, a starry desert and the windy highlands.\n- **The festival.** Every spring, the whole island blooms in a single week, the *Settimana dei Fiori*.\n- **The premise.** It is the first morning of the festival. You (Leo or Lea), a young driver with a borrowed Fioretti\n  and big dreams, promised your passenger the perfect day: \"coast to coast before the sun goes down\". Bruno Brezza, a\n  rally legend who now runs the island's festival tour, waves you off from the Sunrise Strand.\n- **Story beats** (lines in §8.11):\n\n| Beat | Mode | What happens |\n|---|---|---|\n| Dawn start | Arcade, Love | Bruno: \"Morning, champ! The west coast is waiting, and so is the sunset!\" The passenger answers with her own opener. |\n| Each fork | Love | the passenger's preference, with a reason in character |\n| Each checkpoint | all | a line about the next region (\"Lavender! Smell that?\") |\n| Time running low | all | the passenger worries in character |\n| Goal | all | the ending vignette for that goal and mood (§8.3) |\n| Tour opening | Tour | Bruno's garage. He hands you the Tour's postcard book. |\n| Giver intros and outros | Tour | 3 lines each, opening and closing every giver's arc |\n| Finale | Tour | the group photo at the goal, Bruno's toast (\"To the road, and to whoever's beside you on it!\"), then the credits |\n\n### 8.2 The stages (all 15)\n\nShared rules for every stage:\n- 820–980 segments long, with a junction or goal run-in after that.\n- The first 150 segments are flat.\n- Two or three request slots (in Love in Bloom), never inside the first 120 segments or the junction.\n- 4–8 scenery rules and at least 2 landmarks.\n- A target time of 52–64 s for the reference bot with the Orchidea on Normal. The test checks every stage falls in\n  48–70 s.\n\nCurve values run from −6 to 6 (+ bends right). Hills are in units over the section.\n\n| ID | Name (row/col) | Kit · time of day | Lanes · difficulty | Layout beats (in order) | Landmarks and set pieces | Traffic mix | Love in Bloom slots |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| S1 | **Sunrise Strand** (1/1) | beach · dawn | 3 · ●○○○○ | the start grid; a long straight; 3 gentle S-bends (curves of 2–3); a palm avenue whose fronds meet overhead (a span); a seawall bend (4); a rise and a dip to the junction | the pier with its Ferris wheel (left, mid-stage); pastel beach huts; lifeguard towers; the start gantry with Bruno | hatch 3, van 1, icecream 0.4, bus 0.6 | balloons (12) at 18 %; pass (8) at 62 % |\n| S2 | **Lavender Ridge** (2/1) | fields (lavender) · morning | 3 · ●○○○○ | 6 rolling crests (hills of ±1500, small air on 2); long sweepers (2–3); one 4 with a farmhouse on the outside | hot-air balloons in the sky; a stone farmhouse with a water tower; beehive rows (the bees' home) | hatch 2, van 2, pickup 1, camper 1 | bees (8) at 25 %; drift (target 2 long drifts) at 65 % |\n| S3 | **Lemon Cliffs** (2/2) | cliffs · morning | 2 · ●●●○○ | the cliff wall on the right (edge `cliff`), a rail and the sea drop on the left; tunnel 1 (60 segments); bends of 4–5; a gallery tunnel open to the sea (light stripes); a 6 hairpin before the junction | a terraced pastel village across the bay; lemon groves; a cliff chapel | scooter 2, hatch 2, van 1, bus 0.5 | cones (10) at 20 %; close (5) at 60 % |\n| S4 | **Sunflower Prairie** (3/1) | fields (sunflower) · late morning | 4 · ●○○○○ | very flat; two 30-second straights; wide sweepers (1–2); a level crossing at 50 % with a train in the backdrop | red barns and silos; wind turbines with turning blades; a giant hay-bale sculpture of a sunflower | tractor 1.5, pickup 2, sedan 2, truck 1 | draft (6 s) at 20 %; speed (≥ 250) at 60 % |\n| S5 | **Lake Serenata** (3/2) | lake · noon | 3 · ●●○○○ | lake water on the left, pines on the right; a long timber truss bridge (rails both sides, shadow stripes); a waterfall bend (4); a sun-shower event from 55 to 75 % (wet shine, spray, a rainbow in the sky) | a paddle steamer moving on the lake; boathouses; the waterfall | sedan 2, camper 2, van 1, bus 0.5 | arches (7) at 22 %; bouquets (12) at 66 % |\n| S6 | **Rosa Canyon** (3/3) | canyon · noon | 2 · ●●●●○ | big dips and crests (±4000, air ×3); bends of 5–6; 3 rock arches (short tunnels); heat shimmer | mesas; a natural rock arch over the road; flowering cacti; a gnome souvenir stand | pickup 2, truck 1, camper 1 | gnomes (10) at 30 %; drift at 70 % |\n| S7 | **Cherry Pass** (4/1) | alpine · afternoon | 3 · ●●○○○ | a climb of +6000 overall; 3 hairpin sequences (6, then 5); 2 snow sheds; a petal storm from 40 to 70 %; the descent to the junction | snowy peaks; chalets; cherry trees in bloom; cows in meadows | sedan 2, camper 1, bus 1, van 1 | drift at 25 %; clean at 60 % |\n| S8 | **Old Town Fiorenza** (4/2) | town · afternoon | 3 (2 in the gate) · ●●●○○ | cobbles; facades on both sides; bunting spans; a city-gate tunnel; four sharp corners (6, short); a piazza where the road widens to 4 around a fountain sweep | the cathedral dome (photo spot); a flower market; café awnings; a fountain | taxi 2, scooter 3, van 1, bus 1 | photo (cathedral) at 35 %; pass (10) at 70 % |\n| S9 | **Vineyard Way** (4/3) | vineyard · late afternoon | 3 · ●●●○○ | rolling hills (±2000); a cypress avenue (dense runs both sides); sweepers (3–4); one 5 below the castle | a hilltop castle; villas; grape rows (ground `rows`); barrel stacks | tractor 1, pickup 2, van 1, sedan 1 | gnomes (10) at 20 %; ribbons (5) at 62 % |\n| S10 | **Seafoam Causeway** (4/4) | lagoon · late afternoon | 2 · ●●●●● | water on both sides, behind low rails; long and fast; 5 crosswind gusts; a lift-bridge hump (air); salt pans with pink water | a flamingo flock taking off as you pass; salt pyramids; the lift bridge | sedan 2, truck 2, camper 1 | balloons in the wind (12, drifting sideways) at 25 %; speed (≥ 240) at 65 % |\n| S11 | **Golden Bay** (5/1) | beach · sunset | 3 · ●○○○○ | the sea on the right this time; a promenade with lantern strings (lit); gentle sweepers; the run-in to the goal | sailboats; beach volleyball nets; a lantern-festival stage → **Goal A** | hatch 2, icecream 0.5, bus 1, van 1 | bouquets (12) at 25 %; balloons (14) at 62 % |\n| S12 | **Orchid Jungle** (5/2) | jungle · sunset | 3 · ●●○○○ | canopy spans (light shafts); a wet road; bends of 3–5; a vine bridge with rails; ruin arches | waterfalls; ancient stone ruins; glowing orchids (emissive); parrots | pickup 2, camper 1, van 1 | arches (8) at 22 %; bees (8) at 60 % → **Goal B** |\n| S13 | **Moonlit Riviera** (5/3) | night city · night | 4 · ●●●○○ | a marina promenade; wide sweepers; 2 tunnels under the city with sodium lamps; fireworks over the water from 60 % | the casino with its giant neon flower; yachts with lights; a funicular | taxi 2, sedan 3, bus 1, scooter 2 | close (6) at 30 %; ribbons (6) at 65 % → **Goal C** |\n| S14 | **Starlight Desert** (5/4) | night desert · night | 3 · ●●●●○ | long fast straights; big crests (air ×4); 2 bends of 5 | night-blooming cacti (emissive white); a neon motel; a radio-telescope dish; shooting stars | truck 2, pickup 2, camper 1 | draft (6 s) at 25 %; cones (12) at 60 % → **Goal D** |\n| S15 | **Aurora Highlands** (5/5) | highlands · twilight | 2 · ●●●●● | stone walls on both edges; blind crests; bends of 5–6 in pairs; a narrow stone bridge | the aurora overhead; standing stones; a castle ruin; heather | sedan 1, camper 1, truck 1, pickup 1 | drift at 20 %; clean at 58 % → **Goal E** |\n\n**Forks** (left/right): S1→S2/S3 · S2→S4/S5 · S3→S5/S6 · S4→S7/S8 · S5→S8/S9 · S6→S9/S10 · S7→S11/S12 ·\nS8→S12/S13 · S9→S13/S14 · S10→S14/S15. Each row-5 stage ends at its own goal: S11→A, S12→B, S13→C, S14→D, S15→E.\n\n**S1 in full, the worked example** (`[segments, curve, hill]`):\n\n```\n[150,0,0]  start grid at seg 20 (gantry, Bruno at x = −1.6), flat by rule\n[70,2,0] [40,0,0] [70,-3,0] [60,0,800] [50,3,-800]\n[90,0,0] {span: 'palms', 90 segs}\n[60,-2,0] [50,4,0] {edgeL: 'stonewall', groundL: 'water' (the seawall bend)}\n[80,0,1200] [70,0,-1200] [60,-3,0]\n[40,0,0]   → junction (80) to S2 | S3\n```\n\nThat totals 890 segments plus the junction. Ground: sea on the left (`water` with foam rows) and sand on the right\n(`sand`). Scenery rules: palm (R, offset 0.4–1.6, every 4, jitter 2), umbrella clusters (R, 0.6–2.2, every 9, cluster\n3), beach huts (R, 2.4, every 26), lifeguard tower (R, 1.8, every 60), surfboard racks (R, every 33), buoys on the water\n(L, every 12, 1.5–4.0).\n\n### 8.3 Goals and endings (5 goals × 3 moods)\n\nEach ending is a 10–14 s side-view vignette. It has a backdrop (the goal's own sky), props, the car arriving and\nparking, the couple stepping out, a local character or the crowd, and **3 beats**: an arrival line, the passenger's line\nfor this mood (one per passenger, so 5 × 3 × 3 = 45 lines), and a caption card. Arcade mode picks the mood from the time\nleft: Bloom at 20 s or more, Bud at 5 s or more, Wilt below. Love in Bloom picks it from the grade (§7.7).\n\n| Goal | Place | Bloom | Bud | Wilt |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A | **Lantern Pier**, Porto Lucciola | a thousand lanterns rise; the pier cheers; she kisses your cheek; fireworks | the two of you release one lantern together | the lanterns are already gone, so she sulks: \"You owe me gelato.\" |\n| B | **Petal Falls Chapel** | you crash a wedding by accident, she catches the bouquet and winks: \"Don't get any ideas. Yet.\" | the guests wave you in for cake | soaked by the waterfall spray, you run off laughing |\n| C | **Grand Hotel Vespera** | red carpet, flashbulbs, fireworks over the bay | the valet takes the keys with a bow | the valet won't park \"that dusty thing\", so you eat at the noodle cart and she secretly loves it |\n| D | **Stargazer Observatory** | the dome opens to a meteor shower and you both make wishes | one shooting star | clouds roll in; the astronomer offers cocoa |\n| E | **Finisterra Lighthouse** | the green flash at sunset: \"Best. Day. Ever.\" | the last sliver of sun | the sun is gone and the wind is cold, so you share your jacket |\n\nCaption card: \"GOAL C · GRAND HOTEL VESPERA\". Underneath is the mood title: \"BLOOM: A Star Arrives\" / \"BUD: Valet\nParking\" / \"WILT: Noodles Under the Neon\". Write all 15 titles in that pattern.\n\n### 8.4 The cars: Fioretti, five open-tops\n\n| Id | Name | Look (recipe, §9.3) | Top km/h | 0–100 s | Steer | Drift grip | Weight | Engine | Class · how to get it |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `primavera` | **Primavera 300 Spider** (1962) | round fenders, chrome bumpers, tiny fins, a wood steering wheel; cream | 245 | 5.8 | 2.35 | 0.92 (easy) | 0.85 | 4 cylinders, warm | Novice · start |\n| `orchidea` | **Orchidea Spider** (2004), the default | a modern wedge-soft body, twin roll hoops, round tail lamps; coral red | 272 | 4.5 | 2.20 | 0.85 | 1.00 | V8 | Novice+ · start |\n| `gardenia` | **Gardenia GT Targa** (1987) | a sharp wedge, pop-up lamps, side strakes, a targa bar; pearl white with pink pinstripes | 292 | 4.0 | 2.00 | 0.78 (hard) | 1.05 | flat-12, rasp | Intermediate · garden, 600 |\n| `magnolia` | **Magnolia V12 Barchetta** (1998) | a long bonnet, no roof at all, twin humps; deep blue | 305 | 4.2 | 1.95 | 0.84 | 1.20 | V12, smooth | Intermediate+ · garden, 900 |\n| `girasole` | **Girasole Hyper Spider** (2026) | a low canopy-less hypercar, a sunflower-petal rear diffuser, LED strips; sunflower yellow | 330 | 3.0 | 1.85 | 0.80 (tricky) | 1.10 | V12 hybrid with an electric whine | Expert · garden, 1500 (needs the Finale cleared) |\n\n- **Bloom Editions** of the Primavera (400) and the Orchidea (500): +8 km/h, −0.3 s to 100, +0.1 steer, +0.04 drift\n  grip, petal decals and a gold trim colour.\n- **Paints.** Each car has 4 base paints. Four packs come from the garden (100 each): Pastel, Metallic, Retro and Neon,\n  4 paints each.\n- **Recolouring.** Paint is a 5-tone ramp swapped with `E.recolor` on the pre-rendered frames; the art uses a key ramp\n  (§9.3).\n- **Unlock flow.** A car's garden bed only appears once its mission reward has \"delivered the seed packet\": Gardenia\n  after B2, Magnolia after V2, Girasole after the Finale. Until then the bed is locked with that text.\n\n### 8.5 The cast\n\n| Who | Role | Look (Humanoid options) | Personality, and the requests she loves |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Leo** / **Lea** | you, the driver | `build: 'heroic'`, short quiff or ponytail, aviator shades (a face sprite), open-collar shirt; 3 palettes | silent; reacts with body language |\n| **Marisol Fontaine** | passenger, florist, giver | long wavy auburn hair, a sunflower clip, a sundress (`outfit: 'robe'`, short sleeves), heart sunglasses | sunny, playful, giggly; loves **balloons, gnomes, bouquets, arches** |\n| **Vivienne \"Vivi\" Montclair** | passenger, socialite, giver | sleek black bob, a silk scarf (ribbon), a cream blazer (`outfit: 'coat'`), pearl trim | dramatic, demanding, secretly soft; loves **clean, speed, photo, pass** |\n| **Juniper \"Juni\" Park** | passenger, stunt photographer, giver | short spiky pink hair, cap backwards (`hat: 'cap'`), cargo jacket, a camera strap | thrill-seeking, fast-talking; loves **close, drift, ribbons, draft** |\n| **Bruno Brezza** | the starter, mentor, giver | `build: 'bulky'`, a big grey moustache, panama hat, floral shirt, the sunflower flag | booming, warm, nostalgic: \"In my day…\" |\n| **Kiki Rapido** | rival, the drift queen | short green hair, racing gloves; drives a lime Gardenia | cheeky; taunts with a wink |\n| **Bram and Basil Bramble** | rivals, brothers who love the draft | twin beards, overalls; drive a hot-rod pickup | bicker with each other |\n| **Lance and Lacey Lustre** | the rival couple | matching gold outfits, sunglasses at night; drive a gold Magnolia | smug glamour |\n| **Old Ottavio** | rival, a Knockout surprise | white hair, beret; drives a burgundy Primavera | sweet grandpa, late braker |\n| **Locals** | ending extras | a lantern-seller, the wedding party, a valet, an astronomer, the lighthouse keeper | one line each |\n\n**Outfits** (garden, 150 each): Marisol \"Picnic Gingham\", Vivi \"Gala Gown\" (with sequins), Juni \"Rally Suit\".\n\n### 8.6 Traffic and the roadside\n\n**Traffic types** (`OB.TRAFFIC`). Half-width is in half-roads.\n\n| Id | Vehicle | km/h | Half-width | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `hatch` | small hatchback | 95–120 | 0.18 | 5 paints |\n| `sedan` | family sedan | 100–130 | 0.20 | — |\n| `taxi` | city taxi | 90–115 | 0.20 | roof light |\n| `van` | delivery van | 85–110 | 0.22 | flower decals |\n| `pickup` | pickup | 90–115 | 0.21 | crates in the bed |\n| `camper` | camper van | 70–95 | 0.23 | surfboard on top |\n| `bus` | tour bus | 70–90 | 0.27 | long: 2 lengths |\n| `truck` | flower lorry | 65–85 | 0.30 | potted plants; 2 lengths |\n| `tractor` | tractor | 35–50 | 0.24 | farm stages only; blinking orange light |\n| `scooter` | scooter | 80–100 | 0.10 | rider rig |\n| `icecream` | ice-cream truck | 60–75 | 0.24 | plays its jingle within 3,000 units |\n\nDensities by stage come from §8.2's mix weights × the base density 0.32 (vehicles per 10,000 units per lane). Hard\nstages add 0.05.\n\n**Roadside sets per kit.** These are sprites. Core props are `E.prop(name)`, store and own shapes are `E.shapeSprite`,\nand ★ marks what we draw new.\n\n| Kit | Ground and strips | Scenery |\n|---|---|---|\n| beach | sand and water | palm, ★umbrella, ★beach hut, ★lifeguard tower, ★surf rack, rock, ★buoy, ★Ferris-wheel pier |\n| fields | `rows` in lavender or sunflower colours | ★cypress, bush, ★farmhouse, ★beehives, ★silo, ★barn, ★wind turbine (animated), ★hay bale, fence |\n| cliffs | cliff edge and rail | rock (store `kenney-nature` cliff pieces), ★lemon tree, ★terraced house cluster, ★chapel, sign |\n| lake | water and pines | pine, ★boathouse, ★paddle steamer (moving), ★truss bridge (strip and spans), ★waterfall (animated) |\n| canyon | rock and sand | ★mesa pillar, ★rock arch (span), cactus in flower (★ a variant of the core cactus), rock, ★souvenir stand |\n| alpine | snow and grass | ★cherry tree (blossom, petals), pine, ★chalet, ★cow, ★snow shed (span), rock |\n| town | cobble and facades | ★arcade facade (strip), awning, ★market stall, lantern, streetlamp, ★fountain, ★cathedral dome (landmark), banners (bunting span) |\n| vineyard | `rows` of grapes | ★cypress, ★villa, barrel, ★castle on a hill (landmark), ★wine cart, fence |\n| lagoon | water both sides, low rails | ★flamingo (flock animation), ★salt pyramid, ★lift bridge, lamp |\n| jungle | grass and wet | palm, ★giant leaf, ★fern, ★ruin arch (span), ★orchid (emissive), ★waterfall, ★parrot |\n| night city | facades and a neon strip | ★neon sign set (6 messages), ★yacht, ★casino flower (landmark), streetlamp (lit), ★funicular |\n| night desert | sand | cactus, ★night-bloom cactus (emissive), ★motel sign, ★radio dish, rock |\n| highlands | heather and stone walls | ★standing stone, ★castle ruin, rock, bush (heather tint), ★sheep |\n\n**Request objects** (★ all): heart balloon (3 colours), gnome (googly eyes), bee swarm (Blobs with `wings: true`), ribbon\n(a pink strip between 2 cars), cone, flower arch, bouquet, photo box (asphalt paint) with a photographer rig, gate\nflowers in 5 colours (Memory Lane).\n\n**Signs** (★): the fork sign (two names, arrows, flowers), CHECKPOINT and GOAL arches, ROAD CLOSED barriers, the\nwind-sock warning, the stage name boards, and billboards with original fake ads: \"Gelato Gelatissimo\", \"Fioretti — Born\nto Bloom\", \"Radio Petal FM\", \"Hotel Vespera\". Billboards are written in the pixel font.\n\n### 8.7 Love in Bloom requests (all 14)\n\nThresholds are scaled by the `requestDifficulty` tuning. A miss gives 0 petals and a mood drop.\n\n| Type | Call-out (card) | Setup in the window | Measure | Perfect / Great / Good |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `pass` | PASS {n} CARS! | traffic ×1.6; n is 6–10 | clean overtakes | ≥ n / ≥ .75n / ≥ .5n |\n| `drift` | DRIFT FOR ME! | the window covers the stage's twistiest part | drift score against target T (2 long drifts) | ≥ T / ≥ .7T / ≥ .4T |\n| `ribbons` | CUT THE RIBBONS! | k = 4–6 ribbon pairs | snapped / k | 100 % / ≥ 70 % / ≥ 40 % |\n| `balloons` | POP THE BALLOONS! | 10–16 balloons at car height in lines and zigzags | popped / total | ≥ 90 % / ≥ 70 % / ≥ 45 % |\n| `gnomes` | BONK THE GNOMES! | 8–12 gnomes standing on the road | bonked / total | ≥ 90 % / ≥ 70 % / ≥ 45 % |\n| `bees` | DODGE THE BEES! | 6–10 swarms crossing lanes on sine paths | hits (a hit stings: a wobble and a mood drop) | 0 / 1 / 2 |\n| `cones` | SLALOM THE CONES! | 8–12 cones on a line | cones passed on the correct alternating side | ≥ 90 % / ≥ 70 % / ≥ 45 % |\n| `arches` | THROUGH THE ARCHES! | 6–8 one-lane flower arches that step between lanes | passed / total | 100 % / ≥ 75 % / ≥ 50 % |\n| `speed` | KEEP IT OVER {v}! | v is 230–260 (in the player's units) | share of the window above v | ≥ 95 % / ≥ 80 % / ≥ 60 % |\n| `clean` | NOT A SCRATCH! | a window of about 15 s with dense traffic | contacts (bumps, crashes, scrapes) | 0 / — / 1 |\n| `draft` | STAY IN THE DRAFT! | traffic queues to draft behind | seconds of slipstream above 0.5 | ≥ 6 / ≥ 4 / ≥ 2 |\n| `close` | CLOSE CALLS! | denser traffic | near misses | ≥ n / ≥ .7n / ≥ .4n (n is 5–6) |\n| `bouquets` | GRAB THE BOUQUETS! | 10–14 bouquets, some on the inside of bends and beside traffic | collected / total | ≥ 90 % / ≥ 70 % / ≥ 45 % |\n| `photo` | STOP FOR A PHOTO! | a painted box, 2 segments long, at a landmark, with a photographer | **Perfect:** below 50 km/h with the car wholly inside the box and under 3.0 s lost. **Great:** stopped in the box. **Good:** through the box below 100 km/h. | — |\n\nThe photo stop costs clock time, a deliberate risk against the reward. Juni and Vivi say a different line for it.\n\n### 8.8 The Coast 2 Coast Tour missions (14)\n\n| # | Giver · title | Route | Rules (`OB.Rules` kind) | C / B / A / AAA | Seeds (base) and unlock |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| B1 | Bruno · **First Light Sprint** | S1 to its checkpoint | `sprint`: reach it with the clock from 52 s | 0 s / 5 s / 9 s / 13 s left | 150 |\n| B2 | Bruno · **Drift Duel** | S2 | `driftduel` against Kiki: more drift points by the stage's end. Live points bar for both. | win / +10 % / +25 % / +45 % margin | 200 · the Gardenia bed |\n| B3 | Bruno · **Draft Dash** | S4 | `draftdash` against the Bramble Brothers: slipstream points (seconds × speed) plus finishing first | win / +10 % / +25 % / +40 % | 250 |\n| B4 | Bruno · **Last One Wilts** | S5 → S8 → S12, then Goal B | `knockout`: 5 racers (you, Kiki, Brambles, Lustres, Ottavio). The last at each checkpoint is out; finish first. | top 2 / 1st / 1st +2 s / 1st +5 s | 300 · the \"Lemon Drop Rush\" bed |\n| M1 | Marisol · **Balloon Bonanza** | S1 | `collect` balloons: 40 spread over the stage | 20 / 26 / 32 / 38 | 150 |\n| M2 | Marisol · **Gnome Patrol** | S9 | `collect` gnomes (24) with bees: a bee hit costs 2 | 10 / 14 / 18 / 22 net | 200 · the Picnic Gingham outfit |\n| M3 | Marisol · **Memory Lane** | S2 | `memory`: she shows a 5-colour flower sequence (2 s per colour), then 5 gate sets with 3 coloured gates each; drive through the right colour | 3 / 4 / 5 / 5 with no hints shown | 250 · the \"Moonflower\" bed |\n| V1 | Vivi · **Not a Scratch** | S8 | `clean`: zero contacts, ranked by time | under par +12 s / +6 s / +2 s / under par | 200 |\n| V2 | Vivi · **Fashionably Fast** | S10 | `speedkeep`: above 240 km/h for the stage, in crosswinds | 70 % / 80 % / 88 % / 95 % | 250 · the Magnolia bed |\n| V3 | Vivi · **Heartbreakers** | S11 → Goal A | `heartbreak` against Lance and Lacey: both couples start with 20 petals. Every 2 s behind them drains 1, every pass steals 2 (both ways), and theirs drain the same. End with more than 0, or make theirs 0. | > 0 / ≥ 10 / ≥ 18 / theirs at 0 | 300 · the Gala Gown outfit |\n| J1 | Juni · **Close Calls** | S3 | `count` near misses | 8 / 12 / 15 / 20 | 150 |\n| J2 | Juni · **Ribbon Cutter** | S7 | `count` ribbons: 16 pairs | 8 / 11 / 14 / 16 | 200 · the Rally Suit outfit |\n| J3 | Juni · **The Perfect Shot** | S6 | `photo3`: 3 photo boxes; the score is the sum of photo ratings | 1 Good each / 2 Great / 3 Great / 3 Perfect | 250 · the Mirror Roads bed |\n| F | Bruno and all · **Coast 2 Coast** | the full pyramid, forks open | `love`: Love in Bloom rules with all request types, and the passenger you choose | grade B / A / AA / AAA at any goal | 500 · the Girasole bed, the credits |\n\nEvery rival mission shows the rival's portrait and a live gap on the HUD.\n\n### 8.9 The Garage Garden: beds and costs","numLines":420,"startLine":860,"totalLines":1758}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_019YgKDULaMdWkZowFhHiibM","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghpAoesDDP9CrJ259br9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Ew9z2jkPA7o4NwLb3Pw6Uo","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-043153__xe1b7e1/work/PLAN.md","offset":1279,"limit":480},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":18351,"cache_read_input_tokens":58095,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":18351},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f95c51ac-ca98-4f0d-bbff-431b6595447e","uuid":"06790ac8-b9f3-4287-8c0c-89b091cbd6ae","timestamp":"2026-11-04T11:32:14.790Z","request_id":"req_011CghpAoNkzhY9EGJDqzkNX","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Ew9z2jkPA7o4NwLb3Pw6Uo":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-043153__xe1b7e1/work/PLAN.md","offset":1279,"limit":480}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ew9z2jkPA7o4NwLb3Pw6Uo","type":"tool_result","content":"1279\t### 8.9 The Garage Garden: beds and costs\n1280\t\n1281\t| Bed | Seeds | Bed | Seeds |\n1282\t|---|---|---|---|\n1283\t| Gardenia GT Targa | 600 | Song \"Lemon Drop Rush\" | 300 |\n1284\t| Magnolia V12 Barchetta | 900 | Song \"Moonflower\" | 300 |\n1285\t| Girasole Hyper Spider | 1500 | Paint packs ×4 | 100 each |\n1286\t| Primavera Bloom Edition | 400 | Outfits ×3 | 150 each |\n1287\t| Orchidea Bloom Edition | 500 | Mirror Roads | 800 |\n1288\t\n1289\tThat totals **6,150 seeds**. All 14 missions at A (base 3,350 × 1.5) give 5,025. Arcade runs give about 150–350 each, so\n1290\tthe economy test's \"everything within 14 A-ranks plus 6 runs\" holds.\n1291\t\n1292\t### 8.10 Music (all original, all in code)\n1293\t\n1294\tRadio stations, in dial order:\n1295\t\n1296\t| # | Station | Song | BPM · key | Style and instruments | Progression (per 2 bars) | Hook idea |\n1297\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n1298\t| 1 | 88.6 PETAL FM | **Sunburst Samba** | 128 · D major | Latin fusion: congas, timbales, slap bass, brass stabs, steel-drum lead, e-piano comping | Dmaj7 · Bm7 · Em7 · A7sus4–A7 | the steel drum climbs a 6th and falls by step, with a timbale fill into each chorus |\n1299\t| 2 | 91.2 SUN FM | **Coastline Crush** | 116 · F major | Balearic pop-funk: four on the floor, a chorus-guitar skank, finger bass, a soft pulse lead, pad | Fmaj7 · Em7 · Dm7 · Gm7–C9 | a breezy 3-note pickup into a long held note |\n1300\t| 3 | 94.8 SHORE FM | **Shuffle on the Shore** | 110 shuffle (swing in 12/8 feel, steps 3) · B♭ major | jazz shuffle: organ, walking bass, brushed snare, sax lead | B♭6 · G7 · Cm7 · F7 (turnaround) | call and response between the sax and the organ |\n1301\t| 4 | 99.1 VESPA FM | **Vespa Vendetta** | 152 · A minor | Eurobeat: octave saw bass, supersaw stabs, fast 16th arps, a bright lead | Am · F · G · E7 | the chorus jumps an octave on beat 3 |\n1302\t| 5 | 103.5 PASTEL FM | **Pastel Highway** | 104 · E♭ major | city pop: DX-style e-piano, slap bass, sax or lead, claps on 2 and 4, pad | A♭maj7 · Gm7 · Fm7 · B♭7sus4 | a sax lick answered by e-piano chords |\n1303\t| 6 | 106.3 ZEST FM (unlock) | **Lemon Drop Rush** | 140 · G major | acid jazz into breakbeat: breakbeat drums, a rubbery 303-ish bass (resonant filter env), organ stabs | G7 · C7 · G7 · D7–C7 | an organ riff in 4ths |\n1304\t| 7 | 108.8 MOON FM (unlock) | **Moonflower** | 92 · C♯ minor | a night synth ballad: a pad, marimba arps, a soft lead, a slow kick | C♯m9 · Amaj7 · E · B | a long lead line with a slide |\n1305\t\n1306\tOther music:\n1307\t\n1308\t| Song | Use | Notes |\n1309\t|---|---|---|\n1310\t| **OutBloom!** | title | 120, C major; a brass fanfare into a samba groove |\n1311\t| **Garden Party** | menus, the Tour, the garden | 100 bossa nova; e-piano and nylon pluck |\n1312\t| **Petal Count** | results | 112; a light funk loop |\n1313\t| **Last Light Lullaby** | the credits and Bloom endings | 84 |\n1314\t\n1315\tJingles (played with `sting`):\n1316\t\n1317\t| Jingle | Feel |\n1318\t|---|---|\n1319\t| start fanfare | 2 bars |\n1320\t| checkpoint | a bright V–I |\n1321\t| request start | a rising triplet |\n1322\t| request success | a major arpeggio up |\n1323\t| request fail | the trombone slide |\n1324\t| fork tick | — |\n1325\t| Full Bloom | a sparkle sweep |\n1326\t| goal fanfare | 4 bars |\n1327\t| new record | — |\n1328\t| unlock bloom | — |\n1329\t| game over | \"Sunset Blues\", a slow minor-blues turnaround |\n1330\t\n1331\tEvery song needs its named layers and a `sparkle` layer (§7.14).\n1332\t\n1333\t### 8.11 Writing style and the line bank\n1334\t\n1335\t**Style.**\n1336\t- Short, sunny, cheeky, like a peppy arcade passenger who is half sweetheart, half backseat driver.\n1337\t- Exclamation-heavy but never mean. A touch of cheese and a wink. No profanity.\n1338\t- **Lengths:** call-outs ≤ 20 characters, in caps. Spoken lines ≤ 40 characters, 1–2 per bubble.\n1339\t- Banners are 1–3 words. Ending captions are a postcard sentence.\n1340\t- Each character's voice stays distinct: Marisol bubbly, Vivi theatrical (\"Darling…\"), Juni breathless slang, Bruno\n1341\t  booming with \"champ\".\n1342\t- **Required counts:** each passenger has ≥ 4 variants for each reaction (request intro per type, Perfect, Great, Good,\n1343\t  Miss, bump, crash, near miss, air, fork preference left and right, checkpoint, low time, goal, start). Bruno has ≥ 3\n1344\t  per event.\n1345\t\n1346\t**Seed lines** (use them, then write the rest in the same voice):\n1347\t\n1348\t| Who | Event | Lines |\n1349\t|---|---|---|\n1350\t| Marisol | start | \"Coast to coast? With you? Let's GO!\" |\n1351\t| Marisol | balloons | \"Ooh, balloons! Pop them all for me!\" |\n1352\t| Marisol | Perfect | \"Eee! You're amazing!\" · \"Again, again!\" |\n1353\t| Marisol | Miss | \"Aww… you missed so many.\" |\n1354\t| Marisol | crash | \"Kyaaa! My hair!\" |\n1355\t| Marisol | fork left | \"Left! I want to smell the lavender!\" |\n1356\t| Marisol | low time | \"Hurry, the sun's slipping!\" |\n1357\t| Vivi | start | \"Darling, do try not to dent anything.\" |\n1358\t| Vivi | speed | \"Faster. I refuse to arrive second.\" |\n1359\t| Vivi | Great | \"Hmm. Adequate. Barely.\" |\n1360\t| Vivi | Perfect | \"Now THAT is how one arrives!\" |\n1361\t| Vivi | bump | \"Was that a… car? Unacceptable.\" |\n1362\t| Vivi | fork right | \"Right, the cliffs. Everyone who matters goes that way.\" |\n1363\t| Juni | start | \"Okay okay okay, get me some ACTION!\" |\n1364\t| Juni | close | \"Closer! Closer! I need the shot!\" |\n1365\t| Juni | near miss | \"WHOA, did you see that?!\" |\n1366\t| Juni | Miss | \"Boooring. Give me danger!\" |\n1367\t| Juni | Perfect | \"Best shot of my LIFE!\" |\n1368\t| Juni | air | \"We're FLYING!\" |\n1369\t| Bruno | start | \"Morning, champ! The west coast is waiting!\" |\n1370\t| Bruno | drift | \"Ho ho! You drift like my rally days!\" |\n1371\t| Bruno | mission win | \"Mamma mia, what a finish!\" |\n1372\t| Bruno | mission fail | \"Again, champ! Even I spun out in '79.\" |\n1373\t| Announcer | — | \"CHECKPOINT!\" · \"TIME EXTENDED!\" · \"GOAL!\" · \"FULL BLOOM!\" · \"PERFECT START!\" · \"NEW RECORD!\" |\n1374\t\n1375\tEnding captions (one per goal and mood), for example: \"GOAL A · LANTERN PIER: A thousand lanterns, and one just for\n1376\tyou two.\" · \"GOAL E · FINISTERRA LIGHTHOUSE: The green flash. Best. Day. Ever.\"\n1377\t\n1378\tHelp, briefs and the How to Play text use the same voice, plainly.\n1379\t\n1380\t---\n1381\t\n1382\t## 9. Art direction and asset recipes\n1383\t\n1384\t### 9.1 Rules\n1385\t\n1386\t- **Base style.** The engine's native style: pixel art at 480×270, shaded with `E.tones` (cool shadows, warm lights, a\n1387\t  `hi` sparkle). Everything is drawn with `E.px.*`, `E.font`, sprites and shapes. Never use `ctx.arc`, `stroke`,\n1388\t  `fillText` or canvas gradients on the buffer. Translucency goes through `px.blend` or the dither helpers.\n1389\t- **Fog and distance.** Use the road module's fog (colour per time of day). Far sprites fade to the fog colour at the\n1390\t  module's stepped alpha.\n1391\t- **Palettes.** Every kit has five swatches: `ground`, `ground2`, `accent`, `rock` and `sky` ramps. Time of day applies\n1392\t  a grade on top: dawn peach and lilac, noon clean and saturated, late afternoon gold, sunset magenta to orange, night\n1393\t  indigo with neon.\n1394\t- **Sizes.**\n1395\t  - **The player's car** is about 128 px wide at the screen's bottom (480 wide).\n1396\t  - **Traffic** is drawn from the same model scale.\n1397\t  - **Palms** at the edge reach about 160 px tall near the camera.\n1398\t  - **Rigs in the car:** heads about 12–14 px, with the upper body showing.\n1399\t  - **Ending rigs** are about 70 px tall (size 1.6).\n1400\t\n1401\t### 9.2 Sky presets (`OB.Sky`, in `01-data-world.js`)\n1402\t\n1403\tEach sky is `{ gradient: [5 colours], sun | moon: { x, y, r, color, halo }, clouds: { kind, color, y, speed },\n1404\tlayers: [...] }`.\n1405\t\n1406\t- **Layer kinds:** the engine's `sea`, `hills`, `mountains` (snow), `city` (windows), `clouds` and `fog`, plus our\n1407\t  painter layers: `mesa`, `cliffs`, `village` (terraced houses), `balloons` (drifting), `lanterns` (rising), `aurora`\n1408\t  (animated ribbons), `stars` (with shooting stars), `skyline-neon` (blinking) and `rainbow` (sun shower).\n1409\t- **Count.** About 16 presets in all: one per stage, with the beach kit's dawn and sunset variants.\n1410\t\n1411\t### 9.3 Cars as shapes, pre-rendered\n1412\t\n1413\t- **Writing the shape.** Each car is a set of `E.SHAPES` entries in `src/02-shapes.js` (`ob-car-orchidea-under`, `-over`, `-lights`), written facing south per the asset card\n1414\t  (front +y, so *rear* is −y).\n1415\t  - **Parts:** body side profile as `prism` (outline on x/z, pushed along y), bonnet and boot as `wedge`/`prism`, wheels\n1416\t    as `cyl` rotated onto the x axis, seats as `box`, roll hoops as `lathe`/`cyl`, lamps as `box` in glow materials.\n1417\t  - **Materials:** the slot `paint` uses a key ramp of five tones, so recolouring is a pure palette swap. Also `chrome`\n1418\t    (`steel`), `glass` and `tyre`.\n1419\t- **Three shapes per car:** `…-under` (floor, seats, dash, wheel), `…-over` (doors, rear deck, bumpers, lamps' housings,\n1420\t  wheels) and `…-lights` (tail, brake and head lamps only, glow material).\n1421\t- **Pre-rendering.** At boot, render each with `E.shapeSprite(id, { view: OB.CHASE_VIEW, facing, size })`. The view is\n1422\t  `new E.View('chase', 'Chase', 0, 9)`, a camera looking north. The facing is `−π/2 + yaw`. That gives 33 yaws (−90° to\n1423\t  +90°, 5.625° apart) and 32 spin frames.\n1424\t  - **Check it** with `node engine/tools/asset-sheet.mjs src/02-shapes.js#ob-car-orchidea-over`: the sheet shows every view\n1425\t    and facing, and its lint flags thin parts.\n1426\t- **Seat anchors** per yaw are computed by projecting the seat points through the same view and stored with the frames.\n1427\t- **Traffic.** Ten traffic shapes, rendered at 9 yaws (−40° to +40°) × 2 layers (body and lights).\n1428\t- **Headless tests skip all pre-rendering.** `OB.Art` hands out stand-in frames with the right `w`/`h` and anchors, so\n1429\t  the sim and its tests run in about a second.\n1430\t\n1431\t### 9.4 Scenery and other sprites\n1432\t\n1433\t- **Core props:** `E.prop(name).frames[0]` for palm, tree, pine, bush, flowers, rock, cactus, streetlamp, sign, fence,\n1434\t  lamp, awning, lantern and barrel. Tint them with `color` where the kit needs it.\n1435\t- **Store shapes.** `kenney-nature` gives cliffs, trees and rocks, and `kenney-fantasy-town` houses for villages. Cut\n1436\t  only the ids used into `src/assets-ob.js` with `engine/tools/asset-set.mjs`. Load it with `E.addAssets` and credit\n1437\t  the sets on the About screen.\n1438\t- **New sprites** (★ in §8.6) are either `E.SHAPES` entries rendered at 2–3 facings with `OB.CHASE_VIEW`, or string\n1439\t  sprites (`E.sprite`) for the small things: balloons, bees, cones, bouquets, petals, glyph icons.\n1440\t- **Animated scenery** uses `E.anim`: turbines, waterfalls, flags, lanterns, neon, aurora, flamingos.\n1441\t- **Landmarks** (the Ferris wheel, cathedral, casino flower, castle, lift bridge, radio dish, lighthouse) are shapes or\n1442\t  custom `draw(g, sx, sy, scale)` objects built with `px.poly`/`px.rect` and toned with `E.tones`.\n1443\t\n1444\t### 9.5 UI art\n1445\t\n1446\t- **Logo.** `E.font.title` at scale 5, with `colors: ['#fff4e0', '#ffb3d1', '#ff7a59', '#ffcf4a']`, depth 4 and\n1447\t  `depthColor: '#5a1e4a'`. A sunflower replaces the \"O\", and a white shine sweeps across every 6 s.\n1448\t- **Panels.** `E.ui.box` with `bg: ['#2a1450', '#5a2a6a']` (dusk), a border in `#ffd6a0`, and accents in pink\n1449\t  `#ff5fa2`, sun `#ffcf4a` and sea `#3ac8e0`.\n1450\t- **Glyph icons.** 12×12 sprites, drawn at scale 2 for prompts (Appendix C).\n1451\t- **Postcards and garden beds** are drawn panels with a stamp, a portrait and an icon. Every icon gets a shape or\n1452\t  letter as well as its colour.\n1453\t\n1454\t---\n1455\t\n1456\t## 10. Build order and milestones\n1457\t\n1458\tBuild in this order. Each milestone ends with `node tests/run.mjs` green and a fresh bundle that opens from `file://`.\n1459\tEarlier milestones may use plain art, but never stubs. Anything built must work fully. Polish comes later, and nothing\n1460\tis cut.\n1461\t\n1462\t| Milestone | Build | Done when |\n1463\t|---|---|---|\n1464\t| **M0 Foundations** | the dev folder, the handoff engine copied, Playwright installed; `src/manifest.json`, `index.html`, `build.mjs` with the session client inlined first in `<head>`; `tests/run.mjs` printing only failures and a one-line count; `00-core.js` (bus, rand, tuning), `90-main.js` (game, boot steps, a real title scene with the logo); `game.debug` | the bundle opens with no errors (browser smoke test); the headless boot reaches `title` |\n1465\t| **M1 Road and feel** (the riskiest first) | E1/E2 road junctions and dressing; S1, S2 and S3 with real layouts and first-pass scenery; `CarSim` complete (§7.3); traffic (§7.4); the clock and checkpoints; the HUD core; the Orchidea model pre-rendered (layers and yaws); `OB.Brain` bot; the dev panel's **Tuning** tab and debug overlay | the physics suite passes (curve-hold targets, drift entry and exit, slipstream, bumps, spins, rails); the junction suite shows a seamless switch (≤ 1 px); the bot drives S1→S2 and S1→S3 inside the target times; a filmstrip of a drift through a 5-curve looks right |\n1466\t| **M2 The Arcade loop** | all 15 stages and 5 goals as real data with the kit rules; the route graph; scoring and the Bloom chain; results and initials; first-pass ending vignettes; title → menu → garage → radio → race; the UI kit with glyph prompts; settings (§7.20) with E5; the profile save; the synth extension E6/E7 with 2 radio songs, the engine note and core sound effects; E3 analog input | the 16-route playthrough passes (the bot reaches every goal in time on Normal); UI navigation passes for pad, keys and mouse; the glyph suite passes; content lint passes for the stages |\n1467\t| **M3 Platform features** | AI Hub ghost session with our card (§7.12); attract mode (§7.22); touch-only (§7.21); every Dev Mode tab (§7.23) in the pause menu; local Versus split screen (§7.11); E4, E8 | the fake-hub suite and the **harness in a browser** pass (`__hubCheck.pass`, 2 and 4 players, latency and loss); the attract suite passes; touch emulation shows the notice; the dev suite passes (slider changes behaviour, reset restores it); two Versus bots finish |\n1468\t| **M4 The passenger** | the cast's rigs in the car with layers and anchors; portraits; speech bubbles; the babble voice; Love in Bloom with all 14 request types and their objects; petals, grades, fork preferences; every ending (5 × 3) with all 45 passenger lines | the request suite has a pass and a fail script per type; the grade maths passes; a Love in Bloom playthrough reaches each mood; babble planner tests pass |\n1469\t| **M5 Career and meta** | rival personas; all 14 missions and their rules; the Tour map, briefs and debriefs; the garden and economy; Time Trial with ghosts and medals; records | every mission is cleared by its bot at C or better and AAA is possible with cheats off; the economy suite passes; ghosts survive record → save → load → replay |\n1470\t| **M6 Audio content** | all 7 radio songs, 4 themes, every jingle; the full sound-effect list (§7.15); per-car engine notes; ambience per kit; radio switching; ducking; tunnel reverb | audio lint (levels, clipping, silence) and the spectrum bands pass for every song; every sound-effect name in the data exists |\n1471\t| **M7 Art and effects** | all 13 kits fully dressed; every landmark; all skies; bloom, flare, shimmer and weather (sun shower, petal storm, aurora, fireworks, shooting stars); every car, traffic vehicle, rig and request-object animation (§7.13) | the screenshot review of each stage at 1920×1080 is done and noted in the log; `tools/check.mjs` look notes are acted on; there are no flat empty roadsides |\n1472\t| **M8 UX polish and balance** | transitions; hints; How to Play pages with live demos; About & Credits; comfort settings; HUD size; balance with the sliders and bot metrics (§11) | the readability suite passes (no text below scale 1, ≤ 7 items per screen); the balance windows hold (§11, R2) |\n1473\t| **M9 Release** | the full test pass; the final bundle; `exit-interview.json` (parsed); the About pages and the chat reply with the real Source Information and feedback; `engine/CHANGES.md` complete | everything in §13 is ticked |\n1474\t\n1475\t**Work habits.**\n1476\t- Rerun only the suite that covers what you changed (`node tests/run.mjs --only physics`), and the full command before\n1477\t  ending a milestone.\n1478\t- Write nothing outside the dev folder and the root deliverables (the HTML and `exit-interview.json`).\n1479\t- Note every engine change in `engine/CHANGES.md` as you make it.\n1480\t\n1481\t---\n1482\t\n1483\t## 11. Risks and how to de-risk them\n1484\t\n1485\t| # | Risk | Mitigation |\n1486\t|---|---|---|\n1487\t| R1 | **The fork renderer and the seamless stage switch.** The road module draws one road, so junctions are new code in the hottest path. | Build it first (M1). Keep it simple with the flat-junction rule (§7.1), one shared height profile and per-branch lateral offsets. Unit-test the projection: both branch centres; the road centre and player's screen x before and after the switch within 1 px; the gore cushion pushes to the nearer side. Record a filmstrip of a fork at 250 km/h, and review frames 1–16 for pops. |\n1488\t| R2 | **Drift and handling feel**, the heart of the game, tuned without hands on a pad. | Every constant is a live slider from M1. The physics suite asserts the design targets (§7.3: a 3 holdable flat out, a 4 needing a lift, a 6 drifted at about 85 %). **Balance windows**, asserted by the bot suite: on Normal, the reference bot finishes the all-left route with 15–30 s spare and the all-right route with 2–12 s. A \"sloppy\" bot (1 spin per stage) fails at least half the routes. A \"no-drift\" bot finishes no more than 6 of the 16. |\n1489\t| R3 | **Car sprites from shapes might look crude, or boot slowly.** | Prototype the Orchidea in M1 and check it with `asset-sheet.mjs` and in-race screenshots. If the shape renderer can't give clean curves at 128 px, draw the body with `px.poly` profiles per yaw band (the ROAD kit's method, 33 frames), which is just as complete. If boot exceeds about 8 s, render the cars a race needs behind a short \"Polishing the chrome…\" boot step at race start. |\n1490\t| R4 | **AI Hub conformance**, which is a hard gate. | Integrate in M3 using the ghost example's pattern. `ready()` never waits for input, and the client stays verbatim. Run the official harness in Playwright with 2 and 4 players, `latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`, and the `host-leave` scenario. Re-run it on the final bundle (M9). |\n1491\t| R5 | **Audio quality, judged by builders who can't hear.** | Composition rules (§7.14), named instrument presets, and the offline render lint: in Playwright, `OfflineAudioContext` renders 12 s of each song through the real synth code. It asserts a peak under 0.98, RMS between 0.06 and 0.25, no silence over 1 s (except a designed rest), and band energy order (low < 250 Hz present, 1–4 kHz present). It also writes spectrogram PNGs to `.cache/audio/` to look at. |\n1492\t| R6 | **Content volume** (15 stages, 13 kits, 45 ending lines, 14 missions, 11 songs) causing gaps or placeholders. | Data schemas plus the **content-lint suite**: every stage has its fields, slots, landmarks and par; every goal has 3 moods × 3 passengers; every line key used exists and none is empty; every mission has rules, rewards and a bot; every bed has a cost and a reward source; bundle text has no TODO, placeholder or lorem. M2 lands all stages as real data early. |\n1493\t| R7 | **10-foot readability.** | UI-kit rules: a minimum scale and an item count, enforced in a debug-build assertion that the readability suite runs headless. Screenshots at 1920×1080 and 1280×720 reviewed in M7 and M8. The HUD's Extra-large size is tested too. |\n1494\t| R8 | **Headless speed and determinism.** | No pre-rendering headless. All gameplay randomness comes from seeded streams. `game.stateHash` covers the race state. A proof hash of 1,200 scripted steps run twice must match. |\n1495\t| R9 | **Split-screen layout.** | A per-scene `res` of `[640, 360]`, the rows layout, a HUD scaled for 180-px-tall viewports. Two-bot Versus test and screenshots. |\n1496\t| R10 | **Single-file bundle under `file://`.** | `build.mjs` lint (§4.3). The Playwright suite opens the root HTML by `file://`: no request leaves the page, there are no console errors, and the title is reached. |\n1497\t| R11 | **Touch-only false positives** (touchscreen laptops). | Require no fine pointer plus no keydown and no pad. Any key or pad clears the notice at once. Tested with emulation both ways. |\n1498\t| R12 | **Scope.** | Everything in this plan is in scope, and nothing more. Prefer finishing a system completely before starting the next, in milestone order. |\n1499\t\n1500\t---\n1501\t\n1502\t## 12. Testing\n1503\t\n1504\t### 12.1 The one command\n1505\t\n1506\t`node tests/run.mjs` runs everything from the dev folder:\n1507\t- the headless suites first (in about 10 s), then the browser suites;\n1508\t- `--quick` skips the browser suites, `--only <suite>[,<suite>]` runs only those, and `--update-par` rewrites the\n1509\t  stages' par and medal times from the reference bot.\n1510\t\n1511\t**Output.** One line per failure (`FAIL physics/curve-4-needs-lift: held 1.18 at 96% vtop`), then one summary line:\n1512\t`212 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped (14.2 s)`. It exits with code 1 on any failure and prints nothing else.\n1513\t\n1514\t**The harness** (`tests/lib/boot.mjs`), as `game/test.mjs` does it:\n1515\t- Make a `vm` context with `window` (the context itself), `addEventListener`, `location` and `console` stand-ins.\n1516\t- Load `engine/my-3d2dge.js` and every file in `src/manifest.json`. The headless `E.Game` skips drawing.\n1517\t- Return `{ E, game, OB, I: game.input, snap: () => game.debug.snapshot(), events }`.\n1518\t- **Inputs:** `I.tap`, `I.hold`, `I.setAnalog` (E3), `I.feed` for the mouse. **Stepping:** `game.advance(s)`.\n1519\t- **Fresh state:** each suite boots a fresh context, so suites never share state.\n1520\t\n1521\t### 12.2 Headless suites\n1522\t\n1523\t| Suite | What it proves |\n1524\t|---|---|\n1525\t| `core` | boot reaches `title`; no `game.errors`; the bus delivers in order; tuning reset restores every default; rand streams repeat for a seed |\n1526\t| `road` | every stage builds within its length bounds; the first 150 segments and the junctions are flat; `info()` reports ground, edges and spans as the data says; the scenery is placed the same twice for one seed |\n1527\t| `junction` | branch centres at the decision point; the side chosen equals `sign(x)`; **seamless switch**: before and after it, the road centre's screen x is within 1 px and the player's z continues; gore cushion hits push to the nearer branch; a closed fork on a fixed route blocks that side |\n1528\t| `physics` | per-car top speed and 0–100 within 3 %; brakes; the curve-hold design targets; drift entry by all three inputs and none below 120 km/h; counter-steer straightens; drift score accumulates; the slipstream builds and the slingshot fires; off-road slowdown; rails scrape without spinning; bump against spin by closing speed; crest air and steering cut; gusts push |\n1529\t| `traffic` | the mix and density per stage; the minimum spacing; a two-lane gap always exists; lane changes avoid overlaps; junction branch assignment; ribbon pairs stay paired; overtake and near-miss detection with exact geometry cases; the horn makes the car ahead change lane |\n1530\t| `race` | the clock, checkpoint bonuses, difficulty scaling; time up → `gameover`; goal → `ending` → `results`; score maths; Bloom chain levels, decay and crash halving; the route string is recorded |\n1531\t| `playthrough-arcade` | **the bot drives all 16 routes to all 5 goals** on Normal within the R2 balance windows; no errors; every stage enters and every checkpoint fires once |\n1532\t| `requests` | **for each of the 14 types**, a scripted *pass* run (the bot targets the objects) rates Perfect, and a scripted *fail* run (drive straight in lane 1, or crash on purpose) rates Miss; petals and the favourite ×1.2; fork preference ±; penalties |\n1533\t| `playthrough-love` | Love in Bloom on 3 routes with 3 passengers reaches Bloom, Bud and Wilt (by scripted quality); every ending vignette runs to its end with every line found |\n1534\t| `missions` | **every mission** is cleared by its bot at C or better, and AAA is reachable with the \"perfect\" bot; the failure path works for each; rewards are paid once per rank improvement; unlock chains open the right postcards; the Finale opens at 9 cleared |\n1535\t| `economy` | 14 missions at A plus 6 arcade runs buy every bed; buying with too few seeds is refused; unlocks take effect in the garage and radio |\n1536\t| `trial` | a ghost records, saves, loads and replays to the same positions; splits; medals from par |\n1537\t| `versus` | two players join (pad and keyboard halves); a split-screen race with 2 bots finishes; places; rematch |\n1538\t| `hub` | with a **fake `AIHub`** in the context (`active: true`, `connect`, `ready`, `on`, `time`, `send`, `progress`, `result`, `answer`, `rng`), check: the title is skipped; `connect` uses ghost and `[1, 4]` with a card; `ready()` comes without input; Enter or ✕ on the card answers ready and △ answers more; the start syncs to `time() ≥ 0`; ghost sends happen at 8–12 Hz with `area`; progress comes once a second; the goal reports `result({ time })`; `time-up` reports `result({ score })` once; `end` stops input and sound; quit, restart and dev mode are hidden; Options with Create held doesn't pause. With `connect` resolving `null`, it boots to the title. |\n1539\t| `attract` | 10 s idle on each listed screen starts `attract`; 9.9 s doesn't; any key, pad, mouse move or click returns to the same screen and focus, and that input is consumed; a 100 s run emits fork, drift, request-Perfect, rival-or-versus and goal events; no profile writes |\n1540\t| `touch` | with stand-ins for touch-only media queries: the notice shows, the scenes have no touch buttons, and the attract mode runs; a tap doesn't end the attract mode; a keydown clears the notice |\n1541\t| `ui` | from the title, every screen in §5.3 is reachable with pad, keys and mouse (`I.feed`), and each has a way back; focus never lands on nothing; the readability assertion (text at scale 1 or more, at most 7 focusable items) holds on every screen |\n1542\t| `glyphs` | every action's prompt for keyboard, mouse, PlayStation and Xbox; the default is PlayStation; the setting switches to Xbox; rebinding changes the prompt; the device switch updates the prompts on the next step |\n1543\t| `dev` | the pause-menu Dev Mode item opens the panel; each slider changes its sim effect (for example, top speed changes v after 5 s of full gas); per-row and global reset; cheats work; every warp lands where it says; \"Keep after reload\" saves and restores |\n1544\t| `save` | the profile round-trip; a migration from a v0 fixture; blocked storage is survived; settings persist |\n1545\t| `voice` | the babble planner: syllable counts for fixtures, total duration in range, the same text giving the same plan, moods shifting pitch, punctuation pauses |\n1546\t| `audio-data` | every song parses (notes, chords, accents, slides); every track has an instrument; every song has a `sparkle` layer; every sound-effect and jingle name used exists |\n1547\t| `content` | the lint of R6: stages, goals, lines, missions, beds, songs, credits, `OB.ABOUT` fields all filled (no empty, \"TBD\" or placeholder strings) |\n1548\t| `determinism` | `proof()`: 1,200 scripted steps from a seed, hashed twice with the same result; the hash differs when the seed differs |\n1549\t\n1550\t### 12.3 Browser suites (Playwright, `tests/browser/`)\n1551\t\n1552\t| Suite | What it proves |\n1553\t|---|---|\n1554\t| `bundle` | `node build.mjs`, then open the **root HTML by `file://`** at 1920×1080: no console errors, no network requests, the title scene is reached, `game.debug.snapshot().scene === 'title'`; the lint of §4.3 |\n1555\t| `check` | `node engine/tools/check.mjs <root html>` exits 0; act on its look notes (and record them in the log) |\n1556\t| `hub-harness` | copy the bundle and `vendor/hub/*` into `.cache/hubtest/`; start `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` in that folder; read the port it prints; confirm `/` serves our title; open `harness.html?game=<bundle>&players=2&auto=1`, then `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, then `&scenario=host-leave`; each must reach `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`; kill the server by its PID |\n1557\t| `touch-emulation` | `check.mjs --size 390x844@3`: the notice is visible, no touch buttons are drawn, and the snapshot shows attract running after 11 s |\n1558\t| `audio-lint` | the offline render checks of R5 for every song and jingle |\n1559\t| `screens` | screenshots of title, menu, garage, radio, race (one per kit), a fork, Love in Bloom request, Tour map, garden, results, ending, Versus split, hub card and dev panel, at 1920×1080, into `.cache/shots/`, for the review in M7 and M8. This suite fails only on errors, never on looks. |\n1560\t\n1561\t### 12.4 What only eyes can check (screenshots and filmstrips)\n1562\t\n1563\tCheck these with `tools/filmstrip.mjs` or screenshots, and note them in the build log:\n1564\t- the drift and spin animation (filmstrip);\n1565\t- a fork at speed (filmstrip);\n1566\t- bloom and lens flare not smearing pixels;\n1567\t- night and tunnel tints;\n1568\t- the car layers and the occupants' alignment at every yaw (a contact sheet of 33 frames);\n1569\t- each kit's density and composition;\n1570\t- HUD legibility at 1280×720;\n1571\t- the hub card's look.\n1572\t\n1573\tEverything that can be a number, a state or an event is a script instead.\n1574\t\n1575\t---\n1576\t\n1577\t## 13. Definition of done: every TASK.md requirement and where it lives\n1578\t\n1579\t| TASK.md requirement | Where | Proved by |\n1580\t|---|---|---|\n1581\t| Genre and perspective that fit the source | §1, §7.2 | screens |\n1582\t| Title riffing on the name, picked by a real RNG | header | — |\n1583\t| Single HTML named per TASK.md; may use CDNs | §1 names, §4.3 (we need none) | `bundle` |\n1584\t| Built on the my-3D2dge handoff; engine changes allowed | §5, §6, `engine/CHANGES.md` | all suites |\n1585\t| Engine-native art plus new assets that feel like the source | §8.6, §9 | screens |\n1586\t| Engine animation plus new animations | §7.13 | filmstrips |\n1587\t| All audio in code; gibberish voices | §7.14–7.16 | `audio-data`, `audio-lint`, `voice` |\n1588\t| AI Hub multiplayer and the game on its own | §7.11, §7.12 | `hub`, `hub-harness`, `versus` |\n1589\t| Dev subfolder, bundled to the root | §4 | `bundle` |\n1590\t| Shared machine: no stray reads, own ports, own PIDs | §4.1 | — |\n1591\t| Performance unlimited, so add detail | §7.2, §9 | — |\n1592\t| Headless tests behind one command, with a whole-game playthrough | §12 | `run.mjs` |\n1593\t| A feature-complete, polished vertical slice showing all mechanics | §7, §8, §10 | playthrough suites |\n1594\t| Dev mode in the pause menu, with live sliders and reset | §7.23, Appendix A | `dev` |\n1595\t| Attract mode after 10 s idle; any input exits; cheats allowed | §7.22 | `attract` |\n1596\t| Narrative tone like the source | §8.11 | `content` |\n1597\t| 10-foot, controller-first; keyboard and mouse native; hover focus; PlayStation glyphs (Xbox option); prompts follow the last device | §7.17–7.20, Appendices B–C | `ui`, `glyphs` |\n1598\t| No dense web-style screens | §7.17 | `ui` readability |\n1599\t| Touch-only: no touch controls, the notice and Settings, attract runs | §7.21 | `touch`, `touch-emulation` |\n1600\t| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | §4.3 lint, R6 | `content`, `bundle` |\n1601\t| Source Information and three feedback blocks in the reply and the About screen | §7.25 | `content` |\n1602\t| `exit-interview.json` in the exact shape, parsed | §7.25, M9 | `bundle` (parses it, checks every key is non-empty) |\n1603\t\n1604\t**Final steps (M9), in order:**\n1605\t1. Run `node tests/run.mjs` and get it green.\n1606\t2. Run `node build.mjs`.\n1607\t3. Fill `OB.ABOUT` with the real answers:\n1608\t   - **Date** and **Model:** from TASK.md.\n1609\t   - **Plan:** `PLAN.md`.\n1610\t   - **Usage:** say \"unknown\" if no estimate is available.\n1611\t   - **Feedback answers:** from the build log.\n1612\t4. Rebuild, so the About pages and `exit-interview.json` match.\n1613\t5. Validate the JSON: `node -e \"JSON.parse(require('fs').readFileSync('exit-interview.json','utf8'))\"`.\n1614\t6. Write the chat reply with the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback.\n1615\t\n1616\tFill the `aiHub` block as follows:\n1617\t\n1618\t| Field | Value |\n1619\t|---|---|\n1620\t| `name` | \"OutBloom 2006: Coast 2 Coast\" |\n1621\t| `tagline` | \"One day. One road. Every flower in bloom.\" |\n1622\t| `type` | \"Game\" |\n1623\t| `genre` | \"Racing\" |\n1624\t| `source` | \"OutRun 2006: Coast 2 Coast\" |\n1625\t| `series` | \"OutRun\" |\n1626\t| `players` | \"1–4\" |\n1627\t| `controls` | \"Steer: stick/←→/A D · Gas: R2/✕/W · Brake: L2/□/S · Drift: ○/Space · Pause: Options/Esc\" |\n1628\t| `tags` | [\"arcade\", \"vehicles\", \"pixel art\", \"retro\", \"comedy\", \"versus\"] |\n1629\t\n1630\t---\n1631\t\n1632\t## Appendix A. Tuning defaults (the Dev Mode sliders)\n1633\t\n1634\tAll of these are live (`OB.tuning`), and `OB.TUNING_DEFAULTS` holds the defaults that reset restores. \"×\" means a\n1635\tmultiplier on the per-car or per-stage value.\n1636\t\n1637\t| Group | Key | Default | Range | Meaning |\n1638\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n1639\t| Car | `topSpeedScale` | 1.00 | 0.6–1.4 | × car top speed |\n1640\t| Car | `accelScale` | 1.00 | 0.5–2.0 | × car acceleration |\n1641\t| Car | `brakeDecel` | 240 | 120–400 | km/h per second at full brake |\n1642\t| Car | `steerRate` | 2.20 | 1.0–4.0 | half-roads per second at full lock (× car steer / 2.2) |\n1643\t| Car | `steerResponse` | 9 | 3–20 | per second; how fast lateral speed follows the stick |\n1644\t| Car | `curvePush` | 0.62 | 0.2–1.2 | the throw outward per curve unit at top speed |\n1645\t| Car | `offroadTop` | 0.50 | 0.2–1.0 | share of top speed off the asphalt |\n1646\t| Car | `offroadDrag` | 120 | 0–300 | km/h per second above the off-road top |\n1647\t| Drift | `driftMinSpeed` | 120 | 60–200 | km/h |\n1648\t| Drift | `driftTapWindow` | 0.35 | 0.1–0.8 | s, the longest brake tap that counts |\n1649\t| Drift | `driftGrip` | 0.85 | 0–1 | how much the drift cancels the curve push (× car drift grip / 0.85) |\n1650\t| Drift | `driftAngleMax` | 0.75 | 0.3–1.2 | rad |\n1651\t| Drift | `driftSpeedLoss` | 16 | 0–60 | km/h per second at full angle |\n1652\t| Drift | `driftSteerBoost` | 1.30 | 1–2 | × steering while drifting |\n1653\t| Slipstream | `slipRange` | 1800 | 600–3000 | units behind a vehicle |\n1654\t| Slipstream | `slipWidth` | 0.22 | 0.1–0.5 | half-roads |\n1655\t| Slipstream | `slipTopBonus` | 0.06 | 0–0.2 | × top speed at full slip |\n1656\t| Slipstream | `slingshotBoost` | 14 | 0–40 | km/h |\n1657\t| Traffic | `trafficDensity` | 1.00 | 0–3 | × stage density |\n1658\t| Traffic | `trafficSpeedScale` | 1.00 | 0.5–1.5 | × type speeds |\n1659\t| Traffic | `spinThreshold` | 70 | 20–150 | km/h of closing speed that spins you |\n1660\t| Traffic | `bumpSpeedLoss` | 0.08 | 0–0.5 | share of speed lost below the other car's |\n1661\t| Clock | `startTime` | 75 | 30–150 | s |\n1662\t| Clock | `checkpointBonus` | 58/57/56/55 | 20–120 each | s for rows 2–5 |\n1663\t| Rivals | `rivalSkill` | 1.00 | 0.5–1.5 | × rival top speed and cornering |\n1664\t| Rivals | `rivalRubber` | 0.50 | 0–1 | rubber-band strength |\n1665\t| Love in Bloom | `petalScale` | 1.00 | 0.5–2 | × petals per rating |\n1666\t| Love in Bloom | `requestDifficulty` | 1.00 | 0.5–1.5 | × request thresholds and counts |\n1667\t| Love in Bloom | `bloomDecay` | 4 | 1–10 | s before the chain drains |\n1668\t| Camera | `cameraHeight` | 1000 | 600–1600 | units (chase) |\n1669\t| Camera | `cameraFov` | 100 | 70–130 | degrees at rest |\n1670\t| World | `gameSpeed` | 1.00 | 0.25–2 | `game.timeScale` while racing |\n1671\t\n1672\t## Appendix B. Input map (defaults; every action rebindable on the Controls screen)\n1673\t\n1674\t| Action | Keyboard | Mouse | Pad (PlayStation · Xbox) |\n1675\t|---|---|---|---|\n1676\t| Steer | A / D, ← / → | — | left stick, d-pad ← → |\n1677\t| Gas (analog) | W, ↑ | — | R2 · RT (analog), ✕ · A |\n1678\t| Brake / reverse (analog) | S, ↓ | — | L2 · LT (analog), □ · X |\n1679\t| Drift (handbrake) | Space | — | ○ · B |\n1680\t| Shift up / down (manual) | E / Q (also Left Shift / Left Ctrl) | — | R1 / L1 · RB / LB |\n1681\t| Horn | H | — | △ · Y |\n1682\t| Camera | C | — | d-pad ↓ |\n1683\t| Radio: next station | R | — | d-pad ↑ |\n1684\t| Pause | Esc, P | — | Options · Menu |\n1685\t| Menus: move · select · back | arrows, WASD · Enter, Space · Esc, Backspace | hover · left click · right click | d-pad, stick · ✕ · A · ○ · B |\n1686\t| Menus: tab or page | Q / E | wheel | L1 / R1 · LB / RB |\n1687\t| Versus P2 (shared keyboard) | arrows to steer and gas or brake, `.` drift, `/` horn, `,` camera | — | the second pad |\n1688\t\n1689\t## Appendix C. Glyphs (`OB.UI.prompt`)\n1690\t\n1691\t| Engine label | PlayStation icon | Xbox icon | Keyboard and mouse |\n1692\t|---|---|---|---|\n1693\t| Pad0 | ✕ blue cross on a dark disc | A green disc | — |\n1694\t| Pad1 | ○ red ring | B red disc | — |\n1695\t| Pad2 | □ pink square | X blue disc | — |\n1696\t| Pad3 | △ green triangle | Y yellow disc | — |\n1697\t| Pad4 / 5 | L1 / R1 pills | LB / RB pills | — |\n1698\t| Pad6 / 7 | L2 / R2 trigger shapes | LT / RT trigger shapes | — |\n1699\t| Pad8 / 9 | Create / Options ovals | View / Menu ovals | — |\n1700\t| Pad12–15 | d-pad with the arm lit | the same | — |\n1701\t| left stick | an L stick with arrows | the same | — |\n1702\t| keys | — | — | a keycap with the key's label (W, ↑, Space, Esc, Enter…), wide caps for long names |\n1703\t| Mouse0 / 2 / wheel | — | — | a mouse with the left or right button lit, or the wheel |\n1704\t\n1705\tEvery icon is 12×12 at scale 2, with a 1-px dark outline so it reads on any background. The text label beside it is the\n1706\taction's name (\"DRIFT\").\n1707\t\n1708\t## Appendix D. The debug handle and events\n1709\t\n1710\t`game.debug`:\n1711\t- `snapshot()` returns `{ scene, mode, stage, seg, z, x, v, gear, rpm, drift: { on, a, score }, slip, air, crash,\n1712\t  clock, score, bloom: { level, points }, petals, grade, request: { type, state, progress }, route, goal, rivals:\n1713\t  [{ id, z, x, place }], traffic: n, attract: phase, hub: { active, phase, sent, progress }, ui: { screen, focus },\n1714\t  lastDevice, glyphs, errors: n }`.\n1715\t- `events(n)` returns the last n bus events.\n1716\t- `teleport(stage, pct, x)`.\n1717\t- `tune(key, v)` and `resetTuning()`.\n1718\t- `cheat(name, on)`.\n1719\t- `go(scene, data)`.\n1720\t- `bot(on, params)`.\n1721\t- `ui.press(action)` and `ui.click(x, y)`.\n1722\t\n1723\t`game.stateHash = () => [stage, z, x, v, clock, score, petals]`, rounded.\n1724\t\n1725\t**Bus events:**\n1726\t\n1727\t| Area | Events |\n1728\t|---|---|\n1729\t| run | `run:start`, `run:end {reason}`, `stage:enter {id}`, `checkpoint {id, bonus}`, `fork:approach {left, right}`, `fork:pref {side}`, `fork:choose {side, next}`, `goal {id, mood}`, `time:low`, `time:up` |\n1730\t| car | `car:bump {with}`, `car:crash {with}`, `car:scrape`, `car:offroad {on}`, `air:start`, `air:land {hard}`, `start:perfect`, `drift:start {dir}`, `drift:end {score, length}`, `slip:start`, `slip:end`, `slingshot`, `nearmiss {id}`, `overtake {id, kind}`, `gust`, `horn` |\n1731\t| Love in Bloom | `req:start {type}`, `req:progress {n, of}`, `req:end {type, rating, petals}`, `petals {delta, reason}`, `bloom:level {n}` |\n1732\t| objects | `obj:pop`, `obj:bonk`, `obj:sting`, `obj:snap`, `obj:cone`, `obj:arch`, `obj:bouquet`, `obj:photo {rating}` |\n1733\t| rivals and missions | `rival:pass {id, by}`, `rival:out {id}`, `mission:end {id, cleared, rank}` |\n1734\t| other | `radio:tune {i}`, `ui:screen {name}`, `attract:start`, `attract:end`, `hub:*` |\n1735\t\n1736\t## Appendix E. Research notes (what the plan relied on)\n1737\t\n1738\t- **The engine.** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff: `README.md`, `API.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md` (the road-racing recipe, standard\n1739\t  features, testing, sessions), `docs/ASSETS.md` (shapes, `E.shapeSprite`), `modules/road.js` (the current single-road\n1740\t  renderer), `kits/road.js` (a complete coast-road slice: clock, checkpoints, radio, bot), `API-INDEX.md` (input,\n1741\t  glyphs, settings, audio, `E.Arcade`, `E.Road`). Its audio is a chip synth with one-shot voices and step-sequenced\n1742\t  songs on a game-time music clock. Its gamepad buttons are digital. Its standard menus are HTML.\n1743\t- **The AI Hub.** `lib/hub/README.md` (Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0, modes, card, start, reporting, rules, the\n1744\t  harness checks), `examples/ghost.html` (the ghost pattern: send every 100 ms unreliable, progress once a second,\n1745\t  result on finish, a result on time-up) and `harness.html` (its checks and its synthetic key pokes).\n1746\t- **The source**, from Wikipedia and a moegamer retrospective. *OutRun 2006: Coast 2 Coast* (SEGA, developed by Sumo\n1747\t  Digital, 2006; PS2, PSP, Xbox, PC) has these modes and mechanics:\n1748\t  - OutRun, Heart Attack (on-the-fly passenger requests rated with heart points and a final grade), Time Attack, and\n1749\t    the Coast 2 Coast career, whose missions come from characters.\n1750\t  - Mission types including Test Your Drift, Test Your Slipstream, Avoid the Knockout and Don't Lose Your Girlfriend.\n1751\t  - Unlocks bought with \"OutRun Miles\", and up to 6 players online.\n1752\t  - A brake-tap drift and slipstreaming.\n1753\t  - The mood: blue skies and escapist fun.\n1754\t\n1755\t  Nothing here copies its content (§3).\n1756\t- **Not found, so don't claim it.** Exact request lists, stage counts per course set, the music track list. Write\n1757\t  \"unknown\" in Source Information for any such detail.\n1758\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f95c51ac-ca98-4f0d-bbff-431b6595447e","uuid":"bc33b3e2-9f74-462b-8f05-a0c32cf158a4","timestamp":"2026-11-04T11:32:14.947Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-043153__xe1b7e1/work/PLAN.md","content":"### 8.9 The Garage Garden: beds and costs\n\n| Bed | Seeds | Bed | Seeds |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Gardenia GT Targa | 600 | Song \"Lemon Drop Rush\" | 300 |\n| Magnolia V12 Barchetta | 900 | Song \"Moonflower\" | 300 |\n| Girasole Hyper Spider | 1500 | Paint packs ×4 | 100 each |\n| Primavera Bloom Edition | 400 | Outfits ×3 | 150 each |\n| Orchidea Bloom Edition | 500 | Mirror Roads | 800 |\n\nThat totals **6,150 seeds**. All 14 missions at A (base 3,350 × 1.5) give 5,025. Arcade runs give about 150–350 each, so\nthe economy test's \"everything within 14 A-ranks plus 6 runs\" holds.\n\n### 8.10 Music (all original, all in code)\n\nRadio stations, in dial order:\n\n| # | Station | Song | BPM · key | Style and instruments | Progression (per 2 bars) | Hook idea |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 88.6 PETAL FM | **Sunburst Samba** | 128 · D major | Latin fusion: congas, timbales, slap bass, brass stabs, steel-drum lead, e-piano comping | Dmaj7 · Bm7 · Em7 · A7sus4–A7 | the steel drum climbs a 6th and falls by step, with a timbale fill into each chorus |\n| 2 | 91.2 SUN FM | **Coastline Crush** | 116 · F major | Balearic pop-funk: four on the floor, a chorus-guitar skank, finger bass, a soft pulse lead, pad | Fmaj7 · Em7 · Dm7 · Gm7–C9 | a breezy 3-note pickup into a long held note |\n| 3 | 94.8 SHORE FM | **Shuffle on the Shore** | 110 shuffle (swing in 12/8 feel, steps 3) · B♭ major | jazz shuffle: organ, walking bass, brushed snare, sax lead | B♭6 · G7 · Cm7 · F7 (turnaround) | call and response between the sax and the organ |\n| 4 | 99.1 VESPA FM | **Vespa Vendetta** | 152 · A minor | Eurobeat: octave saw bass, supersaw stabs, fast 16th arps, a bright lead | Am · F · G · E7 | the chorus jumps an octave on beat 3 |\n| 5 | 103.5 PASTEL FM | **Pastel Highway** | 104 · E♭ major | city pop: DX-style e-piano, slap bass, sax or lead, claps on 2 and 4, pad | A♭maj7 · Gm7 · Fm7 · B♭7sus4 | a sax lick answered by e-piano chords |\n| 6 | 106.3 ZEST FM (unlock) | **Lemon Drop Rush** | 140 · G major | acid jazz into breakbeat: breakbeat drums, a rubbery 303-ish bass (resonant filter env), organ stabs | G7 · C7 · G7 · D7–C7 | an organ riff in 4ths |\n| 7 | 108.8 MOON FM (unlock) | **Moonflower** | 92 · C♯ minor | a night synth ballad: a pad, marimba arps, a soft lead, a slow kick | C♯m9 · Amaj7 · E · B | a long lead line with a slide |\n\nOther music:\n\n| Song | Use | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **OutBloom!** | title | 120, C major; a brass fanfare into a samba groove |\n| **Garden Party** | menus, the Tour, the garden | 100 bossa nova; e-piano and nylon pluck |\n| **Petal Count** | results | 112; a light funk loop |\n| **Last Light Lullaby** | the credits and Bloom endings | 84 |\n\nJingles (played with `sting`):\n\n| Jingle | Feel |\n|---|---|\n| start fanfare | 2 bars |\n| checkpoint | a bright V–I |\n| request start | a rising triplet |\n| request success | a major arpeggio up |\n| request fail | the trombone slide |\n| fork tick | — |\n| Full Bloom | a sparkle sweep |\n| goal fanfare | 4 bars |\n| new record | — |\n| unlock bloom | — |\n| game over | \"Sunset Blues\", a slow minor-blues turnaround |\n\nEvery song needs its named layers and a `sparkle` layer (§7.14).\n\n### 8.11 Writing style and the line bank\n\n**Style.**\n- Short, sunny, cheeky, like a peppy arcade passenger who is half sweetheart, half backseat driver.\n- Exclamation-heavy but never mean. A touch of cheese and a wink. No profanity.\n- **Lengths:** call-outs ≤ 20 characters, in caps. Spoken lines ≤ 40 characters, 1–2 per bubble.\n- Banners are 1–3 words. Ending captions are a postcard sentence.\n- Each character's voice stays distinct: Marisol bubbly, Vivi theatrical (\"Darling…\"), Juni breathless slang, Bruno\n  booming with \"champ\".\n- **Required counts:** each passenger has ≥ 4 variants for each reaction (request intro per type, Perfect, Great, Good,\n  Miss, bump, crash, near miss, air, fork preference left and right, checkpoint, low time, goal, start). Bruno has ≥ 3\n  per event.\n\n**Seed lines** (use them, then write the rest in the same voice):\n\n| Who | Event | Lines |\n|---|---|---|\n| Marisol | start | \"Coast to coast? With you? Let's GO!\" |\n| Marisol | balloons | \"Ooh, balloons! Pop them all for me!\" |\n| Marisol | Perfect | \"Eee! You're amazing!\" · \"Again, again!\" |\n| Marisol | Miss | \"Aww… you missed so many.\" |\n| Marisol | crash | \"Kyaaa! My hair!\" |\n| Marisol | fork left | \"Left! I want to smell the lavender!\" |\n| Marisol | low time | \"Hurry, the sun's slipping!\" |\n| Vivi | start | \"Darling, do try not to dent anything.\" |\n| Vivi | speed | \"Faster. I refuse to arrive second.\" |\n| Vivi | Great | \"Hmm. Adequate. Barely.\" |\n| Vivi | Perfect | \"Now THAT is how one arrives!\" |\n| Vivi | bump | \"Was that a… car? Unacceptable.\" |\n| Vivi | fork right | \"Right, the cliffs. Everyone who matters goes that way.\" |\n| Juni | start | \"Okay okay okay, get me some ACTION!\" |\n| Juni | close | \"Closer! Closer! I need the shot!\" |\n| Juni | near miss | \"WHOA, did you see that?!\" |\n| Juni | Miss | \"Boooring. Give me danger!\" |\n| Juni | Perfect | \"Best shot of my LIFE!\" |\n| Juni | air | \"We're FLYING!\" |\n| Bruno | start | \"Morning, champ! The west coast is waiting!\" |\n| Bruno | drift | \"Ho ho! You drift like my rally days!\" |\n| Bruno | mission win | \"Mamma mia, what a finish!\" |\n| Bruno | mission fail | \"Again, champ! Even I spun out in '79.\" |\n| Announcer | — | \"CHECKPOINT!\" · \"TIME EXTENDED!\" · \"GOAL!\" · \"FULL BLOOM!\" · \"PERFECT START!\" · \"NEW RECORD!\" |\n\nEnding captions (one per goal and mood), for example: \"GOAL A · LANTERN PIER: A thousand lanterns, and one just for\nyou two.\" · \"GOAL E · FINISTERRA LIGHTHOUSE: The green flash. Best. Day. Ever.\"\n\nHelp, briefs and the How to Play text use the same voice, plainly.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Art direction and asset recipes\n\n### 9.1 Rules\n\n- **Base style.** The engine's native style: pixel art at 480×270, shaded with `E.tones` (cool shadows, warm lights, a\n  `hi` sparkle). Everything is drawn with `E.px.*`, `E.font`, sprites and shapes. Never use `ctx.arc`, `stroke`,\n  `fillText` or canvas gradients on the buffer. Translucency goes through `px.blend` or the dither helpers.\n- **Fog and distance.** Use the road module's fog (colour per time of day). Far sprites fade to the fog colour at the\n  module's stepped alpha.\n- **Palettes.** Every kit has five swatches: `ground`, `ground2`, `accent`, `rock` and `sky` ramps. Time of day applies\n  a grade on top: dawn peach and lilac, noon clean and saturated, late afternoon gold, sunset magenta to orange, night\n  indigo with neon.\n- **Sizes.**\n  - **The player's car** is about 128 px wide at the screen's bottom (480 wide).\n  - **Traffic** is drawn from the same model scale.\n  - **Palms** at the edge reach about 160 px tall near the camera.\n  - **Rigs in the car:** heads about 12–14 px, with the upper body showing.\n  - **Ending rigs** are about 70 px tall (size 1.6).\n\n### 9.2 Sky presets (`OB.Sky`, in `01-data-world.js`)\n\nEach sky is `{ gradient: [5 colours], sun | moon: { x, y, r, color, halo }, clouds: { kind, color, y, speed },\nlayers: [...] }`.\n\n- **Layer kinds:** the engine's `sea`, `hills`, `mountains` (snow), `city` (windows), `clouds` and `fog`, plus our\n  painter layers: `mesa`, `cliffs`, `village` (terraced houses), `balloons` (drifting), `lanterns` (rising), `aurora`\n  (animated ribbons), `stars` (with shooting stars), `skyline-neon` (blinking) and `rainbow` (sun shower).\n- **Count.** About 16 presets in all: one per stage, with the beach kit's dawn and sunset variants.\n\n### 9.3 Cars as shapes, pre-rendered\n\n- **Writing the shape.** Each car is a set of `E.SHAPES` entries in `src/02-shapes.js` (`ob-car-orchidea-under`, `-over`, `-lights`), written facing south per the asset card\n  (front +y, so *rear* is −y).\n  - **Parts:** body side profile as `prism` (outline on x/z, pushed along y), bonnet and boot as `wedge`/`prism`, wheels\n    as `cyl` rotated onto the x axis, seats as `box`, roll hoops as `lathe`/`cyl`, lamps as `box` in glow materials.\n  - **Materials:** the slot `paint` uses a key ramp of five tones, so recolouring is a pure palette swap. Also `chrome`\n    (`steel`), `glass` and `tyre`.\n- **Three shapes per car:** `…-under` (floor, seats, dash, wheel), `…-over` (doors, rear deck, bumpers, lamps' housings,\n  wheels) and `…-lights` (tail, brake and head lamps only, glow material).\n- **Pre-rendering.** At boot, render each with `E.shapeSprite(id, { view: OB.CHASE_VIEW, facing, size })`. The view is\n  `new E.View('chase', 'Chase', 0, 9)`, a camera looking north. The facing is `−π/2 + yaw`. That gives 33 yaws (−90° to\n  +90°, 5.625° apart) and 32 spin frames.\n  - **Check it** with `node engine/tools/asset-sheet.mjs src/02-shapes.js#ob-car-orchidea-over`: the sheet shows every view\n    and facing, and its lint flags thin parts.\n- **Seat anchors** per yaw are computed by projecting the seat points through the same view and stored with the frames.\n- **Traffic.** Ten traffic shapes, rendered at 9 yaws (−40° to +40°) × 2 layers (body and lights).\n- **Headless tests skip all pre-rendering.** `OB.Art` hands out stand-in frames with the right `w`/`h` and anchors, so\n  the sim and its tests run in about a second.\n\n### 9.4 Scenery and other sprites\n\n- **Core props:** `E.prop(name).frames[0]` for palm, tree, pine, bush, flowers, rock, cactus, streetlamp, sign, fence,\n  lamp, awning, lantern and barrel. Tint them with `color` where the kit needs it.\n- **Store shapes.** `kenney-nature` gives cliffs, trees and rocks, and `kenney-fantasy-town` houses for villages. Cut\n  only the ids used into `src/assets-ob.js` with `engine/tools/asset-set.mjs`. Load it with `E.addAssets` and credit\n  the sets on the About screen.\n- **New sprites** (★ in §8.6) are either `E.SHAPES` entries rendered at 2–3 facings with `OB.CHASE_VIEW`, or string\n  sprites (`E.sprite`) for the small things: balloons, bees, cones, bouquets, petals, glyph icons.\n- **Animated scenery** uses `E.anim`: turbines, waterfalls, flags, lanterns, neon, aurora, flamingos.\n- **Landmarks** (the Ferris wheel, cathedral, casino flower, castle, lift bridge, radio dish, lighthouse) are shapes or\n  custom `draw(g, sx, sy, scale)` objects built with `px.poly`/`px.rect` and toned with `E.tones`.\n\n### 9.5 UI art\n\n- **Logo.** `E.font.title` at scale 5, with `colors: ['#fff4e0', '#ffb3d1', '#ff7a59', '#ffcf4a']`, depth 4 and\n  `depthColor: '#5a1e4a'`. A sunflower replaces the \"O\", and a white shine sweeps across every 6 s.\n- **Panels.** `E.ui.box` with `bg: ['#2a1450', '#5a2a6a']` (dusk), a border in `#ffd6a0`, and accents in pink\n  `#ff5fa2`, sun `#ffcf4a` and sea `#3ac8e0`.\n- **Glyph icons.** 12×12 sprites, drawn at scale 2 for prompts (Appendix C).\n- **Postcards and garden beds** are drawn panels with a stamp, a portrait and an icon. Every icon gets a shape or\n  letter as well as its colour.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Build order and milestones\n\nBuild in this order. Each milestone ends with `node tests/run.mjs` green and a fresh bundle that opens from `file://`.\nEarlier milestones may use plain art, but never stubs. Anything built must work fully. Polish comes later, and nothing\nis cut.\n\n| Milestone | Build | Done when |\n|---|---|---|\n| **M0 Foundations** | the dev folder, the handoff engine copied, Playwright installed; `src/manifest.json`, `index.html`, `build.mjs` with the session client inlined first in `<head>`; `tests/run.mjs` printing only failures and a one-line count; `00-core.js` (bus, rand, tuning), `90-main.js` (game, boot steps, a real title scene with the logo); `game.debug` | the bundle opens with no errors (browser smoke test); the headless boot reaches `title` |\n| **M1 Road and feel** (the riskiest first) | E1/E2 road junctions and dressing; S1, S2 and S3 with real layouts and first-pass scenery; `CarSim` complete (§7.3); traffic (§7.4); the clock and checkpoints; the HUD core; the Orchidea model pre-rendered (layers and yaws); `OB.Brain` bot; the dev panel's **Tuning** tab and debug overlay | the physics suite passes (curve-hold targets, drift entry and exit, slipstream, bumps, spins, rails); the junction suite shows a seamless switch (≤ 1 px); the bot drives S1→S2 and S1→S3 inside the target times; a filmstrip of a drift through a 5-curve looks right |\n| **M2 The Arcade loop** | all 15 stages and 5 goals as real data with the kit rules; the route graph; scoring and the Bloom chain; results and initials; first-pass ending vignettes; title → menu → garage → radio → race; the UI kit with glyph prompts; settings (§7.20) with E5; the profile save; the synth extension E6/E7 with 2 radio songs, the engine note and core sound effects; E3 analog input | the 16-route playthrough passes (the bot reaches every goal in time on Normal); UI navigation passes for pad, keys and mouse; the glyph suite passes; content lint passes for the stages |\n| **M3 Platform features** | AI Hub ghost session with our card (§7.12); attract mode (§7.22); touch-only (§7.21); every Dev Mode tab (§7.23) in the pause menu; local Versus split screen (§7.11); E4, E8 | the fake-hub suite and the **harness in a browser** pass (`__hubCheck.pass`, 2 and 4 players, latency and loss); the attract suite passes; touch emulation shows the notice; the dev suite passes (slider changes behaviour, reset restores it); two Versus bots finish |\n| **M4 The passenger** | the cast's rigs in the car with layers and anchors; portraits; speech bubbles; the babble voice; Love in Bloom with all 14 request types and their objects; petals, grades, fork preferences; every ending (5 × 3) with all 45 passenger lines | the request suite has a pass and a fail script per type; the grade maths passes; a Love in Bloom playthrough reaches each mood; babble planner tests pass |\n| **M5 Career and meta** | rival personas; all 14 missions and their rules; the Tour map, briefs and debriefs; the garden and economy; Time Trial with ghosts and medals; records | every mission is cleared by its bot at C or better and AAA is possible with cheats off; the economy suite passes; ghosts survive record → save → load → replay |\n| **M6 Audio content** | all 7 radio songs, 4 themes, every jingle; the full sound-effect list (§7.15); per-car engine notes; ambience per kit; radio switching; ducking; tunnel reverb | audio lint (levels, clipping, silence) and the spectrum bands pass for every song; every sound-effect name in the data exists |\n| **M7 Art and effects** | all 13 kits fully dressed; every landmark; all skies; bloom, flare, shimmer and weather (sun shower, petal storm, aurora, fireworks, shooting stars); every car, traffic vehicle, rig and request-object animation (§7.13) | the screenshot review of each stage at 1920×1080 is done and noted in the log; `tools/check.mjs` look notes are acted on; there are no flat empty roadsides |\n| **M8 UX polish and balance** | transitions; hints; How to Play pages with live demos; About & Credits; comfort settings; HUD size; balance with the sliders and bot metrics (§11) | the readability suite passes (no text below scale 1, ≤ 7 items per screen); the balance windows hold (§11, R2) |\n| **M9 Release** | the full test pass; the final bundle; `exit-interview.json` (parsed); the About pages and the chat reply with the real Source Information and feedback; `engine/CHANGES.md` complete | everything in §13 is ticked |\n\n**Work habits.**\n- Rerun only the suite that covers what you changed (`node tests/run.mjs --only physics`), and the full command before\n  ending a milestone.\n- Write nothing outside the dev folder and the root deliverables (the HTML and `exit-interview.json`).\n- Note every engine change in `engine/CHANGES.md` as you make it.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Risks and how to de-risk them\n\n| # | Risk | Mitigation |\n|---|---|---|\n| R1 | **The fork renderer and the seamless stage switch.** The road module draws one road, so junctions are new code in the hottest path. | Build it first (M1). Keep it simple with the flat-junction rule (§7.1), one shared height profile and per-branch lateral offsets. Unit-test the projection: both branch centres; the road centre and player's screen x before and after the switch within 1 px; the gore cushion pushes to the nearer side. Record a filmstrip of a fork at 250 km/h, and review frames 1–16 for pops. |\n| R2 | **Drift and handling feel**, the heart of the game, tuned without hands on a pad. | Every constant is a live slider from M1. The physics suite asserts the design targets (§7.3: a 3 holdable flat out, a 4 needing a lift, a 6 drifted at about 85 %). **Balance windows**, asserted by the bot suite: on Normal, the reference bot finishes the all-left route with 15–30 s spare and the all-right route with 2–12 s. A \"sloppy\" bot (1 spin per stage) fails at least half the routes. A \"no-drift\" bot finishes no more than 6 of the 16. |\n| R3 | **Car sprites from shapes might look crude, or boot slowly.** | Prototype the Orchidea in M1 and check it with `asset-sheet.mjs` and in-race screenshots. If the shape renderer can't give clean curves at 128 px, draw the body with `px.poly` profiles per yaw band (the ROAD kit's method, 33 frames), which is just as complete. If boot exceeds about 8 s, render the cars a race needs behind a short \"Polishing the chrome…\" boot step at race start. |\n| R4 | **AI Hub conformance**, which is a hard gate. | Integrate in M3 using the ghost example's pattern. `ready()` never waits for input, and the client stays verbatim. Run the official harness in Playwright with 2 and 4 players, `latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5`, and the `host-leave` scenario. Re-run it on the final bundle (M9). |\n| R5 | **Audio quality, judged by builders who can't hear.** | Composition rules (§7.14), named instrument presets, and the offline render lint: in Playwright, `OfflineAudioContext` renders 12 s of each song through the real synth code. It asserts a peak under 0.98, RMS between 0.06 and 0.25, no silence over 1 s (except a designed rest), and band energy order (low < 250 Hz present, 1–4 kHz present). It also writes spectrogram PNGs to `.cache/audio/` to look at. |\n| R6 | **Content volume** (15 stages, 13 kits, 45 ending lines, 14 missions, 11 songs) causing gaps or placeholders. | Data schemas plus the **content-lint suite**: every stage has its fields, slots, landmarks and par; every goal has 3 moods × 3 passengers; every line key used exists and none is empty; every mission has rules, rewards and a bot; every bed has a cost and a reward source; bundle text has no TODO, placeholder or lorem. M2 lands all stages as real data early. |\n| R7 | **10-foot readability.** | UI-kit rules: a minimum scale and an item count, enforced in a debug-build assertion that the readability suite runs headless. Screenshots at 1920×1080 and 1280×720 reviewed in M7 and M8. The HUD's Extra-large size is tested too. |\n| R8 | **Headless speed and determinism.** | No pre-rendering headless. All gameplay randomness comes from seeded streams. `game.stateHash` covers the race state. A proof hash of 1,200 scripted steps run twice must match. |\n| R9 | **Split-screen layout.** | A per-scene `res` of `[640, 360]`, the rows layout, a HUD scaled for 180-px-tall viewports. Two-bot Versus test and screenshots. |\n| R10 | **Single-file bundle under `file://`.** | `build.mjs` lint (§4.3). The Playwright suite opens the root HTML by `file://`: no request leaves the page, there are no console errors, and the title is reached. |\n| R11 | **Touch-only false positives** (touchscreen laptops). | Require no fine pointer plus no keydown and no pad. Any key or pad clears the notice at once. Tested with emulation both ways. |\n| R12 | **Scope.** | Everything in this plan is in scope, and nothing more. Prefer finishing a system completely before starting the next, in milestone order. |\n\n---\n\n## 12. Testing\n\n### 12.1 The one command\n\n`node tests/run.mjs` runs everything from the dev folder:\n- the headless suites first (in about 10 s), then the browser suites;\n- `--quick` skips the browser suites, `--only <suite>[,<suite>]` runs only those, and `--update-par` rewrites the\n  stages' par and medal times from the reference bot.\n\n**Output.** One line per failure (`FAIL physics/curve-4-needs-lift: held 1.18 at 96% vtop`), then one summary line:\n`212 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped (14.2 s)`. It exits with code 1 on any failure and prints nothing else.\n\n**The harness** (`tests/lib/boot.mjs`), as `game/test.mjs` does it:\n- Make a `vm` context with `window` (the context itself), `addEventListener`, `location` and `console` stand-ins.\n- Load `engine/my-3d2dge.js` and every file in `src/manifest.json`. The headless `E.Game` skips drawing.\n- Return `{ E, game, OB, I: game.input, snap: () => game.debug.snapshot(), events }`.\n- **Inputs:** `I.tap`, `I.hold`, `I.setAnalog` (E3), `I.feed` for the mouse. **Stepping:** `game.advance(s)`.\n- **Fresh state:** each suite boots a fresh context, so suites never share state.\n\n### 12.2 Headless suites\n\n| Suite | What it proves |\n|---|---|\n| `core` | boot reaches `title`; no `game.errors`; the bus delivers in order; tuning reset restores every default; rand streams repeat for a seed |\n| `road` | every stage builds within its length bounds; the first 150 segments and the junctions are flat; `info()` reports ground, edges and spans as the data says; the scenery is placed the same twice for one seed |\n| `junction` | branch centres at the decision point; the side chosen equals `sign(x)`; **seamless switch**: before and after it, the road centre's screen x is within 1 px and the player's z continues; gore cushion hits push to the nearer branch; a closed fork on a fixed route blocks that side |\n| `physics` | per-car top speed and 0–100 within 3 %; brakes; the curve-hold design targets; drift entry by all three inputs and none below 120 km/h; counter-steer straightens; drift score accumulates; the slipstream builds and the slingshot fires; off-road slowdown; rails scrape without spinning; bump against spin by closing speed; crest air and steering cut; gusts push |\n| `traffic` | the mix and density per stage; the minimum spacing; a two-lane gap always exists; lane changes avoid overlaps; junction branch assignment; ribbon pairs stay paired; overtake and near-miss detection with exact geometry cases; the horn makes the car ahead change lane |\n| `race` | the clock, checkpoint bonuses, difficulty scaling; time up → `gameover`; goal → `ending` → `results`; score maths; Bloom chain levels, decay and crash halving; the route string is recorded |\n| `playthrough-arcade` | **the bot drives all 16 routes to all 5 goals** on Normal within the R2 balance windows; no errors; every stage enters and every checkpoint fires once |\n| `requests` | **for each of the 14 types**, a scripted *pass* run (the bot targets the objects) rates Perfect, and a scripted *fail* run (drive straight in lane 1, or crash on purpose) rates Miss; petals and the favourite ×1.2; fork preference ±; penalties |\n| `playthrough-love` | Love in Bloom on 3 routes with 3 passengers reaches Bloom, Bud and Wilt (by scripted quality); every ending vignette runs to its end with every line found |\n| `missions` | **every mission** is cleared by its bot at C or better, and AAA is reachable with the \"perfect\" bot; the failure path works for each; rewards are paid once per rank improvement; unlock chains open the right postcards; the Finale opens at 9 cleared |\n| `economy` | 14 missions at A plus 6 arcade runs buy every bed; buying with too few seeds is refused; unlocks take effect in the garage and radio |\n| `trial` | a ghost records, saves, loads and replays to the same positions; splits; medals from par |\n| `versus` | two players join (pad and keyboard halves); a split-screen race with 2 bots finishes; places; rematch |\n| `hub` | with a **fake `AIHub`** in the context (`active: true`, `connect`, `ready`, `on`, `time`, `send`, `progress`, `result`, `answer`, `rng`), check: the title is skipped; `connect` uses ghost and `[1, 4]` with a card; `ready()` comes without input; Enter or ✕ on the card answers ready and △ answers more; the start syncs to `time() ≥ 0`; ghost sends happen at 8–12 Hz with `area`; progress comes once a second; the goal reports `result({ time })`; `time-up` reports `result({ score })` once; `end` stops input and sound; quit, restart and dev mode are hidden; Options with Create held doesn't pause. With `connect` resolving `null`, it boots to the title. |\n| `attract` | 10 s idle on each listed screen starts `attract`; 9.9 s doesn't; any key, pad, mouse move or click returns to the same screen and focus, and that input is consumed; a 100 s run emits fork, drift, request-Perfect, rival-or-versus and goal events; no profile writes |\n| `touch` | with stand-ins for touch-only media queries: the notice shows, the scenes have no touch buttons, and the attract mode runs; a tap doesn't end the attract mode; a keydown clears the notice |\n| `ui` | from the title, every screen in §5.3 is reachable with pad, keys and mouse (`I.feed`), and each has a way back; focus never lands on nothing; the readability assertion (text at scale 1 or more, at most 7 focusable items) holds on every screen |\n| `glyphs` | every action's prompt for keyboard, mouse, PlayStation and Xbox; the default is PlayStation; the setting switches to Xbox; rebinding changes the prompt; the device switch updates the prompts on the next step |\n| `dev` | the pause-menu Dev Mode item opens the panel; each slider changes its sim effect (for example, top speed changes v after 5 s of full gas); per-row and global reset; cheats work; every warp lands where it says; \"Keep after reload\" saves and restores |\n| `save` | the profile round-trip; a migration from a v0 fixture; blocked storage is survived; settings persist |\n| `voice` | the babble planner: syllable counts for fixtures, total duration in range, the same text giving the same plan, moods shifting pitch, punctuation pauses |\n| `audio-data` | every song parses (notes, chords, accents, slides); every track has an instrument; every song has a `sparkle` layer; every sound-effect and jingle name used exists |\n| `content` | the lint of R6: stages, goals, lines, missions, beds, songs, credits, `OB.ABOUT` fields all filled (no empty, \"TBD\" or placeholder strings) |\n| `determinism` | `proof()`: 1,200 scripted steps from a seed, hashed twice with the same result; the hash differs when the seed differs |\n\n### 12.3 Browser suites (Playwright, `tests/browser/`)\n\n| Suite | What it proves |\n|---|---|\n| `bundle` | `node build.mjs`, then open the **root HTML by `file://`** at 1920×1080: no console errors, no network requests, the title scene is reached, `game.debug.snapshot().scene === 'title'`; the lint of §4.3 |\n| `check` | `node engine/tools/check.mjs <root html>` exits 0; act on its look notes (and record them in the log) |\n| `hub-harness` | copy the bundle and `vendor/hub/*` into `.cache/hubtest/`; start `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` in that folder; read the port it prints; confirm `/` serves our title; open `harness.html?game=<bundle>&players=2&auto=1`, then `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, then `&scenario=host-leave`; each must reach `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`; kill the server by its PID |\n| `touch-emulation` | `check.mjs --size 390x844@3`: the notice is visible, no touch buttons are drawn, and the snapshot shows attract running after 11 s |\n| `audio-lint` | the offline render checks of R5 for every song and jingle |\n| `screens` | screenshots of title, menu, garage, radio, race (one per kit), a fork, Love in Bloom request, Tour map, garden, results, ending, Versus split, hub card and dev panel, at 1920×1080, into `.cache/shots/`, for the review in M7 and M8. This suite fails only on errors, never on looks. |\n\n### 12.4 What only eyes can check (screenshots and filmstrips)\n\nCheck these with `tools/filmstrip.mjs` or screenshots, and note them in the build log:\n- the drift and spin animation (filmstrip);\n- a fork at speed (filmstrip);\n- bloom and lens flare not smearing pixels;\n- night and tunnel tints;\n- the car layers and the occupants' alignment at every yaw (a contact sheet of 33 frames);\n- each kit's density and composition;\n- HUD legibility at 1280×720;\n- the hub card's look.\n\nEverything that can be a number, a state or an event is a script instead.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Definition of done: every TASK.md requirement and where it lives\n\n| TASK.md requirement | Where | Proved by |\n|---|---|---|\n| Genre and perspective that fit the source | §1, §7.2 | screens |\n| Title riffing on the name, picked by a real RNG | header | — |\n| Single HTML named per TASK.md; may use CDNs | §1 names, §4.3 (we need none) | `bundle` |\n| Built on the my-3D2dge handoff; engine changes allowed | §5, §6, `engine/CHANGES.md` | all suites |\n| Engine-native art plus new assets that feel like the source | §8.6, §9 | screens |\n| Engine animation plus new animations | §7.13 | filmstrips |\n| All audio in code; gibberish voices | §7.14–7.16 | `audio-data`, `audio-lint`, `voice` |\n| AI Hub multiplayer and the game on its own | §7.11, §7.12 | `hub`, `hub-harness`, `versus` |\n| Dev subfolder, bundled to the root | §4 | `bundle` |\n| Shared machine: no stray reads, own ports, own PIDs | §4.1 | — |\n| Performance unlimited, so add detail | §7.2, §9 | — |\n| Headless tests behind one command, with a whole-game playthrough | §12 | `run.mjs` |\n| A feature-complete, polished vertical slice showing all mechanics | §7, §8, §10 | playthrough suites |\n| Dev mode in the pause menu, with live sliders and reset | §7.23, Appendix A | `dev` |\n| Attract mode after 10 s idle; any input exits; cheats allowed | §7.22 | `attract` |\n| Narrative tone like the source | §8.11 | `content` |\n| 10-foot, controller-first; keyboard and mouse native; hover focus; PlayStation glyphs (Xbox option); prompts follow the last device | §7.17–7.20, Appendices B–C | `ui`, `glyphs` |\n| No dense web-style screens | §7.17 | `ui` readability |\n| Touch-only: no touch controls, the notice and Settings, attract runs | §7.21 | `touch`, `touch-emulation` |\n| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | §4.3 lint, R6 | `content`, `bundle` |\n| Source Information and three feedback blocks in the reply and the About screen | §7.25 | `content` |\n| `exit-interview.json` in the exact shape, parsed | §7.25, M9 | `bundle` (parses it, checks every key is non-empty) |\n\n**Final steps (M9), in order:**\n1. Run `node tests/run.mjs` and get it green.\n2. Run `node build.mjs`.\n3. Fill `OB.ABOUT` with the real answers:\n   - **Date** and **Model:** from TASK.md.\n   - **Plan:** `PLAN.md`.\n   - **Usage:** say \"unknown\" if no estimate is available.\n   - **Feedback answers:** from the build log.\n4. Rebuild, so the About pages and `exit-interview.json` match.\n5. Validate the JSON: `node -e \"JSON.parse(require('fs').readFileSync('exit-interview.json','utf8'))\"`.\n6. Write the chat reply with the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback.\n\nFill the `aiHub` block as follows:\n\n| Field | Value |\n|---|---|\n| `name` | \"OutBloom 2006: Coast 2 Coast\" |\n| `tagline` | \"One day. One road. Every flower in bloom.\" |\n| `type` | \"Game\" |\n| `genre` | \"Racing\" |\n| `source` | \"OutRun 2006: Coast 2 Coast\" |\n| `series` | \"OutRun\" |\n| `players` | \"1–4\" |\n| `controls` | \"Steer: stick/←→/A D · Gas: R2/✕/W · Brake: L2/□/S · Drift: ○/Space · Pause: Options/Esc\" |\n| `tags` | [\"arcade\", \"vehicles\", \"pixel art\", \"retro\", \"comedy\", \"versus\"] |\n\n---\n\n## Appendix A. Tuning defaults (the Dev Mode sliders)\n\nAll of these are live (`OB.tuning`), and `OB.TUNING_DEFAULTS` holds the defaults that reset restores. \"×\" means a\nmultiplier on the per-car or per-stage value.\n\n| Group | Key | Default | Range | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Car | `topSpeedScale` | 1.00 | 0.6–1.4 | × car top speed |\n| Car | `accelScale` | 1.00 | 0.5–2.0 | × car acceleration |\n| Car | `brakeDecel` | 240 | 120–400 | km/h per second at full brake |\n| Car | `steerRate` | 2.20 | 1.0–4.0 | half-roads per second at full lock (× car steer / 2.2) |\n| Car | `steerResponse` | 9 | 3–20 | per second; how fast lateral speed follows the stick |\n| Car | `curvePush` | 0.62 | 0.2–1.2 | the throw outward per curve unit at top speed |\n| Car | `offroadTop` | 0.50 | 0.2–1.0 | share of top speed off the asphalt |\n| Car | `offroadDrag` | 120 | 0–300 | km/h per second above the off-road top |\n| Drift | `driftMinSpeed` | 120 | 60–200 | km/h |\n| Drift | `driftTapWindow` | 0.35 | 0.1–0.8 | s, the longest brake tap that counts |\n| Drift | `driftGrip` | 0.85 | 0–1 | how much the drift cancels the curve push (× car drift grip / 0.85) |\n| Drift | `driftAngleMax` | 0.75 | 0.3–1.2 | rad |\n| Drift | `driftSpeedLoss` | 16 | 0–60 | km/h per second at full angle |\n| Drift | `driftSteerBoost` | 1.30 | 1–2 | × steering while drifting |\n| Slipstream | `slipRange` | 1800 | 600–3000 | units behind a vehicle |\n| Slipstream | `slipWidth` | 0.22 | 0.1–0.5 | half-roads |\n| Slipstream | `slipTopBonus` | 0.06 | 0–0.2 | × top speed at full slip |\n| Slipstream | `slingshotBoost` | 14 | 0–40 | km/h |\n| Traffic | `trafficDensity` | 1.00 | 0–3 | × stage density |\n| Traffic | `trafficSpeedScale` | 1.00 | 0.5–1.5 | × type speeds |\n| Traffic | `spinThreshold` | 70 | 20–150 | km/h of closing speed that spins you |\n| Traffic | `bumpSpeedLoss` | 0.08 | 0–0.5 | share of speed lost below the other car's |\n| Clock | `startTime` | 75 | 30–150 | s |\n| Clock | `checkpointBonus` | 58/57/56/55 | 20–120 each | s for rows 2–5 |\n| Rivals | `rivalSkill` | 1.00 | 0.5–1.5 | × rival top speed and cornering |\n| Rivals | `rivalRubber` | 0.50 | 0–1 | rubber-band strength |\n| Love in Bloom | `petalScale` | 1.00 | 0.5–2 | × petals per rating |\n| Love in Bloom | `requestDifficulty` | 1.00 | 0.5–1.5 | × request thresholds and counts |\n| Love in Bloom | `bloomDecay` | 4 | 1–10 | s before the chain drains |\n| Camera | `cameraHeight` | 1000 | 600–1600 | units (chase) |\n| Camera | `cameraFov` | 100 | 70–130 | degrees at rest |\n| World | `gameSpeed` | 1.00 | 0.25–2 | `game.timeScale` while racing |\n\n## Appendix B. Input map (defaults; every action rebindable on the Controls screen)\n\n| Action | Keyboard | Mouse | Pad (PlayStation · Xbox) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Steer | A / D, ← / → | — | left stick, d-pad ← → |\n| Gas (analog) | W, ↑ | — | R2 · RT (analog), ✕ · A |\n| Brake / reverse (analog) | S, ↓ | — | L2 · LT (analog), □ · X |\n| Drift (handbrake) | Space | — | ○ · B |\n| Shift up / down (manual) | E / Q (also Left Shift / Left Ctrl) | — | R1 / L1 · RB / LB |\n| Horn | H | — | △ · Y |\n| Camera | C | — | d-pad ↓ |\n| Radio: next station | R | — | d-pad ↑ |\n| Pause | Esc, P | — | Options · Menu |\n| Menus: move · select · back | arrows, WASD · Enter, Space · Esc, Backspace | hover · left click · right click | d-pad, stick · ✕ · A · ○ · B |\n| Menus: tab or page | Q / E | wheel | L1 / R1 · LB / RB |\n| Versus P2 (shared keyboard) | arrows to steer and gas or brake, `.` drift, `/` horn, `,` camera | — | the second pad |\n\n## Appendix C. Glyphs (`OB.UI.prompt`)\n\n| Engine label | PlayStation icon | Xbox icon | Keyboard and mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Pad0 | ✕ blue cross on a dark disc | A green disc | — |\n| Pad1 | ○ red ring | B red disc | — |\n| Pad2 | □ pink square | X blue disc | — |\n| Pad3 | △ green triangle | Y yellow disc | — |\n| Pad4 / 5 | L1 / R1 pills | LB / RB pills | — |\n| Pad6 / 7 | L2 / R2 trigger shapes | LT / RT trigger shapes | — |\n| Pad8 / 9 | Create / Options ovals | View / Menu ovals | — |\n| Pad12–15 | d-pad with the arm lit | the same | — |\n| left stick | an L stick with arrows | the same | — |\n| keys | — | — | a keycap with the key's label (W, ↑, Space, Esc, Enter…), wide caps for long names |\n| Mouse0 / 2 / wheel | — | — | a mouse with the left or right button lit, or the wheel |\n\nEvery icon is 12×12 at scale 2, with a 1-px dark outline so it reads on any background. The text label beside it is the\naction's name (\"DRIFT\").\n\n## Appendix D. The debug handle and events\n\n`game.debug`:\n- `snapshot()` returns `{ scene, mode, stage, seg, z, x, v, gear, rpm, drift: { on, a, score }, slip, air, crash,\n  clock, score, bloom: { level, points }, petals, grade, request: { type, state, progress }, route, goal, rivals:\n  [{ id, z, x, place }], traffic: n, attract: phase, hub: { active, phase, sent, progress }, ui: { screen, focus },\n  lastDevice, glyphs, errors: n }`.\n- `events(n)` returns the last n bus events.\n- `teleport(stage, pct, x)`.\n- `tune(key, v)` and `resetTuning()`.\n- `cheat(name, on)`.\n- `go(scene, data)`.\n- `bot(on, params)`.\n- `ui.press(action)` and `ui.click(x, y)`.\n\n`game.stateHash = () => [stage, z, x, v, clock, score, petals]`, rounded.\n\n**Bus events:**\n\n| Area | Events |\n|---|---|\n| run | `run:start`, `run:end {reason}`, `stage:enter {id}`, `checkpoint {id, bonus}`, `fork:approach {left, right}`, `fork:pref {side}`, `fork:choose {side, next}`, `goal {id, mood}`, `time:low`, `time:up` |\n| car | `car:bump {with}`, `car:crash {with}`, `car:scrape`, `car:offroad {on}`, `air:start`, `air:land {hard}`, `start:perfect`, `drift:start {dir}`, `drift:end {score, length}`, `slip:start`, `slip:end`, `slingshot`, `nearmiss {id}`, `overtake {id, kind}`, `gust`, `horn` |\n| Love in Bloom | `req:start {type}`, `req:progress {n, of}`, `req:end {type, rating, petals}`, `petals {delta, reason}`, `bloom:level {n}` |\n| objects | `obj:pop`, `obj:bonk`, `obj:sting`, `obj:snap`, `obj:cone`, `obj:arch`, `obj:bouquet`, `obj:photo {rating}` |\n| rivals and missions | `rival:pass {id, by}`, `rival:out {id}`, `mission:end {id, cleared, rank}` |\n| other | `radio:tune {i}`, `ui:screen {name}`, `attract:start`, `attract:end`, `hub:*` |\n\n## Appendix E. Research notes (what the plan relied on)\n\n- **The engine.** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff: `README.md`, `API.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md` (the road-racing recipe, standard\n  features, testing, sessions), `docs/ASSETS.md` (shapes, `E.shapeSprite`), `modules/road.js` (the current single-road\n  renderer), `kits/road.js` (a complete coast-road slice: clock, checkpoints, radio, bot), `API-INDEX.md` (input,\n  glyphs, settings, audio, `E.Arcade`, `E.Road`). Its audio is a chip synth with one-shot voices and step-sequenced\n  songs on a game-time music clock. Its gamepad buttons are digital. Its standard menus are HTML.\n- **The AI Hub.** `lib/hub/README.md` (Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0, modes, card, start, reporting, rules, the\n  harness checks), `examples/ghost.html` (the ghost pattern: send every 100 ms unreliable, progress once a second,\n  result on finish, a result on time-up) and `harness.html` (its checks and its synthetic key pokes).\n- **The source**, from Wikipedia and a moegamer retrospective. *OutRun 2006: Coast 2 Coast* (SEGA, developed by Sumo\n  Digital, 2006; PS2, PSP, Xbox, PC) has these modes and mechanics:\n  - OutRun, Heart Attack (on-the-fly passenger requests rated with heart points and a final grade), Time Attack, and\n    the Coast 2 Coast career, whose missions come from characters.\n  - Mission types including Test Your Drift, Test Your Slipstream, Avoid the Knockout and Don't Lose Your Girlfriend.\n  - Unlocks bought with \"OutRun Miles\", and up to 6 players online.\n  - A brake-tap drift and slipstreaming.\n  - The mood: blue skies and escapist fun.\n\n  Nothing here copies its content (§3).\n- **Not found, so don't claim it.** Exact request lists, stage counts per course set, the music track list. Write\n  \"unknown\" in Source Information for any such detail.\n","numLines":480,"startLine":1279,"totalLines":1758}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Ew9z2jkPA7o4NwLb3Pw6Uo","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
