{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghtnNWWJsB5XNY88RV9h","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MHNoEbNNRP7hD5mmUVcPCj","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\np='src/01-about.js'; s=open(p).read()\na=s.index('  testingFeedback: ['); b=s.index('  aiHub: {')\nnew='''  testingFeedback: [\n    'About twenty real defects surfaced through the checks and the runs: a stage transition that dropped the carried distance, a goal that re-entered its own stage, a fork that read stage definitions as built stages, a bus used before it was set, a drift exit tied to the wrong value, a curve push applied as a velocity increment, drag that capped top speed near 170 km/h, a time-based traffic spawner, lane changes that oscillated, a garage row count that sent START back to the menu, a hub ready() sent before the hub had welcomed the copy, and a mission photo judged too early.',\n    'Four would have broken a run outright: the stage skip, the goal re-entry, the fork crash and the bus crash on the first race. The browser runs also found the garage row and the hub ready() order, which the protocol checks would have failed.',\n    'Most helpful: the full-route bot runs, which play all 16 forks to their goals with real distances, and the official AI Hub harness in Chromium, which checked the real protocol with latency and loss.',\n    'Least helpful: the draw smoke test. It only shows that drawing runs without an exception on a no-op renderer, so the screenshots in Chromium were the real check of what appears on screen.',\n    'Not verified: the balance on a pad (the bot is the only driver), the feel of the drift, the audio on real speakers, and the look of every stage and ending.',\n  ],\n  engineFeedback: [\n    'Most liked: the road module. E.Road with segments, curves, hills, place() and project() gave a working pseudo-3D road quickly, and the kit gave a complete reference game to read. The split-screen drawing (sc.draw once per viewport, then hud once) made a two-driver race a small job.',\n    'Got stuck on: the game.go options (the fade is the third argument), the pause-menu item and setting shapes, the headless draw path (road.draw needs a canvas stub), and the AI Hub client, whose ready() is dropped unless it is called after the hub has welcomed the copy.',\n    'Most important thing built myself: the car model (acceleration calibrated to each car\\\\'s 0-100 rating, steering with the curve push, drift, slipstream, off-road, manual gears), the traffic and lane logic, the driver brain, the run and request rules, the missions and their pacers.',\n    'Hardest part: a fork needs two roads drawn at once, and road.draw paints one road over the frame. Without a split-road renderer the fork is only signposted. Versus cars do not collide, because each driver has their own race.',\n    'Changed in the engine: nothing. Analog triggers are read from navigator.getGamepads() in game code, and the engine note and tyre squeal are short voices on the chip synth, not continuous loops.',\n    'One change I would make: a headless renderer or canvas stub in the engine, so draw code can run in tests without a browser, and a continuous voice (loop) for engines and tyres.',\n  ],\n  promptFeedback: [\n    'Most liked: the plan is concrete. Every tuning number, every stage layout and every test suite is written down, and the AI Hub protocol is a precise README with a harness to run.',\n    'Confusing: the plan was written for a different run (another model, date and folder names). It asks for far more than one session can build without a cut list, and its stage lengths and its speed model do not agree (a 194,000-unit stage at top speed takes about 59 s, with no losses), so the bot could not meet the 52 to 64 s target until the stages were shortened by 20%.',\n    'Hardest: getting the bot to finish the routes and the balance windows without a pad, and making a fork read as a fork without a split-road renderer.',\n    'Would change: give each run its own names and dates, state what to cut when time runs short, state the stage length and the clock as one rule, and say whether the bot uses the player\\\\'s physics.',\n  ],\n'''\ns=s[:a]+new+s[b:]\nopen(p,'w').write(s); print('ok')\nEOF\nnode --check src/01-about.js && timeout 120 node build.mjs | tail -1 && python3 -c \"import json;d=json.load(open('../exit-interview.json'));print(list(d), len(d['testingFeedback']), len(d['engineFeedback']), len(d['promptFeedback']), d['aiHub']['players'])\"","description":"Write the final feedback answers and rebuild the exit interview"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1638,"cache_read_input_tokens":652750,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1638},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f95c51ac-ca98-4f0d-bbff-431b6595447e","uuid":"97272c4a-786a-4460-9438-a2956c7c87f4","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:33:01.878Z","request_id":"req_011CghtnN9uxeaVFbobU6jzV","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01MHNoEbNNRP7hD5mmUVcPCj":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-043153__xe1b7e1/work/OutBloom2006Coast2Coast-OutRun2006Coast2Coast-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-043153 && python3 - <<'EOF'\np='src/01-about.js'; s=open(p).read()\na=s.index('  testingFeedback: ['); b=s.index('  aiHub: {')\nnew='''  testingFeedback: [\n    'About twenty real defects surfaced through the checks and the runs: a stage transition that dropped the carried distance, a goal that re-entered its own stage, a fork that read stage definitions as built stages, a bus used before it was set, a drift exit tied to the wrong value, a curve push applied as a velocity increment, drag that capped top speed near 170 km/h, a time-based traffic spawner, lane changes that oscillated, a garage row count that sent START back to the menu, a hub ready() sent before the hub had welcomed the copy, and a mission photo judged too early.',\n    'Four would have broken a run outright: the stage skip, the goal re-entry, the fork crash and the bus crash on the first race. The browser runs also found the garage row and the hub ready() order, which the protocol checks would have failed.',\n    'Most helpful: the full-route bot runs, which play all 16 forks to their goals with real distances, and the official AI Hub harness in Chromium, which checked the real protocol with latency and loss.',\n    'Least helpful: the draw smoke test. It only shows that drawing runs without an exception on a no-op renderer, so the screenshots in Chromium were the real check of what appears on screen.',\n    'Not verified: the balance on a pad (the bot is the only driver), the feel of the drift, the audio on real speakers, and the look of every stage and ending.',\n  ],\n  engineFeedback: [\n    'Most liked: the road module. E.Road with segments, curves, hills, place() and project() gave a working pseudo-3D road quickly, and the kit gave a complete reference game to read. The split-screen drawing (sc.draw once per viewport, then hud once) made a two-driver race a small job.',\n    'Got stuck on: the game.go options (the fade is the third argument), the pause-menu item and setting shapes, the headless draw path (road.draw needs a canvas stub), and the AI Hub client, whose ready() is dropped unless it is called after the hub has welcomed the copy.',\n    'Most important thing built myself: the car model (acceleration calibrated to each car\\\\'s 0-100 rating, steering with the curve push, drift, slipstream, off-road, manual gears), the traffic and lane logic, the driver brain, the run and request rules, the missions and their pacers.',\n    'Hardest part: a fork needs two roads drawn at once, and road.draw paints one road over the frame. Without a split-road renderer the fork is only signposted. Versus cars do not collide, because each driver has their own race.',\n    'Changed in the engine: nothing. Analog triggers are read from navigator.getGamepads() in game code, and the engine note and tyre squeal are short voices on the chip synth, not continuous loops.',\n    'One change I would make: a headless renderer or canvas stub in the engine, so draw code can run in tests without a browser, and a continuous voice (loop) for engines and tyres.',\n  ],\n  promptFeedback: [\n    'Most liked: the plan is concrete. Every tuning number, every stage layout and every test suite is written down, and the AI Hub protocol is a precise README with a harness to run.',\n    'Confusing: the plan was written for a different run (another model, date and folder names). It asks for far more than one session can build without a cut list, and its stage lengths and its speed model do not agree (a 194,000-unit stage at top speed takes about 59 s, with no losses), so the bot could not meet the 52 to 64 s target until the stages were shortened by 20%.',\n    'Hardest: getting the bot to finish the routes and the balance windows without a pad, and making a fork read as a fork without a split-road renderer.',\n    'Would change: give each run its own names and dates, state what to cut when time runs short, state the stage length and the clock as one rule, and say whether the bot uses the player\\\\'s physics.',\n  ],\n'''\ns=s[:a]+new+s[b:]\nopen(p,'w').write(s); print('ok')\nEOF\nnode --check src/01-about.js && timeout 120 node build.mjs | tail -1 && python3 -c \"import json;d=json.load(open('../exit-interview.json'));print(list(d), len(d['testingFeedback']), len(d['engineFeedback']), len(d['promptFeedback']), d['aiHub']['players'])\"","description":"Write the final feedback answers and rebuild the exit interview"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01MHNoEbNNRP7hD5mmUVcPCj":{"cwd":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-043153__xe1b7e1/work/OutBloom2006Coast2Coast-OutRun2006Coast2Coast-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-043153"}}}
