{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghvBKoYPwvTmNYYq9jfT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Status\n\nThe bundle, the exit interview, and the four sections are all here. Most of the plan is now built. Some planned items are still not, so I can't honestly say every feature is complete.\n\n**Files**\n- Bundle: `work/OutBloom2006Coast2Coast-OutRun2006Coast2Coast-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-043153.html`\n- `work/exit-interview.json`: parses, every field filled\n- Dev folder: `work/OutBloom2006Coast2Coast-…-20261104-043153/`. `node tests/run.mjs` runs 155 checks, all passing. `NOTES.md` and `engine/CHANGES.md` list every deviation.\n\n**Done in this round**\n- Rivals and versus cars now collide with the player.\n- The attract mode is a timed playlist of about 100 seconds: arcade, the forks, Love in Bloom, a Drift Duel or a two-bot versus, a goal ending, and the high-score table.\n- The Tour is a stylised island map: the pyramid's roads, stage pins, and postcards pinned to their stages.\n- Passengers are drawn with the engine's Humanoid rig portraits.\n- Fork renderer (plan E1): when a junction is in view, the road splits into two branches with the gore between them, and the car rides its branch. I checked it in Chromium. The renderer is game code, so the engine's `road.js` is unchanged.\n- Engine changes, logged in `engine/CHANGES.md`: analog input (E3), the hub's Create-plus-Options guard (E4), the PlayStation glyph default (E5), continuous voices for the engine note and tyres (E7), and 10-foot shell sizes (E8).\n- Browser checks: no console errors and no external requests from the file page. The touch-phone path shows the notice and starts attract.\n- AI Hub harness, final bundle, in Chromium: 2 players PASS, 4 players with latency and loss PASS, host leaving PASS.\n\n**Still not built, from the plan**\n- Road dressing (E2): edge strips, spans, and tints. The game uses scenery props instead.\n- Synth extension (E6): instrument presets and chord tokens. The songs use plain notes.\n- Seated rigs in the car. The passengers in the car are still drawn heads.\n- A shared-world hub mode. The design is ghost mode only, which the hub protocol supports.\n- A built-in split-road renderer in the engine. The fork renderer is game code.\n\nDeviations I chose on purpose: stage lengths are 80% of the plan's, and the clock is 100 s with 71 s per checkpoint. Both are needed for the bot to finish the routes, and both are recorded in the notes.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 04:31:53\n- **Source:** OutRun 2006: Coast 2 Coast (SEGA / Sumo Digital, 2006), used as inspiration only\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** arcade road racer with a passenger-request layer, a mission career, and a checkpoint route; third-person chase camera on a pseudo-3D road\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (MIT, with the changes in `engine/CHANGES.md`), JavaScript, Canvas 2D, Web Audio, AI Hub client 1.4.0\n- **Art Direction:** engine pixel art at 480×270, drawn in code, dusk and sunrise palettes\n- **Animation:** engine sprite and px drawing; engine Humanoid portraits; wheels, turbines, balloons, lights\n- **Audio:** music and effects in code on the engine synth; continuous voices for the engine note and tyres; syllable babble for the passengers; no audio files\n- **Online:** AI Hub ghost mode (Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0)\n- **Testing:** 155 headless checks; a fake-hub round; the AI Hub harness in Chromium; Chromium runs of the file page, touch emulation, and fork screenshots\n- **Research:** engine docs and the road kit read in full; the AI Hub protocol README; the plan; public summaries of the source\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); Chromium through Playwright\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **Issues caught:** about twenty real defects. The most serious were a stage transition that dropped the carried distance, a goal that re-entered its own stage, forks that read stage definitions as built stages, drag that capped top speed near 170 km/h, a time-based traffic spawner, lane oscillation, a garage row count that sent START back to the menu, a hub `ready()` sent before the hub had welcomed the copy, and a photo box judged too early.\n- **Critical / game-breaking:** four would have broken a run outright: the stage skip, the goal re-entry, the fork crash, and the bus crash on the first race. The browser runs also found the garage row and the hub `ready()` order, which the protocol checks would have failed.\n- **Most helpful test:** the full-route bot runs, which play all 16 forks to their goals with real distances, and the official AI Hub harness in Chromium.\n- **Least helpful test:** the draw smoke test. It only shows drawing runs without an exception on a no-op renderer. The Chromium screenshots were the real check of what appears on screen, and the fork screenshots are one frame each, not a filmstrip.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** the road module. `E.Road` with curves, hills, `place()` and `project()` gave a working pseudo-3D road quickly. Split-screen drawing (the scene drawn once per viewport, then `hud` once) made a two-driver race a small job.\n- **Got stuck on:** the `game.go` options (the fade is the third argument), the pause-menu item and setting shapes, the headless draw path (road drawing needs a canvas stub), and the AI Hub client, whose `ready()` is dropped unless it is called after the hub has welcomed the copy.\n- **Most important thing I built myself:** the car model (acceleration calibrated to each car's 0–100 rating, steering with the curve push, drift, slipstream, off-road, manual gears), the traffic and lane logic, the driver brain, the run and request rules, the missions and pacers, and the fork renderer.\n- **Hardest part:** the fork. The engine's road draws one road per call, so the branches had to be drawn by game code on the road's internals. The car's x also had to change frame at the decision, which was easy to get wrong.\n- **Engine changes:** analog input (E3), the hub corner guard (E4), the PlayStation glyph default (E5), continuous voices (E7), and the 10-foot shell sizes (E8). Road dressing (E2) and the synth fields (E6) were not made.\n- **Would change:** a headless renderer or canvas stub, a built-in split-road renderer so forks need no game code, and a shared-world option in the hub's examples.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** the plan is concrete. Tuning numbers, stage layouts, and test suites are written down, and the AI Hub protocol is a precise README with a harness to run.\n- **Confusing:** the plan was written for a different run (another model, date, and folder names). It asks for far more than one session can build without a cut list. Its stage lengths and speed model don't agree: a 194,000-unit stage at top speed takes about 59 s with no losses, so the bot could not meet the 52–64 s target until the stages were shortened.\n- **Hardest:** getting the bot through the routes without a pad to play by hand, and drawing a fork without a split-road renderer in the engine.\n- **Would change:** give each run its own names and dates, state what to cut when time runs short, state the stage length and clock as one rule, and say whether the bot uses the player's physics.\n\n**Next step:** the open items are road dressing (E2), the synth fields (E6), and seated rigs in the car. I can build those next, or accept them as cut and close the goal."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1098,"cache_read_input_tokens":760502,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1098},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f95c51ac-ca98-4f0d-bbff-431b6595447e","uuid":"39a6e27c-8d0f-4a65-9121-329d80d7684b","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:51:12.901Z","request_id":"req_011CghvBKRDwGRXMQNCUUVox"}
