{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cghvs15mTWVkmK5CCcnKp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Status\n\nEverything in TASK.md is implemented, with no stubs. Three items from the plan are still not built, and I'm saying so rather than claiming full completion:\n\n- **Shared-world hub mode.** The plan (§7.12) chose ghost mode, and TASK.md only asks for multiplayer integration. Ghost mode is built and passes the official harness. I did not build shared-world.\n- **Effects bus (E6 `send`, convolution reverb).** The rest of E6 is built.\n- **Pre-rendered sprites and a shape pipeline.** Everything is drawn in code, as the plan's §9 describes, but not through the plan's shape system.\n\nAlso: in the car, the rigs' legs draw over the body, because the body and occupants are drawn in one pass. The plan wanted them hidden under the body.\n\n**Done this round**\n- **Synth extension (E6):** named instrument presets, chords, accents, slides, FM, unison detune, filters, pan, and more drum sounds. Sunburst's keys use chords. All radio songs play in Chromium with no warnings.\n- **Road dressing (E2):** each stage has ground on its sides (sea, snow, water), edge walls and rails (cliffs, facades, stone walls, bridge rails), and overhead spans (arches, canopies, galleries, bunting). Checked in screenshots of S1, S3, and S8.\n- **Seated rigs in the car:** the driver and passenger are the engine's Humanoid rigs, posed seated, and checked in a screenshot.\n- **Engine changes:** E2, E3, E4, E5, E6, E7, E8 are in the engine file and logged in `engine/CHANGES.md`. E1 (fork) is game code.\n\n**Verified**\n- `tests/run.mjs`: 155 headless checks, all passing.\n- Chromium over `file://`: no console errors, no external requests. Touch-phone emulation shows the notice and starts attract after 11 s.\n- AI Hub harness on the final bundle, all passing: 2 players, 4 players with 150 ms latency and 5% loss, and host-leave.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 04:31:53\n- **Source:** OutRun 2006: Coast 2 Coast (SEGA / Sumo Digital, 2006), used as inspiration only\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** arcade road racer with a passenger-request layer, a mission career, and a checkpoint route; third-person chase camera on a pseudo-3D road\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (MIT, with changes logged in `engine/CHANGES.md`), JavaScript, Canvas 2D, Web Audio, AI Hub client 1.4.0\n- **Art Direction:** engine pixel art at 480×270, drawn in code, dusk and sunrise palettes; road dressing for sea, cliffs, walls and spans\n- **Animation:** engine sprite and px drawing; engine Humanoid rigs for passengers, seated in the car; wheels, turbines, balloons, lights\n- **Audio:** music and effects in code on the engine synth (named presets, chords, accents, slides); continuous voices for the engine note and tyres; syllable babble for the passengers; no audio files\n- **Online:** AI Hub ghost mode (Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0)\n- **Testing:** 155 headless checks; a fake-hub round; the AI Hub harness in Chromium; Chromium runs of the root file, scenes, stages, forks, the radio songs, and touch emulation\n- **Research:** engine docs and the road kit read in full; the AI Hub protocol README; the plan; public summaries of the source\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); Chromium through Playwright\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **Issues caught:** about twenty real defects. The most serious were a stage transition that dropped the carried distance, a goal that re-entered its own stage, forks that read stage definitions as built stages, drag that capped top speed near 170 km/h, a time-based traffic spawner, lane oscillation, a garage row count that sent START back to the menu, a hub `ready()` sent before the hub had welcomed the copy, and a photo box judged too early.\n- **Critical / game-breaking:** four would have broken a run outright: the stage skip, the goal re-entry, the fork crash, and the bus crash on the first race. The browser runs also found the garage row and the hub `ready()` order, which the protocol checks would have failed.\n- **Most helpful test:** the full-route bot runs, which play all 16 forks to their goals with real distances, and the official AI Hub harness in Chromium.\n- **Least helpful test:** the draw smoke test. It only shows drawing runs without an exception on a no-op renderer. The Chromium screenshots were the real check of what appears on screen, and they are single frames, not a filmstrip, so animation is not reviewed.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** the road module. `E.Road` with curves, hills, `place()` and `project()` gave a working pseudo-3D road quickly, and the road kit was a full reference game. The split-screen drawing (the scene drawn once per viewport, then `hud` once) made a two-driver race a small job. The music engine took named presets and chord tokens with small changes.\n- **Got stuck on:** the `game.go` options (the fade is the third argument), the pause-menu item and setting shapes, the headless draw path (road drawing needs a canvas stub), the Humanoid's `draw` needing the real view object, and the AI Hub client, whose `ready()` is dropped unless it is called after the hub has welcomed the copy.\n- **Most important thing I built myself:** the car model (acceleration calibrated to each car's 0–100 rating, steering with the curve push, drift, slipstream, off-road, manual gears), the traffic and lane logic, the driver brain, the run and request rules, the missions and pacers, the fork renderer, and the road dressing in the engine.\n- **Hardest part:** the fork. The engine's road draws one road per call, so the branches had to be drawn in game code on the road's internals, and the car's x had to change frame at the decision.\n- **Engine changes:** analog input (E3), the hub corner guard (E4), the PlayStation glyph default (E5), continuous voices (E7), the synth extension (E6, partly: no effects bus), road dressing (E2), and the 10-foot shell sizes (E8). E1 is game code.\n- **Would change:** a headless renderer or canvas stub, a built-in split-road renderer so forks need no game code, a view-free Humanoid draw for seated figures, and a shared-world option in the hub's examples.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** the plan is concrete. Tuning numbers, stage layouts, and test suites are written down, and the AI Hub protocol is a precise README with a harness to run.\n- **Confusing:** the plan was written for a different run (another model, date, and folder names). It asks for far more than one session can build without a cut list. Its stage lengths and speed model don't agree: a 194,000-unit stage at top speed takes about 59 s with no losses, so the bot could not meet the 52–64 s target until the stages were shortened. The hook's wording (\"every feature in TASK.md\") and the plan's full list also don't say where one ends and the other begins.\n- **Hardest:** getting the bot through the routes without a pad to play by hand, and drawing forks and seated rigs without the engine supporting them.\n- **Would change:** give each run its own names and dates, state the cut list up front, state the stage length and clock as one rule, say whether the bot uses the player's physics, and separate TASK.md's requirements from the plan's design choices (like shared-world mode).\n\n**Next step:** if you want shared-world mode and the effects bus built as well, tell me and I'll do those next. Otherwise, the build is complete against TASK.md and the plan minus those two."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":819,"cache_read_input_tokens":816291,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":819},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f95c51ac-ca98-4f0d-bbff-431b6595447e","uuid":"1ef792a1-8ad9-4717-acd3-1345aa282851","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:00:11.359Z","request_id":"req_011CghvrzfxuTG6D8vghWSc3"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The condition requires every feature in TASK.md to be implemented with no placeholders or stubs. The final assistant message states that shared-world hub mode, the effects bus (E6 send and convolution reverb), and pre-rendered sprites/shape pipeline are not built, and that in-car rig legs draw over the body contrary to the plan. It also asks the user whether to build the two remaining items, so the work is not finished. The bundle, exit-interview.json and the four sections do exist, but feature completeness is not demonstrated."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f95c51ac-ca98-4f0d-bbff-431b6595447e","uuid":"f8c63169-0b1c-41ee-bbab-495877ac5dcb","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:00:29.145Z","isSynthetic":true}
