{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghwJ7vEF7DM9VQ4nGin6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Status\n\nThe bundle and exit interview are in place, and the four sections are below. Everything in TASK.md is built. One plan item is still open: shared-world hub mode. I have not built it, and I need you to decide that.\n\nThe reason: a game declares one hub mode in its tags, and the plan chose ghost mode (§7.12). Shared-world would replace the ghost round, not add to it. I would be changing the game's declared mode without your say-so, so I'm asking first.\n\n**Done this round**\n- **Effects bus (E6):** tracks can send to a tempo-synced delay and a convolution reverb built from a generated impulse. The reverb rises in tunnels (0.15 out, 0.6 in). Speech runs on its own voice bus, with a Voices setting (Off, Half, Full). Songs use sends.\n- **Pre-rendered sprites (§9.3):** traffic vehicles and the player's car bodies render once into a cached canvas, keyed by paint, size, lean, and brake. The seated rigs stay live.\n- **Seated rigs:** the driver and passenger sit in the car, and their legs now draw over the body. The plan asked for them hidden under it. Fixing that needs the body and the occupants drawn in separate passes, and the car code draws them in one.\n\n**Verified**\n- `tests/run.mjs`: 155 headless checks, all passing.\n- Chromium over `file://`: no console errors, no external requests. The radio songs play with no engine warnings. Touch emulation shows the notice and starts attract after 11 s.\n- AI Hub harness on the final bundle, all passing: 2 players, 4 players with 150 ms latency and 5% loss, and host-leave.\n\n**Decision for you:** keep ghost mode, which is built and passes the harness, or switch the game to shared-world. Switching means a different protocol mode and a new round design, and the harness would need a rerun.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 04:31:53\n- **Source:** OutRun 2006: Coast 2 Coast (SEGA / Sumo Digital, 2006), used as inspiration only\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** arcade road racer with a passenger-request layer, a mission career, and a checkpoint route; third-person chase camera on a pseudo-3D road\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (MIT, with changes logged in `engine/CHANGES.md`), JavaScript, Canvas 2D, Web Audio, AI Hub client 1.4.0\n- **Art Direction:** engine pixel art at 480×270, drawn in code, dusk and sunrise palettes; road dressing for sea, cliffs, walls and spans; pre-rendered vehicle sprites\n- **Animation:** engine sprite and px drawing; engine Humanoid rigs for passengers, seated in the car; wheels, turbines, balloons, lights\n- **Audio:** music and effects in code on the engine synth (named presets, chords, accents, slides, sends to a delay and reverb); continuous voices for the engine note and tyres; a voice bus for speech; no audio files\n- **Online:** AI Hub ghost mode (Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0)\n- **Testing:** 155 headless checks; a fake-hub round; the AI Hub harness in Chromium; Chromium runs of the root file, scenes, stages, forks, the radio songs, and touch emulation\n- **Research:** engine docs and the road kit read in full; the AI Hub protocol README; the plan; public summaries of the source\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); Chromium through Playwright\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **Issues caught:** about twenty real defects. The most serious were a stage transition that dropped the carried distance, a goal that re-entered its own stage, forks that read stage definitions as built stages, drag that capped top speed near 170 km/h, a time-based traffic spawner, lane oscillation, a garage row count that sent START back to the menu, a hub `ready()` sent before the hub had welcomed the copy, and a photo box judged too early.\n- **Critical / game-breaking:** four would have broken a run outright: the stage skip, the goal re-entry, the fork crash, and the bus crash on the first race. The browser runs also found the garage row and the hub `ready()` order, which the protocol checks would have failed.\n- **Most helpful test:** the full-route bot runs, which play all 16 forks to their goals with real distances, and the official AI Hub harness in Chromium.\n- **Least helpful test:** the draw smoke test. It only shows drawing runs without an exception on a no-op renderer. The Chromium screenshots were the real check, but they are single frames, so animation is not reviewed.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** the road module. `E.Road` with curves, hills, `place()` and `project()` gave a working pseudo-3D road quickly, and the road kit was a full reference game. Split-screen drawing (the scene drawn once per viewport, then `hud` once) made a two-driver race a small job. The music engine took named presets, chords, and sends with small changes.\n- **Got stuck on:** the `game.go` options (the fade is the third argument), the pause-menu item and setting shapes, the headless draw path (road drawing needs a canvas stub), the Humanoid's `draw` needing the real view object, and the AI Hub client, whose `ready()` is dropped unless it is called after the hub has welcomed the copy.\n- **Most important thing I built myself:** the car model (acceleration calibrated to each car's 0–100 rating, steering with the curve push, drift, slipstream, off-road, manual gears), the traffic and lane logic, the driver brain, the run and request rules, the missions and pacers, the fork renderer, and the road dressing in the engine.\n- **Hardest part:** the fork. The engine's road draws one road per call, so the branches had to be drawn in game code on the road's internals, and the car's x had to change frame at the decision.\n- **Engine changes:** analog input (E3), the hub corner guard (E4), the PlayStation glyph default (E5), the synth extension (E6: presets, chords, accents, slides, FM, unison, filters, pan, drum sounds, sends, reverb, voice bus), continuous voices (E7), road dressing (E2), and the 10-foot shell sizes (E8). E1 is game code.\n- **Would change:** a headless renderer or canvas stub, a built-in split-road renderer so forks need no game code, a view-free Humanoid draw for seated figures, and a per-occupant draw order so figures can sit under the body.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** the plan is concrete. Tuning numbers, stage layouts, and test suites are written down, and the AI Hub protocol is a precise README with a harness to run.\n- **Confusing:** the plan was written for a different run (another model, date, and folder names). It asks for far more than one session can build without a cut list. Its stage lengths and speed model don't agree: a 194,000-unit stage at top speed takes about 59 s with no losses, so the bot could not meet the 52–64 s target until the stages were shortened.\n- **Hardest:** getting the bot through the routes without a pad to play by hand, and drawing forks and seated rigs without the engine supporting them.\n- **Would change:** give each run its own names and dates, state the cut list up front, state the stage length and clock as one rule, say whether the bot uses the player's physics, and say which hub mode the game declares (ghost or shared-world), since the protocol allows only one per game."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":739,"cache_read_input_tokens":844907,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":739},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f95c51ac-ca98-4f0d-bbff-431b6595447e","uuid":"bec165f4-9eb2-451f-8dea-1e31218482d5","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:05:50.694Z","request_id":"req_011CghwJ7STE9KF28GtFg7Me"}
